WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.083
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome
to the Huberman Lab Guest

00:00:02.083 --> 00:00:04.140
Series, where I
and an expert guest

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.840
discuss science and science
based tools for everyday life.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Andrew Huberman
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.330
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.740
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.530
Today's episode marks the
fourth in the six episode

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.110
series on fitness,
exercise, and performance.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.380
And today's episode is all about
optimal fitness programming,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.680
that is how to design a fitness
and exercise program that

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.790
can achieve the goals that you
want for fitness and for sports

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:30.630
performance.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.910
Dr. Andy Galpin,
great to be back.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.220
In previous episodes,
you taught us

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.960
about the various
adaptations that

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.660
occur at the level of cells,
at the level of organs,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.940
indeed at the level of
the entire body that

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.160
underlie things like
improvements and strength

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.410
and speed,
hypertrophy, AKA muscle

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.960
growth, and the various
forms of endurance.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.330
And you laid out beautifully
the various protocols

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.360
that one can do in
order to achieve

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.340
each and every one
of those adaptations.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:58.960
Today I would love
for you to teach us

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.990
how we can combine different
protocols to achieve

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:03.850
multiple adaptations
in parallel--

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.420
for instance, how to improve
endurance and strength, how

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.150
to achieve some
level of hypertrophy,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.790
perhaps directed hypertrophy
at specific muscle groups,

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:17.050
while also maintaining endurance
and perhaps improving speed,

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.790
for instance.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.030
And if you would, I'd
love for you to tell us

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.800
how we can combine
different protocols

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.980
and vary those across the
week, across the month,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.370
across the year so that we
can make regular progress

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.800
and perhaps even could give
us a window into the ways

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.148
to make the fastest
progress possible.

00:01:34.148 --> 00:01:35.940
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, I
would love to do that.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.810
We've invested a lot of time
in the previous episodes

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:44.550
covering background and concepts
and detail about the physiology

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.410
so you understood
why you're making

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.720
the choices you're making
and why other choices are

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.190
less effective.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.870
In this discussion,
I would actually

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.600
like to jump maybe more
directly to the answer

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.360
and kind of get right into
the protocol and maybe

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.962
a little bit less background.

00:01:58.962 --> 00:02:00.420
If you're interested
in that stuff,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.550
I suppose you have to go
backwards a little bit

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.830
and watch some of those
previous episodes.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.160
But I would love to jump in to
just some samples, some case

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.900
studies, if you will,
and kind of walk

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.740
through different protocols.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.810
I know that over the course
of my 11 years as a college

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.300
professor and being
in the public space

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.740
a little bit, probably the
most numerous style of question

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.580
I have gotten is exactly that.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.940
So I know the rep
range for this,

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.310
or I know the style of
training for that adaptation.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.990
But how do I put them together?

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.170
And I would just like
to spend our time

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.800
today going through
those things.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.690
And the reason I want
to do it is this.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.077
Some people listening at home
surely just love exercise.

00:02:43.077 --> 00:02:44.160
They're already bought in.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.910
And they're going to
train no matter what.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.760
And they're interested in just
actually being more effective.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.030
And so the way that you
structure and put your plan

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.120
together will in
large part determine

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.013
getting more progress
for less effort

00:02:57.013 --> 00:02:59.430
or actually being able to put
the same amount of effort in

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.220
and getting results faster.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.990
There's also some folks
probably listening who are like,

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.030
OK, I exercise.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.750
I do what I can.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.690
I'm bought into the
benefits that you've

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.780
talked so elaborately
over the 100 plus episodes

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.170
you've done about the
various benefits of exercise.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.797
But you don't like--

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.130
you're kind of doing it because
you know it's important.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.190
But you're not there.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.382
So for those folks,
it's sort of like, OK,

00:03:23.382 --> 00:03:24.840
how can we actually
make this thing

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.470
more effective so we can make
sure you hit the things you're

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.173
absolutely have to get for the
short and long term benefits

00:03:32.173 --> 00:03:34.590
to make sure that you're looking
the way you want to look,

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.965
you're performing physically
the way you want to perform,

00:03:36.965 --> 00:03:39.130
and that you can do that
across your lifespan?

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.820
So how can we give
you all some structure

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.530
to where, again, you
don't have to turn

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.560
into an absolute lover
of physical fitness

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.310
and it doesn't have
to take over your life

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.540
but you can still get more
results for your same time

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.000
restrictions, whether
that be two days a week

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.970
or five days a week
or only certain access

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:00.690
to equipment or experience,
whatever the case may be?

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.880
How can we help those
folks as well put together

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.280
a protocol that will get
them closer to their goals

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.825
with less restrictions?

00:04:06.825 --> 00:04:07.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fantastic.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.970
And I'm hoping
that along the way,

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:14.040
you'll also point us to how
often to take the fitness

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.750
assessment for each
of the adaptations

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.769
that you referred to
in a previous episode.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:20.970
We will also link to that
fitness assessment segment

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.890
in the show note
captions for this episode

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.767
because that fitness assessment
for different adaptations,

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:27.600
I think, is a really
powerful way for people

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.620
to touch in and see how much
long endurance do they have,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.440
how much anaerobic
capacity do they have,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.180
how much strength
do they really have.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.490
And then perhaps you'd
also be willing to throw

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.470
in a couple of
additional ways that we

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.380
can assess our level
of fitness and progress

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.615
in this arc of fitness
program across the year.

00:04:46.615 --> 00:04:47.490
ANDY GALPIN: Amazing.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.603
I can't wait to do that.

00:04:48.603 --> 00:04:50.520
I think it is also
important before we jump in

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.840
to acknowledge a lot of folks
may be thinking to themselves,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.618
I don't really
necessarily need a plan.

00:04:57.618 --> 00:04:58.660
Why do I have to do that?

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.493
I don't have a certain
goal I'm going after.

00:05:00.493 --> 00:05:02.035
I'm not running a
race any time soon.

00:05:02.035 --> 00:05:03.285
I'm not a competitive athlete.

00:05:03.285 --> 00:05:04.980
I just-- I go to the
gym, and I workout.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.030
And that's great.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.880
Well, I would like to try to
convince you that regardless

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.093
of where you're at,
having a plan will achieve

00:05:12.093 --> 00:05:13.510
those things we
just talked about,

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.200
which is more success
in a shorter time frame.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.420
There's actually a
significant amount of research

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:19.440
to support this.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.380
Those individuals who go
on a specific training plan

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.240
compared to those
who do not will

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.930
receive better
results independent

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.480
of the effectiveness
of the program.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.280
So we've talked in
previous episodes

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.520
about tons of different
styles and strategies.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.560
And to reiterate,
it really doesn't

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.300
matter which one you pick.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.970
The fact that you
have a plan is always

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.070
more effective than
not having a plan.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.260
And so, again,
even if you're not

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.040
planning on competing
with something,

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.040
if you want to shorten
the amount of time you're

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.860
in the gym, get more
results from it,

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.860
I would strongly encourage
to put something together.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.160
The two largest reasons why
people don't get results

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.660
with their fitness
training protocol

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.700
is number one,
adherence, and then

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.730
number two, some sort
of progressive overload.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.770
Both of those two
things are challenging

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.930
to accomplish without a plan.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.390
So the reason people don't
go to the gym, one of them,

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:14.940
and one of the
reasons why it takes

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.690
them so long is because
they don't walk in

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:18.027
with a very specific plan.

00:06:18.027 --> 00:06:19.860
It's sort of like going
to the grocery store

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.930
and figuring out what
you're going to buy

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:23.550
versus knowing
exactly what you're

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.140
going to get in your shopping
list, grabbing those things,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:25.590
and getting out.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.700
You'll notice your
time in the grocery

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:27.992
store is half the length.

00:06:27.992 --> 00:06:28.950
You're more productive.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.320
And you didn't waste
money on extra things.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.820
So that alone will
drive adherence

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.903
because you're now going
to think to yourself, oh,

00:06:34.903 --> 00:06:37.277
that 90-minute workout I do
is actually really just 60.

00:06:37.277 --> 00:06:39.610
And so now the next time you
go to training you're like,

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.070
man, I don't have 90 minutes.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.155
You realize it's only
60 or 40 or 30 or 20

00:06:43.155 --> 00:06:44.280
or whatever it needs to be.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:45.730
So that alone will
get you there.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.580
The second part of
that which is overload.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.700
It's very difficult to
understand and remember, well,

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.940
the last time I did lat raises,
I used I think 5 pounds.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.570
And I think I did like 12.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.350
Well, if you don't have some
sort of system of tracking--

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.060
and this can be as
simple as a notebook,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.950
just writing down
what you did before

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:04.320
and doing a little bit
more the next time.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.080
That is going to almost
guarantee you success.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.890
So having some structure-- and
this structure can be fairly

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.223
loose, so we're going to talk
about a bunch of different

00:07:13.223 --> 00:07:13.770
examples--

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.280
is something I strongly
encourage everyone

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:18.915
to utilize for their exercise.

