00:09:59.373 --> 00:10:00.790
ANDY GALPIN: A few
minutes ago, we

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:03.510
were talking about how two of
the major reasons people don't

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:05.260
get as much out of
their training programs

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:09.860
as they would like is because
of one, a lack of adherence,

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:12.350
and two, a lack of
progressive overload.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:14.410
So the solution to
that is constructing

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:18.440
a plan that lives within
your realistic limitations.

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:22.210
So I would like to walk you
through my 10-step approach

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.190
to how I design
training programs.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:27.790
Now, before I do that, I
think it is fair and important

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:30.160
for the audience to
understand that this is simply

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:31.540
my approach.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.410
I've been doing
this a long time.

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:34.780
I played college football.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.330
And I wrote my own training
programs back then.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:39.910
I have and am still working
with professional athletes

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:44.320
in the PGA TOUR and the NFL,
the NBA, Major League Baseball,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.880
as well as a ton of
general population folks.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.327
So this is a combination
of the evidence base

00:10:50.327 --> 00:10:51.910
that we've been
talking about in terms

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.890
of best practices for
strength and conditioning

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.090
as well as just my
years of experience.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.810
So there are many, many
ways one could do this.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:00.820
I'm not even suggesting
this is the best.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:02.620
This is simply how I do it.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.480
This is exactly how
I handle it when

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.910
a new individual comes to me
and how I teach my students.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:10.990
So step number one is assessing
properly and identifying

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:12.010
a training goal.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.380
Now, that's actually
sort of funny

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:15.550
because we hear that a lot.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:18.580
But a lot of people actually
never take that step,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.570
not to call anybody
in the room out.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
what's happening here

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.080
is this morning
Andy, Dr. Galpin,

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:27.640
and I were training together.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:32.590
And he was providing amazing
tips on form and set rep

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:33.920
cadence and the sort of thing.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.460
And he said, so what's your
training goal for the next 12

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:37.960
months?

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:39.160
And I paused.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.775
And it turned into a
very long pause because--

00:11:41.775 --> 00:11:43.150
ANDY GALPIN: Which
is an nice way

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:44.608
of saying he didn't
have an answer.

00:11:44.608 --> 00:11:46.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
didn't have an answer.

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:48.820
Of course, I don't
just want to maintain

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.180
what I've developed in terms
of strength and hypertrophy

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:53.050
and endurance.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:56.420
But I don't have a clear goal.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:58.900
So I'm hoping that by the
end of today's discussion,

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.995
I will be on track to
a clear set of goals.

00:12:00.995 --> 00:12:01.870
ANDY GALPIN: Amazing.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.330
I'm not going to
bore you all here.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.860
But, really, I
can't stress enough

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:09.880
how important that step really
truly is to getting results.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:13.570
The analogy we use here
is if you left your house

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:17.680
and you were attempting to
get to the grocery store

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.500
and you just started
driving and if you drove

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:22.300
every possible route,
you would eventually

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:23.500
get to a grocery store.

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.840
And so, yes, that can work.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.870
A better approach is
saying, here's where I am.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:30.370
There's where I want to go.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:32.753
What is the optimal route there?

00:12:32.753 --> 00:12:34.670
And that's really what
you're doing with goal.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:35.710
So it is a boring setup.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:36.960
It is not interesting to hear.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:40.700
I don't have any real
hacks or tricks for you.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:43.500
But it is step number
one on purpose.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.650
We have to know exactly
where we're going.

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:47.600
You can do this in two ways.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.910
Way number one is to
just pick something.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.220
Arbitrarily decide
I'm going to run a 5K.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:53.990
Or I've done that
before, and I want

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.300
to improve it by 10 seconds.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:57.830
I want to lose 10 pounds.

00:12:57.830 --> 00:12:58.760
You can just pick one.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.050
That's great.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:02.990
Another way is to run through
that fitness testing protocol

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:05.730
we described a few
episodes before.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.590
And if you do that, you can see
which of these areas that maybe

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:11.330
you have the largest lagging in.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:13.280
Or what is the most
severe performance anchor

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:14.690
is how we refer to it.

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:16.740
And then choose that
as your primary goal.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:17.660
So either option.

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:19.970
Some people come in
to training programs

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:21.530
with a very clear goal in mind.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.080
They want to add more muscle
or whatever, whatever.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:24.680
OK, great.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:27.470
If you're like, I
don't really know,

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:30.470
I just kind of work out,
then run the fitness testing

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:31.220
protocol.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.530
You'll see what score
is the lowest end.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.110
And then you'll maybe make
that a priority for the next,

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:37.213
say, three months.

00:13:37.213 --> 00:13:38.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So the first step

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:42.838
is to identify a specific or
set of specific training goals.

00:13:42.838 --> 00:13:44.630
ANDY GALPIN: A really
nice tool for helping

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:47.240
you set a goal is a
system called SMART.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.340
Right now there's a
little bit of debate

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:51.440
on what those acronyms
actually stand for.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.030
But we'll get close enough.

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:58.850
So SMART is often Specific,
Measurable, Attainable,

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:01.990
Realistic, and Timely.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:05.420
So starting off with
S, specific, in general

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:09.300
the more specific your goal
is, the higher likelihood

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.110
you will have at
succeeding in that.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.480
M, being measurable,
means it needs

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:17.370
to be something that you can
actually put a metric on.

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:19.220
So this can be
objective or subjective.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:23.000
But, generally, I like to have
at least one objective measure.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.400
So remembering
objective is something

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:26.757
that is not based on feeling.

00:14:26.757 --> 00:14:27.590
It is not up to you.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.798
This could be something
simple like your body weight.

00:14:29.798 --> 00:14:32.300
It could be how
much you can bench

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:35.218
press, what's your 1
mile time, whatever

00:14:35.218 --> 00:14:36.260
is most important to you.

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:39.210
It actually doesn't have to
be a fitness related goal.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:40.910
For example, if
you're using fitness

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:44.210
as a way to enhance your
sleep, the main metric

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:47.570
you may be interested in
is amount of hours slept.

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:49.190
It could be something
like efficiency

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:50.430
or whatever is most interesting.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:51.260
It could be work productivity.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:52.302
It doesn't really matter.

00:14:52.302 --> 00:14:54.800
So it doesn't have to
actually be the fitness goal.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.620
But what is the motivation
of why you're doing it?

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:00.990
So that's specific, measurable.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:04.070
Attainable or actionable,
as is often described,

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:07.730
is something that is
within your capability.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:10.610
So attainable-- a bad
example of attainable

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:13.820
is something like, my
goal is to win more games.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.475
That may not be up to you.

00:15:15.475 --> 00:15:16.850
The other team
you're playing, it

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.640
could influence it, the
schedule, et cetera.

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:21.230
So attainable
should be something

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:24.300
that is within your control.

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.540
Realistic or relevant
to you is something

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.318
that is, again, something
realistic that you can achieve.

00:15:30.318 --> 00:15:31.860
You wouldn't want
to make a goal that

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:34.333
is you want to double
your body mass.

00:15:34.333 --> 00:15:35.500
That is not going to happen.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:37.500
So think about the constraints.

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:38.167
How old are you?

00:15:38.167 --> 00:15:39.500
What's your training experience?

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.640
How much time do you really
have to invest in this?

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:43.390
And then pick something
that is realistic.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:44.970
And then, honestly,
my little twist

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:47.685
here is take that in minus 10%.

00:15:47.685 --> 00:15:50.310
Because, typically, when people
put together training programs,

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:53.070
their goal tends
to be quite lofty.

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:55.200
And they get some small
percentage of the way

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.990
and realize they're never going
to get there and then back off.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:00.210
We actually-- this
sort of reminds me

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.580
of a classic deception
study that we

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:06.480
did in my lab one time,
where we took people

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.180
and we had them do this
maximal front raise.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:12.180
Basically, you held a dumbbell
out in front of you for as long

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.410
as you possibly could.

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:16.782
And the goal here was actually--
it's a deception study.

00:16:16.782 --> 00:16:17.740
So we're tricking them.

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:19.365
And so we said, OK,
we want to just get

00:16:19.365 --> 00:16:21.840
normative values to
see how long people

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:23.160
can hold this front raise.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:26.407
And I think we use something
like 5% of their body weight.

00:16:26.407 --> 00:16:27.240
And so they came in.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:28.282
And they did it one time.

00:16:28.282 --> 00:16:29.070
And we timed them.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:30.445
They didn't get
to see the clock.

00:16:30.445 --> 00:16:31.230
They left.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.360
And then we said, we've got
to come back in and repeat it.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:34.818
We got to do a
couple of tries here

00:16:34.818 --> 00:16:37.770
to get a normal value in
case it's off, whatever.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:40.900
Well, the participants were
split up into four groups.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:46.320
So group one actually was told
that their time was 15% lower

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:47.970
than they actually got.

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:50.160
Group two was 5% lower.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.440
Group three was 5% higher.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:55.020
And group four was 15% higher.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:57.750
So the second time
they came in to do it,

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:00.960
our graduate student, quote
unquote, "made a mistake"

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.182
and left the timer
in front of them.

00:17:03.182 --> 00:17:05.099
So the first time again
they did the exercise,

00:17:05.099 --> 00:17:05.730
they're just holding it.

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:07.740
They don't have any idea
how long they're holding it.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:09.480
The second time,
they had a giant iPad

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.470
set like just a little
bit off centered

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:12.720
where they can clearly see it.

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:15.329
So they watched the time go by.

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.300
And, of course, what happened
was those folks, say,

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.119
who did 1 minute the very
first time when they tested,

00:17:21.119 --> 00:17:23.130
they came back in to
do the second time.

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:24.540
And they're holding it.

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:26.010
And we told them
they actually got

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:29.160
45 seconds, when in reality
they had done a minute.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:31.020
So they're holding
it and holding it.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:33.090
And they think they
only did 45 seconds.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:37.080
By the time they get to
like second 40, 41, 42,

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:38.790
they get past 45.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:42.660
They almost all quit,
like 47, 48 seconds,

00:17:42.660 --> 00:17:45.120
because they wanted to
beat their previous score

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.280
but then were like,
cool, I beat it.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:48.990
And then they quit early.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.100
So they were actually
not yet to failure.

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:52.890
But they were just
happy enough to beat

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:54.140
what they thought they'd done.

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:55.230
And then they quit.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:57.150
The other group on
the inverse side--

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:58.650
again, say they got a minute.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.290
We told them they got a
minute and 15 seconds.

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:04.260
They got to like 45
seconds, 50 seconds

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:07.470
and started realizing, oh, man,
I have 30 more seconds to go.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:12.470
And they quit way early because
the carrot was way too far out.

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:14.360
They realized I'm never
going to get there.

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.300
So I'm just going to stop now.

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:19.060
Can you guess which group did
the best on the post-test?

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The ones that
were just within about 5% of.

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:22.605
ANDY GALPIN: Totally.

00:18:22.605 --> 00:18:24.200
So they wanted to improve.

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:27.890
And so, again, say, they
got a minute the first time.

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.620
We told them they
got a minute 5--

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:32.780
or, sorry, they got a
minute 5 the first time.

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:34.280
We told them they
only got a minute.

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.280
They actually
exceeded that greatly

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:38.600
because they wanted the PR.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:42.440
So making sure that goal
is properly aligned,

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.730
it needs to be a
little bit scary,

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:46.810
a little bit unrealistic.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:48.393
You're going to have
to work for this.

00:18:48.393 --> 00:18:49.917
If it's too easy, you'll quit.

00:18:49.917 --> 00:18:51.250
You won't feel like a challenge.

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.080
If it's too hard, though,
you'll quit early as well.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:57.395
So you want to make sure it's
that reasonable balance of, ah,

00:18:57.395 --> 00:18:58.270
should I train today?

00:18:58.270 --> 00:18:59.770
Or like maybe I'll
just go through--

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:01.450
if I do that, I'm
not going to hit.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.920
I got to get after it.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:06.380
But not like, oh my God, like,
there's just no chance here.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.550
So you're going to
walk away early.

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:12.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's a
fantastic study, I have to say.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:13.870
It's very simple.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:16.570
I think it illustrates a number
of important psychological

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:20.210
principles about goal setting,
motivation, self perception,

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:23.590
but also the dopamine system.

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:28.510
The dopamine system is this
universal reward system

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:31.150
that-- meaning it
doesn't only work

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:34.090
for food or only work
for fitness goals

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.400
or only work for academic
goals or relationship goals.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.500
It is the universal
substrate for all of that.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:42.580
And I actually think there are
some real gems of information

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:45.730
in that study design
that you describe.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:48.963
So just cue that for maybe
a potential collaboration

00:19:48.963 --> 00:19:50.380
between our
laboratories because--

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.220
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.178
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --I
think it's very important.

00:19:53.178 --> 00:19:57.430
But it does cue up another
question relevant to fitness.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:20:00.420
In particular, which is
what are your thoughts

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:01.840
on intermediate goals?

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:07.590
So let's say my goal is
to drop 2% of body fat

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:09.940
from where I am now
a year from now.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:16.560
So roll into the next year from
now about 2% lower on body fat

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:23.360
but maintain my lean body mass
or maybe even increase it.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:26.560
How should I assess progress?

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:30.260
Because the dopamine
system loves a goal.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.240
It loves anticipation of a goal.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:35.840
But it responds best to, we
sort of re-up, if you will,

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:39.560
our dopamine any
time we get a signal

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:41.750
that we are on the right
track to that goal.

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:44.030
And that signal could be,
OK, I did the workout.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:45.980
I just trust that these
workouts are going

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:47.540
to give me the result I want.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.610
But, of course,
we know that when

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:54.920
people get a glimmer of the
idea or some objective feedback

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:57.680
that they're on the right path,
that dopamine system really

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:00.710
fires and provides
motivation for continuing

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:02.000
toward the ultimate goal.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:05.580
And as we've talked about
in the strength, speed,

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:08.540
and hypertrophy episode,
resistance training

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:11.267
itself has this built into
it because of the infusion

00:21:11.267 --> 00:21:12.350
of blood into the muscles.

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.570
You actually get a
little window into what

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:16.640
you might get in
terms of an adaptation

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:18.770
simply by way of
the so-called pump,

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:21.620
whereas endurance type work
generally doesn't have that.

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:23.690
You don't see yourself
get better drop back

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:25.100
and then adapting to
actually get better.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.350
But that's actually what you
see with weight training.

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:31.670
So given all of that contour
of the dopamine system, what

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:34.537
sorts of intermediate goals
should I set for myself

00:21:34.537 --> 00:21:36.120
or should somebody
set for themselves?

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:39.020
And I realize it will probably
depend on the ultimate goal.

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:43.980
But would you say check in
on progress once every week,

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.850
every month, three months?

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.420
ANDY GALPIN: I don't know if you
can tell the look on my face.

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:52.760
I love this question
and this topic.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:57.350
I spend so much time on my
senior and graduate level

00:21:57.350 --> 00:21:58.760
program design course.

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:01.040
I've been fortunate to
work with a few athletes

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.210
where we've had multiple years.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.840
And if you can really take
the time to step back and go--

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:08.720
it's not about optimizing
for the next six weeks.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:11.570
And in this case, it's
not the next fight.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:14.180
It is the championship
fight that we

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:15.470
need to get to in three years.

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:17.840
Or it is the Olympics,
which are on a quad program.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:19.940
You're really optimizing
for that for a year.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.940
If you can have that foresight
and really think about that

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:26.420
and then work backwards, you
can see some pretty tremendous

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:27.110
things.

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:30.290
The sort of saying
that is we tend

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:32.420
to overestimate
what we can get done

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:36.080
in a week and underestimate
what can happen in a year.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:39.180
That can be
extraordinarily powerful.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:42.410
However, you have to have
those metrics called out ahead

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:46.113
of time because you will lose
motivation in that short term

00:22:46.113 --> 00:22:48.030
because you won't see
that result immediately.

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:50.735
But if you remember, I'm
on a path to 4% or 2%

00:22:50.735 --> 00:22:52.610
or whatever you need to
be, therefore, I only

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:54.050
need to be this far right now.

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:56.520
I need to be that far
and then that far.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:57.630
It's actually quite clear.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:00.200
And so what we would
actually do in that scenario,

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:02.660
not to go so off track
here because I can really

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:04.910
go on this stuff, is--

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:07.830
let's say it was the
year recommendation.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:11.000
You're going to actually need
to go to the last part of SMART,

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:12.210
which is timely.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:14.210
So part of setting
this goal is making

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:17.240
sure you understand the
time domain responsible.

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:19.130
And it's actually
quite great here

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.590
because, not to go
Inception on us,

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:23.090
where we're like
list within a list,

00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:25.610
and Rob kills us over here.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:27.920
But number one of this
program design thing

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:29.000
was assessing your goal.

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:32.780
Number two is identifying
your defender.

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:35.000
What I mean by that is,
what is stopping you

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:36.750
from hitting that goal?

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:41.120
So you want to lose 2%
body fat in the next year.

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:42.350
OK, great.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:44.450
What's going to stop us?

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:45.950
Once we can achieve
that, and we'll

00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:47.600
go into more of
that in a second,

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:50.087
then you just start
walking that 2% backwards.

00:23:50.087 --> 00:23:51.920
So you might have to
go something like this.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:55.730
Look, every time I start
working out really hard,

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:57.650
I always get hurt.

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:58.490
Interesting.

00:23:58.490 --> 00:23:59.180
OK, great.

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:01.400
So maybe instead
of jumping really

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:06.170
hard into a high intensity
interval training program,

00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:09.890
knowing we're likely to hurt
something or get burnt out

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:13.340
or quit or whatever the
defender is for you, maybe

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:16.310
we invest something right
now, which is maybe improving

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:18.800
your flexibility or working on
movement technique, whatever

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:20.425
is going to stop you
from getting hurt.

00:24:20.425 --> 00:24:23.090
Or maybe we progress slower
so we don't get there.

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:25.850
That will allow us to do
the work necessary to hit

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:28.280
that goal 12 months from
now, not two weeks from now,

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:29.957
not two months from now.

00:24:29.957 --> 00:24:31.040
Maybe that's not the case.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.800
Maybe you're like, no,
look, hey, I move well.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:35.700
I feel like I'm in decent shape.

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:38.030
I've got enough
muscle mass on me.

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:39.440
We've talked in
previous episodes

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:41.660
why having insufficient
muscle mass

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:44.120
is sometimes detrimental
for fat loss.

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:45.600
So you checked all that boxes.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.380
I don't get hurt very often.

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:50.730
I got equipment
around, no problem.

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:52.520
I've got the time
in my schedule.

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:53.760
And I have enough muscle.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:54.260
Great.

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:56.100
Well, now we maybe just
split it up and say,

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:57.500
look, we got 12 months.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:58.250
We got 2%.

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:00.860
It's as simple as doing
half a percent per quarter

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:02.267
of the year.

00:25:02.267 --> 00:25:04.100
And now all we're looking
at is that number.

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:08.040
I don't have to necessarily
get all these things done.

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:10.925
I can go a quarter, half
percent, half percent,

00:25:10.925 --> 00:25:12.050
half percent, half percent.

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:13.920
You're going to get there.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:17.280
The other scenario that
I laid out a second ago,

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:19.280
it maybe needs to look
like something like this.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:22.520
Quarter one is going to be 0%.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:24.890
Well, yeah, that's
right, you may not

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:28.140
lose a pound for the
next three months.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:28.723
We don't care.

00:25:28.723 --> 00:25:30.390
That's not the goal
of these treatments.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:31.810
I know that's the
goal this year.

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.227
That's our major
macro cycle goal.

00:25:33.227 --> 00:25:34.268
We're going to get there.

00:25:34.268 --> 00:25:35.790
But to get there
most effectively,

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:39.960
we need to invest in working
more with your chiropractor

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:41.790
or whatever the thing is.

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:44.325
That will allow us to
then go half a percent

00:25:44.325 --> 00:25:46.290
quarter two, when we can
really start training.

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:47.623
But we're going to ramp into it.

00:25:47.623 --> 00:25:50.100
Quarter three we're going to
go another half a percent.

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:51.780
And now we're halfway there.

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:55.032
Quarter four, we've invested
so much you're ready to go.

00:25:55.032 --> 00:25:55.990
We're going to go hard.

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:58.620
We're going to get that
last 1%, that last quarter.

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.300
And we're going to get there.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:01.505
And you won't be hurt.

00:26:01.505 --> 00:26:03.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So that
makes it very clear.

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:06.800
And I can also envision
how the precise structure

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:08.900
of these intermediate
goals would

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:12.680
vary depending on what sort
of adaptation one is pursuing.

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:15.680
And I do remember from
our previous episodes

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:18.000
that fat loss itself
is not an adaptation.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:19.770
It is a byproduct of
other adaptations.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:21.745
So I just want to make
sure that you know

00:26:21.745 --> 00:26:22.970
that I was paying attention.

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:24.570
It's committed to memory.

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:26.233
ANDY GALPIN: Absolutely.

00:26:26.233 --> 00:26:28.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Some
goals, such as fat loss,

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:30.280
are very quantifiable.

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:32.380
And yet, they might
not be linear.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:34.070
It's hard to know--

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:37.540
the assumption is if you
ingest x fewer calories than

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:40.090
are required per day, then
you'll lose x amount of weight,

00:26:40.090 --> 00:26:41.500
some percentage from body fat.

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:44.860
I think that cues
up the idea that we

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:48.095
need to build some
flexibility into our thinking

00:26:48.095 --> 00:26:50.470
about these intermediate goals
in order to just make sure

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:55.060
that dopamine system isn't
tethered to exact numbers

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:57.940
because after all a
reduction in 2% body fat,

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:02.110
for instance, is really
a desire to achieve

00:27:02.110 --> 00:27:05.403
a different sort of overall body
composition or recomposition.

00:27:05.403 --> 00:27:07.570
I don't know, by the way,
that that's my exact goal.

00:27:07.570 --> 00:27:12.460
I think one of my goals is to
be able to run a mile faster.

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:15.400
And I'm sort of haunted
by this experience

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:17.170
of wanting to run
cross-country in college

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:18.520
and trying to walk on.

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:20.050
We weren't a Division I school.

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:23.680
But the threshold for being
considered for the team

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:26.650
was you had to run a sub
10-minute 2 mile, which turns

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:28.180
out to be very, very fast.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:29.710
ANDY GALPIN: That's really hard.

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:31.168
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I did not do that.

00:27:31.168 --> 00:27:34.615
I didn't even come close.

00:27:34.615 --> 00:27:39.820
And I don't think that I
could reasonably do that now.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.730
I'm not interested in committing
to the kind of training

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:43.420
required.

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:47.132
The sacrifice isn't
meaningful enough for me.

00:27:47.132 --> 00:27:48.340
ANDY GALPIN: Fair and honest.

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:52.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
lowering one's time

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:56.080
to run a mile by,
I don't know, 10%

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:59.170
seems like a reasonable
goal across six months.

00:27:59.170 --> 00:27:59.920
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:00.878
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

00:28:00.878 --> 00:28:03.850
So in the case of a
goal like that, clearly

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:05.620
there are specific
training programs.

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:07.600
But this raises
the issue of, what

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:09.740
if I have other goals as well?

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:12.910
And at what point
do people having

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.620
multiple goals start to set
up collisions between goals?

00:28:15.620 --> 00:28:18.190
How do we know whether or
not something is reasonable

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:21.580
not just on its own but because
of the other things that one

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:24.050
has structured
into their program.

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:28.330
So being able to reduce a mile
time by 10% in six months,

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:29.890
OK, maybe that's doable.

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:30.760
Maybe it's not.

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:31.660
You can tell me.

00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:35.320
But also being able to
double the amount that they

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:40.720
can do for single repetition
leg extension for that matter,

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:44.680
at the same time those seem
like incompatible goals.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:46.340
ANDY GALPIN: So a
couple of things.

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:51.490
Number one, the more
specific and precise

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:54.160
you can be with a
single goal, the faster

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:57.050
you will get there, generally.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:29:01.530
So in theory, if you had one
thing you wanted to achieve,

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.567
the best way to go about
it is to focus on that.

00:29:03.567 --> 00:29:04.650
Give it the most priority.

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:07.108
That doesn't mean you can't do
anything else along the way.

00:29:07.108 --> 00:29:07.650
You can.

00:29:07.650 --> 00:29:09.150
But you would want
to focus on that.

00:29:09.150 --> 00:29:13.060
The more additional
goals you bring in,

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:16.853
the more distraction you're
creating for that primary goal.

00:29:16.853 --> 00:29:18.270
Depending on what
those goals are,

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:20.920
you can actually do
them at the same time.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.618
Some other combinations
are less effective.

00:29:23.618 --> 00:29:24.660
Think about it like this.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:26.700
We went through those
nine adaptations.

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:30.030
And we went through them in
a specific order on purpose.

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:33.540
The closer those adaptations
are together in that list,

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:36.420
the more compatible they
are to training each other.

00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:39.550
The further away, they
become more challenging.

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:41.010
So just to give a few examples.

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:43.320
If you wanted to improve
your speed in power,

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:45.540
you could basically train
those simultaneously.

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:47.540
They would not interfere
with each other at all.

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:51.780
And, in fact, since power
is speed times force,

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:53.402
it would be complementary.

00:29:53.402 --> 00:29:55.110
If you just walk down
the line from there

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:56.770
to strength, hey, same thing.

00:29:56.770 --> 00:30:00.000
If you get faster, that's
going to aid in strength

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.680
because force is mass
times acceleration.

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:04.740
So if you improve
acceleration, you're

00:30:04.740 --> 00:30:05.790
contributing to strength.

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:07.080
Same thing with power.

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:09.693
So speed, power, and
strength are generally

00:30:09.693 --> 00:30:10.485
very complementary.

00:30:10.485 --> 00:30:13.710
You can absolutely train
all three of those goals

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:15.870
at the same time
and have no issues.

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:18.750
Getting into
hypertrophy, now we've

00:30:18.750 --> 00:30:20.340
got a little bit of distinction.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:24.030
If you're going to train
strength and hypertrophy, as we

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:27.090
talked about in that
episode, at the base

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:28.710
those are going to
be complementary.

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:30.360
You add on some muscle.

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:31.620
You're going to get stronger.

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:33.000
You start training for strength.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.760
It's probably going to help
you out on some muscle mass.

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:38.460
As you get to the
end of that spectrum,

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:40.110
the overlap between
the two starts

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:42.330
to go away, such
that if you truly

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:45.810
wanted to maximize strength
above everything else,

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:48.120
if you continue to train
for hypertrophy as well,

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:50.220
that's going to take
too many resources out

00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:51.300
of your recovery bin.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:52.680
And you won't be
able to do that.

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:55.020
The inverse would
also be the same.

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:56.700
If you're training
to maximize strength,

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.950
you wouldn't be able
to put enough volume on

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:01.020
to get sufficient hypertrophy.

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:06.520
So if you wanted to then
combine speed with hypertrophy,

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.570
you're going farther
away from each other,

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:10.900
which means it's going to be
more and more distraction.

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:13.960
So the hypertrophy training
would cause a ton of fatigue.

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:16.210
You wouldn't be able to go
at max speed for your speed

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:17.108
or power strength.

00:31:17.108 --> 00:31:19.150
So you're going to be
compromising those results.

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:22.450
Now, speed training
won't compromise

00:31:22.450 --> 00:31:25.595
your hypertrophy training
because it's non fatiguing.

00:31:25.595 --> 00:31:27.220
And so, boom, here
we have a little bit

00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:30.530
of an interference effect one
way but probably not the other.

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:32.320
Let's move down the
spectrum one more time

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:33.280
and get into endurance.

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:35.510
We won't go through
all of these things.

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:37.060
But you're getting
the idea here.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:39.700
Oh, a little bit of high
intensity intervals?

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:40.870
OK, cool.

00:31:40.870 --> 00:31:45.940
Now, would that compromise
my speed, power, or strength?

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:49.750
Probably because there's a
little bit of residual fatigue.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:52.988
If the volume was low
enough, then you'd be fine.

00:31:52.988 --> 00:31:55.030
All you're worried about
there is not necessarily

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:57.200
like some sort of
cellular mechanism.

00:31:57.200 --> 00:31:58.850
It's just simple fatigue.

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:00.430
It is amount of
energy expenditure

00:32:00.430 --> 00:32:03.730
versus is that compromising
my recovery to come back.

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.590
Would those first three
or four of those-- speed,

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:09.940
power, strength--
interfere with your ability

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:12.070
to elevate your
anaerobic capacity?

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:13.990
Probably not.

00:32:13.990 --> 00:32:15.520
Almost surely, in
fact, if you look

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:18.460
at any of the literature
on endurance training,

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:23.730
you will see that speed, power,
and strength almost always

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:26.240
improve endurance.

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:31.130
Endurance training added on top
of strength can be detrimental,

00:32:31.130 --> 00:32:33.920
can have a neutral
effect, but generally

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:37.280
doesn't help one get stronger by
adding additional conditioning

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:41.030
unless you're so unfit you can't
get through the volume needed

00:32:41.030 --> 00:32:42.710
in the strength training.

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:46.530
One more example here so we
don't drag this out too far.

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:51.580
In the case of something like
I want to lose fat, well, hey,

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:53.800
we don't have to worry
about interference.

00:32:53.800 --> 00:32:55.090
It doesn't really matter.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:59.170
If you're fatigued for
your hypertrophy session,

00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:00.083
not a big deal.

00:33:00.083 --> 00:33:01.750
We're just trying to
get some work done.

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:03.130
If your hypertrophy
session fatigued you

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:04.480
from your
conditioning, it's cool

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:05.730
because you got the work done.

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:07.730
So you don't have to
worry about it so much.

00:33:07.730 --> 00:33:10.018
So it really kind of
depends on the actual goal.

00:33:10.018 --> 00:33:11.560
And what you want
to pay attention to

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:14.830
is, actually, what are the
chances of overlap, which

00:33:14.830 --> 00:33:17.350
means like what are
the adaptations you

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:20.737
get physiologically that cross
over from one to the other?

00:33:20.737 --> 00:33:22.570
And then what are the
ones that are actually

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:23.695
going to start interfering?

00:33:23.695 --> 00:33:28.090
In fact, in my class, I
have this giant matrix chart

00:33:28.090 --> 00:33:30.607
of interference effect
going from adaptations

00:33:30.607 --> 00:33:32.440
through a whole bunch
of systems, everything

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:36.790
from handling pH to
lymphatic drainage

00:33:36.790 --> 00:33:38.225
to bone marrow
density, et cetera.

00:33:38.225 --> 00:33:39.850
You can walk through
these whole things

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:43.690
and see which ones actually have
a positive effect, which ones

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:45.850
have a massive positive
effect, and then

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:48.225
which ones actually have a
little bit of an interference.

00:33:48.225 --> 00:33:49.990
And perhaps if you
guys are nice enough,

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:53.530
we could throw that into
a newsletter or something,

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:54.447
some PDF or something.

00:33:54.447 --> 00:33:56.863
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think that
would be immensely valuable.

00:33:56.863 --> 00:33:59.170
I think some of that more
extensive information,

00:33:59.170 --> 00:34:02.530
when it's laid out in grid form
like that is really useful.

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:04.690
So we should-- well,
now we've said it.

00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:05.890
So we--

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:06.670
ANDY GALPIN: I didn't
say I would do it.

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:07.450
I said perhaps.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:09.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
think it's a great idea.

00:34:09.650 --> 00:34:12.190
I think it's a terrific idea.

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:16.300
The idea that items
closer to each other

00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:18.280
on the list of those nine
different adaptations

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:20.770
are going to be easier to
achieve in parallel than items

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:23.580
further apart makes
perfect sense.

00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:28.500
And what I heard was that
there's a few caveats that

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:29.639
might seem minor.

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:32.909
But they're actually
quite important,

00:34:32.909 --> 00:34:36.139
such as anything that is
relatively low intensity

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:39.080
and doesn't impede
recovery can probably

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:41.010
be included as a parallel goal.

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:43.429
So some speed work
in conjunction

00:34:43.429 --> 00:34:46.250
with some long duration cardio
work, versus even though we're

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:49.040
talking about number 2 on that
list and number 9 on that list.

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:50.873
ANDY GALPIN: In that
case, the long duration

00:34:50.873 --> 00:34:52.580
endurance, even if
it's low intensity,

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:55.159
may actually interfere
with the speed

00:34:55.159 --> 00:34:56.870
if the volume gets too high.

00:34:56.870 --> 00:35:01.220
If you're talking about,
I went on a 30 minute jog,

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:03.290
for most people,
it's totally fine.

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:04.790
What we're really
talking about here

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:06.950
is when the miles
start piling up.

00:35:06.950 --> 00:35:10.220
And the time really starts
getting there in combination

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:12.320
with some of the
things-- the factors

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:14.840
we've talked about earlier,
which is exercise choice.

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:17.870
So more eccentric landing
based exercise choices.

00:35:17.870 --> 00:35:21.260
Running, for example, is
more likely to interfere

00:35:21.260 --> 00:35:24.380
than cycling because
you're not landing.

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:25.338
Swimming is low impact.

00:35:25.338 --> 00:35:26.922
So if you're going
to do those things,

00:35:26.922 --> 00:35:29.450
you can hedge your bets a little
bit by choosing an exercise

00:35:29.450 --> 00:35:31.070
choice that is less impactful.

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:33.320
Again, if we're literally--
because there's oftentimes

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:36.685
confusing here is like,
oh, don't do 10 minutes

00:35:36.685 --> 00:35:38.060
on the treadmill
before you lift.

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:38.935
You're going to cut--

00:35:38.935 --> 00:35:40.940
oh, time out.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:42.210
Warm up is fine.

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:44.510
We're really talking about
probably more than 30

00:35:44.510 --> 00:35:48.662
plus minutes at higher
than 60% heart rate--

00:35:48.662 --> 00:35:50.120
random number,
something like that,

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:52.100
depends on the
person, et cetera.

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:55.340
But it has to be a decent chunk.

00:35:55.340 --> 00:35:58.520
Again, you can actually fix
that by then just consuming

00:35:58.520 --> 00:35:59.120
calories.

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:02.060
You can also fix that by
making sure everything

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:05.108
else in the hidden and visible
stressor bucket is improved.

00:36:05.108 --> 00:36:06.650
So that's just like
one of our tricks

00:36:06.650 --> 00:36:08.660
that we'll get into when
we get to the recovery,

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:11.990
is you don't necessarily need
to reduce your training if you

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:13.362
just ramp up your recovery.

