00:37:06.500 --> 00:37:10.220
I'm going to take the liberty of
assuming that most people fall

00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:15.180
into one of either three
bins in terms of their goals,

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:16.075
again, most people.

00:37:16.075 --> 00:37:17.700
Certainly, there are
going to be people

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:21.600
that lie outside these bins.

00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:25.710
I think if you polled 100 people
or 100,000 or 1 million people

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:28.470
as to what their major
goals were in working out,

00:37:28.470 --> 00:37:32.610
they'd say, as you so
nicely listed out before,

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.880
aesthetic changes,
functionality, and longevity.

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:39.180
But that one in three
really kind of sit higher

00:37:39.180 --> 00:37:42.390
than most people would
like to perhaps even admit.

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:44.400
They want to look good,
which usually means

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:47.772
they want to lose some fat, gain
some muscle in specific places.

00:37:47.772 --> 00:37:49.230
I realize there
are folks out there

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:52.770
who want to gain a lot of muscle
and just muscle everywhere.

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:54.780
But I think most
people would like

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:57.660
to have a little more
shape here, a little more

00:37:57.660 --> 00:38:00.120
muscle there to either
balance out their aesthetic

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:02.700
or to accentuate certain
parts of their physique.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:06.360
And they would probably
like to shave off

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:09.480
some subcutaneous fat, although
there are those exceptionally

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:11.190
lean people out there.

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:13.020
And they exist too.

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:15.690
I think it would be gain
muscle in specific places,

00:38:15.690 --> 00:38:18.090
lose fat, and do
it in a way that

00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:21.090
also provides some boost
to their health span

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:22.050
and longevity.

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:26.640
I would say that that might
even be 50% of people out there.

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:30.060
Again, I'm taking the
liberty of guesstimating.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:34.530
Another bin I would venture is
interested in getting stronger

00:38:34.530 --> 00:38:35.970
and putting on more muscle.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.440
Certainly, there are a
number of people that

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:38.648
are interested in doing that.

00:38:38.648 --> 00:38:40.170
And that could
even be more muscle

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:44.130
all over or more muscle
with some accentuation

00:38:44.130 --> 00:38:45.930
to certain areas
where they happen

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:49.650
to be weaker or less
developed, as it were.

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:52.080
And then the third bin
would be people that really

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:54.150
enjoy cardiovascular work.

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:57.360
Oh, I should say the
second bin probably care

00:38:57.360 --> 00:38:59.070
about their longevity also.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:03.240
But it's not really foremost,
like, yeah, I feel great now,

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:06.010
and I'll live to be whatever.

00:39:06.010 --> 00:39:08.280
But I only want to do it
if I get that much muscle.

00:39:08.280 --> 00:39:10.080
We know these kinds of folks.

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.540
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, I run a
poll in my class every year

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:14.100
when I ask, what,
you guys all lift?

00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:16.180
And, of course, I make
them put their hand up,

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:17.270
like, you guys are
in my class, you're

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:18.960
going to put your hand
up, let you lift weights.

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:20.580
And then I ask,
why do you train?

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:23.280
And long term health
is like on the list.

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:24.600
And they all--

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:27.690
I'm like any of you that
selected health are liars.

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:28.830
You're 20 to 25.

00:39:28.830 --> 00:39:30.510
You are not
exercising for health.

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:33.210
You are exercising because
you want to look a certain way

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:34.050
or get stronger.

00:39:34.050 --> 00:39:36.030
Once you get past that
undergraduate age,

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:41.310
though, the actual desire to
live longer and better actually

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:42.390
becomes pretty real.

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:43.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
I think that there are

00:39:43.530 --> 00:39:44.830
people who want to feel better.

00:39:44.830 --> 00:39:47.580
They know that exercise and
the results from exercise

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:48.730
can make them feel better.

00:39:48.730 --> 00:39:50.855
But, yeah, that second bin
tends to be more focused

00:39:50.855 --> 00:39:53.130
on the aesthetic changes,
it seems, or being strong.

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:56.460
And then the third category,
I think, are people--

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:58.200
I know a lot of
folks like this, who

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:01.740
really enjoy what are normally
considered endurance type

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:02.240
activities.

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:03.865
And here I just want
to highlight again

00:40:03.865 --> 00:40:05.340
what you so
beautifully illustrated

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:08.010
in previous episodes, that you
can gain a lot of endurance

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:09.330
even using weights or machines.

00:40:09.330 --> 00:40:11.250
It just depends on
how you use them.

00:40:11.250 --> 00:40:12.850
It's not about the exercise.

00:40:12.850 --> 00:40:15.600
It's about how you perform
them and et cetera.

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:18.630
And you, again, beautifully
provided all those details

00:40:18.630 --> 00:40:21.330
as how to create endurance
regardless of equipment

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:23.240
standards, et cetera.

00:40:23.240 --> 00:40:25.340
But that third
category seem to be

00:40:25.340 --> 00:40:29.210
people who enjoy running,
cycling, swimming, hiking,

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:32.120
dancing, activities
that they can

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:33.320
do for long periods of time.

00:40:33.320 --> 00:40:35.210
That often will
involve some sort

00:40:35.210 --> 00:40:39.680
of skill that is based on
improving motor patterns,

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:42.320
maybe not so much
stride but certainly

00:40:42.320 --> 00:40:45.320
for people that really
love tennis, people

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:47.930
that love a sport, like golf.

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:49.700
They want to be able
to not just walk.

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:51.870
They want to walk the 18 holes.

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:53.870
They want to have a great
golf swing, et cetera.

00:40:53.870 --> 00:40:54.620
I'm not a golf player.

00:40:54.620 --> 00:40:56.310
So forgive me if my
nomenclature is off.

00:40:56.310 --> 00:40:56.850
So there are three--

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:57.470
ANDY GALPIN: Golfer.

00:40:57.470 --> 00:40:58.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, golfers.

00:40:58.010 --> 00:40:58.970
ANDY GALPIN: You
don't play golf.

00:40:58.970 --> 00:40:59.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, got it.

00:40:59.510 --> 00:41:00.020
You don't play golf.

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:00.710
ANDY GALPIN: Well,
you play golf.

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:02.372
You wouldn't call a golf player.

00:41:02.372 --> 00:41:04.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I played
miniature golf a few times.

00:41:04.580 --> 00:41:06.797
And that's about it,
although Stanford

00:41:06.797 --> 00:41:08.630
does have a beautiful
golf course, I'm told.

00:41:08.630 --> 00:41:10.250
I should learn how to play golf.

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:11.600
ANDY GALPIN: I'll come up and
play it for you if you want.

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:12.050
You come up.

00:41:12.050 --> 00:41:12.410
I'll play it.

00:41:12.410 --> 00:41:13.520
I'll tell you how it goes.

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:14.210
Get me on that course.

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:16.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I can
almost see it from my lap.

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:19.850
So category one, I think, is a
significant fraction of people.

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:24.470
So as we lay out these
different ways to assess goals

00:41:24.470 --> 00:41:29.660
and as we approach the
structure of a program,

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:32.630
as you'll tell us-- if we could
perhaps touch back to those

00:41:32.630 --> 00:41:34.640
every once in a while.

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:36.530
Again, I'm taking the
liberty of assuming

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:40.640
that we will net about 80% to
90% of people out there, again,

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:44.270
those categories being people
who want to lose some fat,

00:41:44.270 --> 00:41:46.820
maybe build some muscle in
specific areas on their body,

00:41:46.820 --> 00:41:48.250
and really want to be healthy.

00:41:48.250 --> 00:41:49.250
They want to feel great.

00:41:49.250 --> 00:41:53.117
And they want to have a long
health span and lifespan.

00:41:53.117 --> 00:41:54.950
They want to live a
long time feeling great.

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:57.815
Second category, people want to
build more muscle and strength.

00:41:57.815 --> 00:41:59.690
Sure, they don't want
to damage their health.

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:02.060
But that's not their main focus.

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:04.610
Their main focus is on
building muscle and strength.

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:06.470
And then that third
category of people

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:10.100
who really want to do more
endurance type work, feel

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:12.830
great and strong
doing it but not

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:15.800
because they can carry heavy
weights while they're doing it.

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:17.660
But rather, they can
feel vital and they

00:42:17.660 --> 00:42:21.890
can push harder for longer
and maybe even translate that

00:42:21.890 --> 00:42:24.530
to some of the more recreational
type activities or sports,

00:42:24.530 --> 00:42:26.840
like tennis, and things
that are more long duration,

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:28.762
playing soccer or
maybe even softball

00:42:28.762 --> 00:42:29.720
or things of that sort.

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:30.710
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah,
surfing, swimming.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:31.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So those
three categories-- maybe we

00:42:31.850 --> 00:42:35.210
could call those bin A, B, and C
for sake of today's discussion.

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:37.432
I think if you're
willing to embrace--

00:42:37.432 --> 00:42:38.640
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, I love it.

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:41.057
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --I think
that will be informative toward

00:42:41.057 --> 00:42:42.800
our listeners in a way that--

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:46.250
simply not assuming what
people's different goals are

00:42:46.250 --> 00:42:47.780
might not be able to accomplish.

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:50.167
Said differently,
hopefully, by doing that,

00:42:50.167 --> 00:42:52.250
people will derive a lot
more from the description

00:42:52.250 --> 00:42:53.540
of the program that
you're going to give us.

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:54.080
ANDY GALPIN: Love it.

00:42:54.080 --> 00:42:56.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Now,
I am certain that I

00:42:56.000 --> 00:42:58.490
want to let you return to
your list of the five things

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:02.030
that people need to consider
when establishing a exercise

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:02.570
program.

00:43:02.570 --> 00:43:04.237
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah,
great, let's do that.

00:43:04.237 --> 00:43:07.550
I also do want to acknowledge
a point you've brought up.

00:43:07.550 --> 00:43:10.220
Exercise doesn't mean
just lifting weights.

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:11.208
That's my background.

00:43:11.208 --> 00:43:12.500
That's what I spend my time on.

00:43:12.500 --> 00:43:16.020
So I sort of default to
examples in that category.

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:17.630
But it doesn't have to be that.

00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:19.910
You've articulated
plenty of other ways

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:24.500
where you can get amazing forms
of exercise that have nothing

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:25.740
to do with lifting weights.

00:43:25.740 --> 00:43:27.630
So for those folks in--

00:43:27.630 --> 00:43:29.360
was it bin C or 3?

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:30.193
I can't remember it.

00:43:30.193 --> 00:43:32.277
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Bin C.
We go A is, again, muscle,

00:43:32.277 --> 00:43:34.010
lose fat, be healthy
now and forever.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:37.790
Bin B is get
stronger, gain muscle,

00:43:37.790 --> 00:43:40.610
don't damage your health, but
not really focus on health

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:41.870
in the immediate term.

00:43:41.870 --> 00:43:50.180
And then bin C is want to play
or do endurance type activities

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:52.700
and, quote unquote,
"feel strong doing it,"

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:55.100
so have more vigor to be
able to do that longer

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.890
and maybe with more attention
to skill, et cetera,

00:43:57.890 --> 00:44:00.200
and, of course, also want
to improve their health.

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:01.190
ANDY GALPIN: Well, what
you've effectively done

00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:03.620
is you've given us three
different avatars with three

00:44:03.620 --> 00:44:05.030
different goals.

00:44:05.030 --> 00:44:08.390
So the next step for
each person or group

00:44:08.390 --> 00:44:10.340
is going to be to
identify their defenders.

00:44:10.340 --> 00:44:12.260
But before we get
that, we've got

00:44:12.260 --> 00:44:14.460
to close the loop
on this SMART thing.

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:17.240
So in each case,
they have either

00:44:17.240 --> 00:44:20.150
chosen that goal based on
their personal preference.

00:44:20.150 --> 00:44:23.270
Or perhaps they did our
fitness testing protocol

00:44:23.270 --> 00:44:25.220
and realized they
need to gain strength.

00:44:25.220 --> 00:44:29.285
So whether the reason they
chose to be in buckets B or A

00:44:29.285 --> 00:44:32.900
or C was because of our protocol
or just personal preference.

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:34.020
It really doesn't matter.

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:37.850
They still want to go through
this process of laying out

00:44:37.850 --> 00:44:41.060
their goals and making sure,
again, they are specific.

00:44:41.060 --> 00:44:44.250
So let's go through bin
C, which is a great one.

00:44:44.250 --> 00:44:46.130
So you want to have more energy.

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:47.600
And you want to
feel stronger when

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:49.393
you're doing your kiteboarding.

00:44:49.393 --> 00:44:50.810
You want to feel
stronger when you

00:44:50.810 --> 00:44:54.470
finish your round of tennis,
round of golf, game of tennis.

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:55.553
OK, great.

00:44:55.553 --> 00:44:56.720
That's a different strength.

00:44:56.720 --> 00:44:58.640
Absolutely see, I hear, I know
what you're saying though.

00:44:58.640 --> 00:44:59.300
Amazing.

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:03.020
So that goal needs to
be specific to that.

00:45:03.020 --> 00:45:06.560
So it would be hard to make
a goal like, I want to feel

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:09.190
better at the end of my round.

00:45:09.190 --> 00:45:11.800
Boy, that depends on
too many other factors.

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:14.350
A better goal would be
something like this.

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:18.520
I want to be able to run
this 2-mile loop that I

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:20.020
do around my neighborhood.

00:45:20.020 --> 00:45:25.300
And I want to do it and have
a lower heart rate at the end.

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:28.750
Or I want to be able
to get my heart rate

00:45:28.750 --> 00:45:31.240
recovery back faster.

00:45:31.240 --> 00:45:34.610
Amazing, that will probably
align with you feeling,

00:45:34.610 --> 00:45:36.700
quote unquote,
"stronger" with it.

00:45:36.700 --> 00:45:37.810
So I did the same course.

00:45:37.810 --> 00:45:39.970
And either I could do
it at the same speed

00:45:39.970 --> 00:45:44.530
and it's not nearly as hard or
I can go faster, whichever one.

00:45:44.530 --> 00:45:45.280
It doesn't matter.

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:47.660
But that would be an
example of a specific goal.

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:49.870
The other buckets you
laid out kind of already

00:45:49.870 --> 00:45:52.630
have specific goals, like
I want to get stronger.

00:45:52.630 --> 00:45:54.290
Well, that's going
to be the goal.

00:45:54.290 --> 00:45:58.360
The other one is going to
be, I want to lose some fat.

00:45:58.360 --> 00:45:59.945
The goal is sort of
implicit in that.

00:45:59.945 --> 00:46:01.820
It's the other people
where you're just like,

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:03.112
I don't really care about that.

00:46:03.112 --> 00:46:05.680
I just want to be able
to surf the great waves

00:46:05.680 --> 00:46:08.320
and then not feel
exhausted afterwards.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:09.190
All right, cool.

00:46:09.190 --> 00:46:11.920
Well, then you still
should pick a metric

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:14.440
that is not that activity
maybe because it won't

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:15.820
be within your
control, depending

00:46:15.820 --> 00:46:17.987
on the waves and the
temperature and all that stuff,

00:46:17.987 --> 00:46:22.090
that you can use as a proxy to
say, if I were to do something

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:24.610
that represented me feeling
probably better when I surf,

00:46:24.610 --> 00:46:25.210
what would that be?

00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:26.085
And it's not perfect.

00:46:26.085 --> 00:46:28.990
But it would be still as
specific as you could get.

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:31.510
You still want to make
sure it's measurable.

00:46:31.510 --> 00:46:33.100
Again, this example
might be something

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:36.460
like you're going to
go to the pool and time

00:46:36.460 --> 00:46:39.340
how long it takes you to
swim 800 meters or something.

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:40.300
It's attainable.

00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:42.670
And then you'll set a goal
that's realistic and timely.

00:46:42.670 --> 00:46:47.020
I'm going to improve by
5% in the next two months.

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:49.360
OK, cool, that probably
falls in the realm.

00:46:49.360 --> 00:46:50.980
And then you're
making the assumption

00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:52.957
that if you did
that, you'll probably

00:46:52.957 --> 00:46:55.540
feel better when you go out to
do your primary activity, which

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:57.130
is, say, surf.

00:46:57.130 --> 00:46:59.500
The reality of it
is every time we

00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:02.380
work with an actual
athlete, that's what we do.

00:47:02.380 --> 00:47:04.967
Athletes don't come
to us to lift weights.

00:47:04.967 --> 00:47:06.550
They don't come to
us to get stronger.

00:47:06.550 --> 00:47:08.723
They come to us because
they want to play better.

00:47:08.723 --> 00:47:10.390
And they want to stay
on the field more.

00:47:10.390 --> 00:47:11.932
What we're trying
to convince them of

00:47:11.932 --> 00:47:14.170
is if you do this
thing in the gym, then

00:47:14.170 --> 00:47:16.630
that should translate into you
being better at your sport,

00:47:16.630 --> 00:47:19.090
recovering faster,
being less injured.

00:47:19.090 --> 00:47:20.610
But it's still just a proxy.

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:23.110
And so that's all you're doing
with these other non-specific

00:47:23.110 --> 00:47:25.450
goals, especially when they're
performance based goals.

00:47:25.450 --> 00:47:28.037
And we didn't
package it that way.

00:47:28.037 --> 00:47:30.370
But that's really what you
talked about for bin C there.

00:47:30.370 --> 00:47:31.720
It is a performance based goal.

00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:34.570
I want to be able to perform
when I'm in the field.

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:36.520
In my brain, that's a sport.

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:38.590
In your brain, it's when
you're at yoga class.

00:47:38.590 --> 00:47:40.420
It's the same thing.

00:47:40.420 --> 00:47:43.250
We've said this earlier in our
series that if you have a body,

00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:44.900
you're an athlete.

00:47:44.900 --> 00:47:47.230
I want to prepare your
body so that it can do

00:47:47.230 --> 00:47:49.360
exactly what you want it to do.

00:47:49.360 --> 00:47:52.120
You then get to have the choice
of what you ask it to do.

00:47:52.120 --> 00:47:53.020
You call it a sport.

00:47:53.020 --> 00:47:55.020
You call it your Saturday
hike with your family.

00:47:55.020 --> 00:47:55.940
I don't really care.

00:47:55.940 --> 00:47:56.920
It's the same thing.

00:47:56.920 --> 00:47:59.590
You get control of
your body performing

00:47:59.590 --> 00:48:01.100
the way you want it to perform.

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:02.950
And that's what this
whole thing is about.

00:48:02.950 --> 00:48:03.460
Great.

00:48:03.460 --> 00:48:05.080
So now that we've
covered, I think,

00:48:05.080 --> 00:48:10.270
as much as we need to regarding
assessing and choosing a goal,

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:13.120
I want to get back to this idea
of identifying your defender.

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:15.040
So you really need
to think carefully

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:18.500
about what is stopping you
from hitting those goals.

00:48:18.500 --> 00:48:20.690
And so you're
forecasting a little bit.

00:48:20.690 --> 00:48:23.770
You're also going back into
your own personal history.

00:48:23.770 --> 00:48:26.110
Do you have history
of knee pain?

00:48:26.110 --> 00:48:29.980
Do you have a history
of working too much?

00:48:29.980 --> 00:48:31.957
Do you have a history
of a lot of travel?

00:48:31.957 --> 00:48:33.790
Do you have a history
of getting sick a lot?

00:48:33.790 --> 00:48:35.457
What are these things
that are happening

00:48:35.457 --> 00:48:38.055
that are going to stop you
from hitting your goal?

00:48:38.055 --> 00:48:39.430
A couple of examples
I've already

00:48:39.430 --> 00:48:41.780
laid out so we don't need
to go too much longer here.

00:48:41.780 --> 00:48:47.170
But in the case of somebody
who is in maybe bin A, which

00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:49.055
is I want to lose
some fat, maybe

00:48:49.055 --> 00:48:50.680
gain a little bit of
muscle, OK, what's

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:53.950
stopping you from the strength
and conditioning side?

00:48:53.950 --> 00:48:56.512
Is it the fact that you can't
train consistent enough?

00:48:56.512 --> 00:48:58.220
Is it the fact that
when you go to train,

00:48:58.220 --> 00:48:59.410
you don't know what to do?

00:48:59.410 --> 00:49:00.850
Is it the fact that
when you go to train,

00:49:00.850 --> 00:49:02.470
you train your ass off and
you're not getting results.

00:49:02.470 --> 00:49:03.160
OK, great.

00:49:03.160 --> 00:49:05.770
All three of these
different scenarios

00:49:05.770 --> 00:49:07.720
are going to result
in different programs

00:49:07.720 --> 00:49:10.810
because they have different
reasons you're failing.

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:12.460
And that is really critical.

00:49:12.460 --> 00:49:14.860
Instead of just going I
want a fat loss program

00:49:14.860 --> 00:49:17.050
and picking one up
off the internet,

00:49:17.050 --> 00:49:19.930
it may not actually
be addressing

00:49:19.930 --> 00:49:21.770
the point of failure for you.

00:49:21.770 --> 00:49:24.700
So the sooner you can
choose your programs

00:49:24.700 --> 00:49:28.600
based on why you're
failing, the sooner

00:49:28.600 --> 00:49:31.150
you'll start getting results.

00:49:31.150 --> 00:49:33.880
You have to run a little bit
of a critical analysis there.

00:49:33.880 --> 00:49:38.270
And it can be
something scientific.

00:49:38.270 --> 00:49:39.520
And it could be a measurement.

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:40.990
And it also could
just be thinking

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:44.660
about you've tried this in the
past and why didn't it work.

00:49:44.660 --> 00:49:46.420
It wasn't that interesting.

00:49:46.420 --> 00:49:50.540
OK, tell me more about
why it wasn't interesting.

00:49:50.540 --> 00:49:52.790
I'm not really into machines.

00:49:52.790 --> 00:49:53.690
And that's all I had.

00:49:53.690 --> 00:49:54.800
OK, great.

00:49:54.800 --> 00:49:56.280
Or I loved it.

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:57.380
I loved the gym I was at.

00:49:57.380 --> 00:49:58.463
And I was getting results.

00:49:58.463 --> 00:50:00.470
But it was so far away.

00:50:00.470 --> 00:50:02.060
OK, interesting.

00:50:02.060 --> 00:50:03.518
Why did you stop before?

00:50:03.518 --> 00:50:04.310
Why didn't it work?

00:50:04.310 --> 00:50:07.550
Or if it did work in
the past, amazing.

00:50:07.550 --> 00:50:09.080
Let's go back to
something similar.

00:50:09.080 --> 00:50:10.662
But has your life
changed at all?

00:50:10.662 --> 00:50:12.620
Is there anything different
about now than when

00:50:12.620 --> 00:50:15.090
it worked three years ago?

00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:15.702
Yes, no.

00:50:15.702 --> 00:50:17.160
If not, maybe we
run it right back.

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:19.260
If it is, OK, we're going
to predict those things.

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:20.940
And you want to work--

00:50:20.940 --> 00:50:23.850
effectively, what I'm saying is
throughout this entire 10-step

00:50:23.850 --> 00:50:27.210
process is going to be
you want to make sure

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:29.423
that there are the
non-negotiables that

00:50:29.423 --> 00:50:31.590
are in your life that you
know are going to be ahead

00:50:31.590 --> 00:50:33.240
of your fitness program.

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:36.180
And you want to work with
those things, not against them

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:37.140
because life will win.

00:50:37.140 --> 00:50:40.110
When it comes to your children,
when it comes to your job,

00:50:40.110 --> 00:50:41.760
life is going to win.

00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:43.590
You're going to have
to give up something.

00:50:43.590 --> 00:50:45.330
It's going to take
some hard work.

00:50:45.330 --> 00:50:48.750
But we want to fight the
right battles for most people.

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:50.588
Even for our
professional athletes,

00:50:50.588 --> 00:50:51.630
we get this all the time.

00:50:51.630 --> 00:50:53.880
It's just like they have
nothing else to do but train.

00:50:53.880 --> 00:50:56.260
Like, whoa, whoa, hold on
now, they're getting traded.

00:50:56.260 --> 00:50:57.510
They have agents to deal with.

00:50:57.510 --> 00:50:58.718
They may not have a contract.

00:50:58.718 --> 00:51:00.360
They have families,
blah, blah, blah.

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:02.400
Life will get in
the way, I promise.

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:06.030
And so you want to fight the
battles that you can win,

00:51:06.030 --> 00:51:07.935
not ones that you're
going to lose.

00:51:07.935 --> 00:51:09.810
And so that's really
what this game is about.

00:51:09.810 --> 00:51:14.268
So if the battle is, hey, my
job is super hectic, OK, great.

00:51:14.268 --> 00:51:16.560
We're going to come up with
a different strategy that's

00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:18.223
more flexible maybe.

00:51:18.223 --> 00:51:19.890
I'm still going to
hold you to the fire.

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:21.307
I'm not going to
be easier on you.

00:51:21.307 --> 00:51:24.120
But we're just not going to try
to set up a situation where you

00:51:24.120 --> 00:51:27.300
have to do this workout Monday,
Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:29.010
because you know your
job is on the road.

00:51:29.010 --> 00:51:33.870
And you're to provide all
income for your family, whatever

00:51:33.870 --> 00:51:35.785
the thing is.

00:51:35.785 --> 00:51:37.410
That's what we really
want to identify.

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:39.240
So when I say identify
your defenders,

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:42.450
you need to run a little bit
of a critical analysis on this.

00:51:42.450 --> 00:51:46.740
And a little bit of a
tool I'll use for this

00:51:46.740 --> 00:51:48.847
is a modification
of another system

00:51:48.847 --> 00:51:50.430
I stole from Kenny
Cain, which is what

00:51:50.430 --> 00:51:52.390
we call the quadrant system.

00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:54.810
So you can imagine
everything in your life

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:57.930
goes into one of four buckets.

00:51:57.930 --> 00:52:00.660
Now, bucket 1 I'm just
going to call business.

00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:03.150
And this is anything to
do with your job, income,

00:52:03.150 --> 00:52:04.770
sort of all those things.

00:52:04.770 --> 00:52:08.140
Bucket 2 is relationships.

00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:10.973
So, again, this could be
family or love life, anything

00:52:10.973 --> 00:52:12.390
that we would call
relationships--

00:52:12.390 --> 00:52:17.490
social connection,
purpose, anything, right?

00:52:17.490 --> 00:52:20.410
Bucket 3 is your fitness.

00:52:20.410 --> 00:52:23.130
And bucket 4 is your recovery.

00:52:23.130 --> 00:52:26.420
So one of the first steps we
take is we walk through this.

00:52:26.420 --> 00:52:30.480
And we say, all right,
you have 10 points total.

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:34.560
And you get to distribute
these 10 points across the four

00:52:34.560 --> 00:52:36.020
areas.

00:52:36.020 --> 00:52:40.122
So not 10 each,
you get 10 total.

00:52:40.122 --> 00:52:41.080
And so we walk through.

00:52:41.080 --> 00:52:43.822
And we say, right now, where
are you giving your points?

00:52:43.822 --> 00:52:46.030
And we could do this right
now for you if you'd like.

00:52:46.030 --> 00:52:48.760
Or I could make up a scenario.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:49.510
You want to do it?

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:50.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sure.

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:50.842
ANDY GALPIN: Great.

00:52:50.842 --> 00:52:54.960
So, Andrew, right now
in the last month,

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:58.170
if you had 10 points total
in those four categories,

00:52:58.170 --> 00:53:00.720
where would you be
distributing the most points?

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:01.500
Which category?

00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:03.610
And how many points
would that be?

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:05.423
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Business, my work.

00:53:05.423 --> 00:53:07.840
ANDY GALPIN: Business, work
job, sort of all those things.

00:53:07.840 --> 00:53:08.830
And how many out of 10?

00:53:08.830 --> 00:53:10.205
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Which doesn't, I

00:53:10.205 --> 00:53:12.460
should say, ever
quite feel like work.

00:53:12.460 --> 00:53:14.850
Running a laboratory
and doing the podcast

00:53:14.850 --> 00:53:17.350
doesn't ever really feel like
work in the traditional sense.

00:53:17.350 --> 00:53:19.000
But it's career.

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:20.410
It's work.

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:22.210
It involves relationships.

00:53:22.210 --> 00:53:26.230
But it certainly doesn't
enhance my fitness

00:53:26.230 --> 00:53:30.520
except of my vocal
chords and recovery.

00:53:30.520 --> 00:53:38.920
So with those notes
there, I would say 4 to 5.

00:53:38.920 --> 00:53:40.018
ANDY GALPIN: You pick.

00:53:40.018 --> 00:53:40.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: 5.

00:53:40.810 --> 00:53:42.190
ANDY GALPIN: 5, fair.

00:53:42.190 --> 00:53:44.892
That's the most common
number, business 5.

00:53:44.892 --> 00:53:45.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

00:53:45.850 --> 00:53:47.890
Once again, I'm typical,
which makes me happy.

00:53:47.890 --> 00:53:48.460
ANDY GALPIN: You nailed it.

00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:49.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
One of the few ways

00:53:49.420 --> 00:53:51.212
in which I've been
accused of being normal.

00:53:51.212 --> 00:53:52.740
I pick 5 for business.

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:53.740
ANDY GALPIN: No kidding.

00:53:53.740 --> 00:53:55.060
What's the next highest?

00:53:55.060 --> 00:53:57.793
And what's that score?

00:53:57.793 --> 00:53:59.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I do
invest in relationships.

00:53:59.710 --> 00:54:05.460
I would say does it
have to be around--

00:54:05.460 --> 00:54:06.420
can it be--

00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:07.560
ANDY GALPIN: It has
to be a whole integer.

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:08.935
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
A whole integer.

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:13.410
2.

00:54:13.410 --> 00:54:16.970
ANDY GALPIN: 2, all right,
we're 7 out of 10 here.

00:54:16.970 --> 00:54:19.040
So would you say
it is fair that you

00:54:19.040 --> 00:54:21.980
spend roughly 2 and 1/2 percent
of your-- it's not necessarily

00:54:21.980 --> 00:54:22.970
time.

00:54:22.970 --> 00:54:26.660
It's energy, time, focus, and
sort of all of these things--

00:54:26.660 --> 00:54:29.450
2 and 1/2 times as much
on your business as you

00:54:29.450 --> 00:54:31.370
do in your relationships?

00:54:31.370 --> 00:54:35.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It varies,
depending on what's going on.

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:38.210
It feels a little skewed in
the direction of business.

00:54:38.210 --> 00:54:40.892
So I might want to adjust
to a 4 to 3 ratio there.

00:54:40.892 --> 00:54:41.850
ANDY GALPIN: Maybe not.

00:54:41.850 --> 00:54:45.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But I think
I'm going to hold to 5, 2,

00:54:45.890 --> 00:54:47.720
business, relationships.

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:50.420
And then, just for
sake of example

00:54:50.420 --> 00:54:55.880
and because this doesn't seem
like an exceedingly precise

00:54:55.880 --> 00:54:57.830
measure, it can have some slop.

00:54:57.830 --> 00:54:59.390
ANDY GALPIN: Of course.

00:54:59.390 --> 00:55:02.760
Where would you put
fitness and recovery?

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:05.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I definitely
put energy into fitness.

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:08.070
So I'm going to
give that also a 2.

00:55:08.070 --> 00:55:11.857
ANDY GALPIN: Yep, which leaves?

00:55:11.857 --> 00:55:13.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: 1 for recovery.

00:55:13.190 --> 00:55:14.390
ANDY GALPIN: Great.

00:55:14.390 --> 00:55:16.700
That what you just
laid out is, again,

00:55:16.700 --> 00:55:18.980
the most quintessential
split you could have.

00:55:18.980 --> 00:55:20.605
In fact, you run this
game on everyone,

00:55:20.605 --> 00:55:22.897
they're going to come up with
basically the same answer

00:55:22.897 --> 00:55:25.010
unless they don't
work out or whatever.

00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:26.690
So a couple of rules here.

00:55:26.690 --> 00:55:31.790
Recovery must be at minimum
half of your fitness allocation.

00:55:31.790 --> 00:55:33.350
In your case, 2 to 1.

00:55:33.350 --> 00:55:34.040
You're fine.

00:55:34.040 --> 00:55:35.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think I'm
going to say it has to be half.

00:55:35.210 --> 00:55:36.683
It has to be 5 out of 10 points.

00:55:36.683 --> 00:55:37.350
ANDY GALPIN: No.

00:55:37.350 --> 00:55:39.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In which
case, it doesn't leave much

00:55:39.050 --> 00:55:39.800
for anything else.

00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:42.020
ANDY GALPIN: I would
like it to be minimum 20%

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:44.580
of the total, which
means 2 out of 10.

00:55:44.580 --> 00:55:48.450
Now, when I say recovery,
I don't simply mean muscle.

00:55:48.450 --> 00:55:50.210
I mean you need personal time.

00:55:50.210 --> 00:55:51.320
You need meditation.

00:55:51.320 --> 00:55:52.522
You need--

00:55:52.522 --> 00:55:53.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sleep.

00:55:53.480 --> 00:55:54.355
ANDY GALPIN: --sleep.

00:55:54.355 --> 00:55:56.910
You need to go to a concert
and get out and see people.

00:55:56.910 --> 00:56:00.110
And so like whatever the things
that give you energy back.

00:56:00.110 --> 00:56:01.580
Some folks, that's
personal time.

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:03.448
Some folks, that's
social time, whatever

00:56:03.448 --> 00:56:04.490
that means to you, right?

00:56:04.490 --> 00:56:06.698
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I
actually get a lot of energy

00:56:06.698 --> 00:56:07.530
from my work.

00:56:07.530 --> 00:56:10.400
And so that's why some of these
numbers are a little bit--

00:56:10.400 --> 00:56:14.178
you can kind of cloak
the underlying dynamics.

00:56:14.178 --> 00:56:16.220
ANDY GALPIN: So here's
what we do from this game.

00:56:16.220 --> 00:56:19.610
We look at that and we say, if
that's our split, Andrew, 5, 3,

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:20.490
2, 1--

00:56:20.490 --> 00:56:22.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: 5, 2, 2, 1.

00:56:22.060 --> 00:56:23.060
ANDY GALPIN: 5, 2, 2, 1.

00:56:23.060 --> 00:56:24.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd
love to be able to put 3

00:56:24.140 --> 00:56:26.120
in relationships just because.

00:56:26.120 --> 00:56:27.428
But then they need 11 out of--

00:56:27.428 --> 00:56:28.220
ANDY GALPIN: Right.

00:56:28.220 --> 00:56:30.140
So here's the fun game we play.

00:56:30.140 --> 00:56:31.248
You're currently at this.

00:56:31.248 --> 00:56:32.540
And you don't get to add to 11.

00:56:32.540 --> 00:56:33.770
You have to stay at 10.

00:56:33.770 --> 00:56:35.900
Your 10 is different
than my 10 maybe, right?

00:56:35.900 --> 00:56:38.120
But 10 is 10, or just--
whatever the maximum

00:56:38.120 --> 00:56:39.440
you can actually do.

00:56:39.440 --> 00:56:40.880
It's you.

00:56:40.880 --> 00:56:44.810
So if we went back to our
training goal, whatever

00:56:44.810 --> 00:56:47.750
that goal was for you, and we
went back to our defenders,

00:56:47.750 --> 00:56:53.180
we would look at this score
now and say, is 3 out of 10--

00:56:53.180 --> 00:56:54.230
fitness is 3, right?

00:56:54.230 --> 00:56:55.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fitness is 2.

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:57.470
So it's 5, 2, 2, 1.

00:56:57.470 --> 00:57:01.070
ANDY GALPIN: Is 2 out of 10
sufficient to hit that training

00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:03.530
goal in that time
frame you described?

00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:08.600
And let's say you said, I want
to hit a new PR in my mile

00:57:08.600 --> 00:57:09.510
six months from now.

00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:10.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
for simplicity's sake

00:57:10.880 --> 00:57:12.338
and also because
it's largely true,

00:57:12.338 --> 00:57:17.570
I'm going to put myself in what
I refer to as bin A earlier.

00:57:17.570 --> 00:57:20.030
My body fat percentage is OK.

00:57:20.030 --> 00:57:22.550
It's in the range
that I would like.

00:57:22.550 --> 00:57:24.528
But I would like to bring
it down a little bit,

00:57:24.528 --> 00:57:26.570
probably gain a little
bit muscle here and there,

00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:28.490
keep or gain some endurance.

00:57:28.490 --> 00:57:32.450
And, certainly, certainly, my
immediate and long term health

00:57:32.450 --> 00:57:33.710
are extremely important to me.

00:57:33.710 --> 00:57:34.250
ANDY GALPIN: Great.

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:35.750
So then the question,
and the answer

00:57:35.750 --> 00:57:39.110
maybe yes, that this is
the optimal split for you.

00:57:39.110 --> 00:57:42.180
If it is not, then we
have to make a choice.

00:57:42.180 --> 00:57:46.850
We either alter the goal or the
timeline to make it realistic

00:57:46.850 --> 00:57:49.220
or we alter our quadrant.

00:57:49.220 --> 00:57:51.380
And then if we're going
to alter our quadrant,

00:57:51.380 --> 00:57:53.130
the next step is
critically important.

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:57.050
We need a list of very
specific life actions

00:57:57.050 --> 00:58:01.320
that we're going to take that
allows that split to happen.

00:58:01.320 --> 00:58:04.790
So if you said, for
example, I want to put three

00:58:04.790 --> 00:58:07.010
into relationships, great.

00:58:07.010 --> 00:58:09.230
What specific life
actions are you

00:58:09.230 --> 00:58:12.650
going to take to pull
one from fitness--

00:58:12.650 --> 00:58:14.940
you can't pull any from
recovery or one from business.

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:16.523
And you don't have
to actually answer.

00:58:16.523 --> 00:58:17.180
This is--

00:58:17.180 --> 00:58:18.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Too personal.

00:58:18.920 --> 00:58:20.270
ANDY GALPIN: I know you don't
like making these things

00:58:20.270 --> 00:58:21.110
about you because--

00:58:21.110 --> 00:58:23.485
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right, that's
the other reason to do it.

00:58:23.485 --> 00:58:26.457
And it is a diabolical
trick to insist

00:58:26.457 --> 00:58:28.040
that these be whole
integers because I

00:58:28.040 --> 00:58:31.940
would have done like a
4.5 for business and a 2.5

00:58:31.940 --> 00:58:32.690
for relationships.

00:58:32.690 --> 00:58:35.363
But, obviously, you write
the rules on this, not me.

00:58:35.363 --> 00:58:37.280
ANDY GALPIN: So you would
just walk that list.

00:58:37.280 --> 00:58:41.480
And the list could be
something like I promise to not

00:58:41.480 --> 00:58:46.750
work after 7:00 PM
Thursday through Sunday

00:58:46.750 --> 00:58:48.130
or whatever the thing is.

00:58:48.130 --> 00:58:51.670
I promise I'm going to make
sure that I don't start work

00:58:51.670 --> 00:58:54.580
before 8:00 AM or blah,
blah, blah, whatever.

00:58:54.580 --> 00:58:56.200
No more trips.

00:58:56.200 --> 00:58:59.050
Just make those things
specific and measurable,

00:58:59.050 --> 00:59:00.815
not just like I'm
going to work less.

00:59:00.815 --> 00:59:03.190
That's never going to go--
what is the very specific life

00:59:03.190 --> 00:59:04.357
action you're going to take?

00:59:04.357 --> 00:59:06.520
There's going to be alarm
that goes off Tuesday

00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:09.100
night at 4:00, 5:00 PM.

00:59:09.100 --> 00:59:10.877
And no matter what we--

00:59:10.877 --> 00:59:13.210
at Barbell Shrugged, we used
to have a little shirt that

00:59:13.210 --> 00:59:15.790
was like D3AT, which
is like drop everything

00:59:15.790 --> 00:59:18.040
and train, which means at
3:00 PM in the afternoon,

00:59:18.040 --> 00:59:20.082
no matter what's happening,
we dropped everything

00:59:20.082 --> 00:59:22.720
and trained because that was
like when you start a business

00:59:22.720 --> 00:59:25.570
and you're going, things
just run away from you.

00:59:25.570 --> 00:59:28.220
And it is just sort of like,
man, it's not my company.

00:59:28.220 --> 00:59:29.650
But those guys are like, we
are a strength and conditioning

00:59:29.650 --> 00:59:30.370
company.

00:59:30.370 --> 00:59:31.817
And we're not training.

00:59:31.817 --> 00:59:33.400
So we had to just
make this hard rule.

00:59:33.400 --> 00:59:34.810
And it was just like a
little thing that came up.

00:59:34.810 --> 00:59:37.120
And it was easy to say drop
everything and train 3:00.

00:59:37.120 --> 00:59:37.750
There you go.

00:59:37.750 --> 00:59:42.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I like
this drop D, E everything, A

00:59:42.840 --> 00:59:45.125
and, blank, like it could
be drop everything and--

00:59:45.125 --> 00:59:46.000
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:47.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--pick your favorite.

00:59:47.350 --> 00:59:47.740
ANDY GALPIN: Totally.

00:59:47.740 --> 00:59:48.610
Drop everything and read.

00:59:48.610 --> 00:59:50.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Pick the
relevant-- read, yeah,

00:59:50.740 --> 00:59:51.430
absolutely.

00:59:51.430 --> 00:59:53.200
I really miss
reading for pleasure.

00:59:53.200 --> 00:59:55.330
I would put that
under recovery and--

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:57.100
ANDY GALPIN: Drop
everything and breathe.

00:59:57.100 --> 00:59:58.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, and
breathe you are saying.

00:59:58.450 --> 00:59:59.117
Oh, and breathe.

00:59:59.117 --> 00:59:59.890
I was saying read.

00:59:59.890 --> 01:00:00.550
ANDY GALPIN: I said that too.

01:00:00.550 --> 01:00:02.967
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, because
for me, reading is actually

01:00:02.967 --> 01:00:05.350
is both recovery
and relationship.

01:00:05.350 --> 01:00:08.200
Because oftentimes
in my relationships,

01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:10.510
I've insisted-- not insisted.

01:00:10.510 --> 01:00:12.550
I certainly didn't insist.

01:00:12.550 --> 01:00:15.012
We've had a format of reading
the same book in parallel.

01:00:15.012 --> 01:00:15.970
ANDY GALPIN: Oh, great.

01:00:15.970 --> 01:00:17.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, not
necessarily side by side

01:00:17.460 --> 01:00:19.370
but the same book in parallel
and then discussing it.

01:00:19.370 --> 01:00:21.250
It's a wonderful
practice or listening

01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:22.210
to the same audio book.

01:00:22.210 --> 01:00:23.590
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah,
it works well.

01:00:23.590 --> 01:00:25.318
You can do drop
everything and play.

01:00:25.318 --> 01:00:26.860
You're just going
to go do something.

01:00:26.860 --> 01:00:27.760
You're going to
play video games.

01:00:27.760 --> 01:00:28.910
You're going to go
play with your kid.

01:00:28.910 --> 01:00:31.420
You're going to-- do you
want to play with your dog?

01:00:31.420 --> 01:00:33.128
It doesn't even have
to actually be play.

01:00:33.128 --> 01:00:35.200
But play to you could
signify personal time.

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:37.600
It doesn't really matter.

01:00:37.600 --> 01:00:38.475
So, yeah, that's it.

01:00:38.475 --> 01:00:40.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I really
like this drop everything

01:00:40.600 --> 01:00:42.940
and blank category
that you probably

01:00:42.940 --> 01:00:44.560
shouldn't have more than what?

01:00:44.560 --> 01:00:46.450
Two or three of those overall?

01:00:46.450 --> 01:00:49.630
ANDY GALPIN: Pretty
much one to two maybe

01:00:49.630 --> 01:00:51.170
is where you want
to go after that.

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:51.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So the idea is then

01:00:51.850 --> 01:00:53.980
to redistribute the
numbers on this list

01:00:53.980 --> 01:00:55.780
but through a very
concrete action.

01:00:55.780 --> 01:00:57.970
And I like this drop
everything and blank

01:00:57.970 --> 01:01:02.080
because it speaks to the
non-negotiable aspect of it.

01:01:02.080 --> 01:01:03.250
ANDY GALPIN: Has to be.

01:01:03.250 --> 01:01:04.390
Life will get pushed.

01:01:04.390 --> 01:01:07.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
not a fine time to--

01:01:07.150 --> 01:01:08.825
it's not in next
year I'm going to--

01:01:08.825 --> 01:01:09.700
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

01:01:09.700 --> 01:01:10.825
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

01:01:10.825 --> 01:01:12.260
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, yeah.

01:01:12.260 --> 01:01:13.930
When you put those things--

01:01:13.930 --> 01:01:16.307
those things, you might
as well just don't even

01:01:16.307 --> 01:01:17.390
put it on your list right.

01:01:17.390 --> 01:01:17.980
It's not going to happen.

01:01:17.980 --> 01:01:19.522
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, you're talking

01:01:19.522 --> 01:01:21.220
to somebody who loves rules.

01:01:21.220 --> 01:01:23.710
Because when they are
non-negotiable rules,

01:01:23.710 --> 01:01:26.890
they provide this incredible
organizing force for the brain.

01:01:26.890 --> 01:01:29.225
It's really a neuroscience
thing in my mind.

01:01:29.225 --> 01:01:30.100
ANDY GALPIN: Totally.

01:01:30.100 --> 01:01:31.870
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And, actually,
we did an episode on happiness

01:01:31.870 --> 01:01:34.570
where you find that once
people make a decision, if they

01:01:34.570 --> 01:01:36.697
eliminate the possibility
of other decisions, like,

01:01:36.697 --> 01:01:38.530
literally, the hatch
is closed, that is it--

01:01:38.530 --> 01:01:39.697
ANDY GALPIN: Burn the boats.

01:01:39.697 --> 01:01:43.743
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --the rates
of subjective happiness,

01:01:43.743 --> 01:01:45.160
immediate and long
term happiness,

01:01:45.160 --> 01:01:47.167
over time go way, way up.

01:01:47.167 --> 01:01:49.000
And so I'm convinced
that the nervous system

01:01:49.000 --> 01:01:51.800
doesn't like to keep the valves
on these dopamine circuits

01:01:51.800 --> 01:01:52.300
open.

01:01:52.300 --> 01:01:54.550
I actually think it diminishes
from the reward component.

01:01:54.550 --> 01:01:56.340
And there are actually
some data on this.

01:01:56.340 --> 01:01:58.090
Anyway, I don't want
to take us off track.

01:01:58.090 --> 01:01:59.140
ANDY GALPIN: The
last part of this,

01:01:59.140 --> 01:02:00.940
what we do then is we
take that quadrant.

01:02:00.940 --> 01:02:01.870
And we take that list.

01:02:01.870 --> 01:02:04.690
And then you're going
to print it physically.

01:02:04.690 --> 01:02:06.808
And you're going to
put it in two places.

01:02:06.808 --> 01:02:08.350
This could actually
be on your phone.

01:02:08.350 --> 01:02:08.830
You don't have to print it.

01:02:08.830 --> 01:02:10.480
You can screenshot and be
the background of your phone.

01:02:10.480 --> 01:02:12.063
So every time you
click on your phone,

01:02:12.063 --> 01:02:14.830
you immediately
see that quadrant.

01:02:14.830 --> 01:02:16.570
It's a very clear
reminder of like,

01:02:16.570 --> 01:02:18.130
what are my priorities today?

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:20.050
Just a simple little picture.

01:02:20.050 --> 01:02:23.840
I also like to put it in
your place of failure.

01:02:23.840 --> 01:02:27.030
So for a lot of people, that is
like on their laptop or right

01:02:27.030 --> 01:02:28.030
above their workstation.

01:02:28.030 --> 01:02:29.950
It's the thing that's
going to lose and beat

01:02:29.950 --> 01:02:31.900
your fitness is your job--

01:02:31.900 --> 01:02:32.950
typically.

01:02:32.950 --> 01:02:36.968
Or it's on your TV, it's
on your Netflix control--

01:02:36.968 --> 01:02:38.260
no, sorry, Netflix, no offense.

01:02:38.260 --> 01:02:38.980
But you know what I mean?

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:40.993
It's whatever the thing
is that you fail for.

01:02:40.993 --> 01:02:42.160
I play too many video games.

01:02:42.160 --> 01:02:42.550
Great.

01:02:42.550 --> 01:02:43.217
I work too much.

01:02:43.217 --> 01:02:43.750
OK, great.

01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:44.625
And you put it there.

01:02:44.625 --> 01:02:45.820
And you put it also--

01:02:45.820 --> 01:02:47.770
the last component
is-- it has to also

01:02:47.770 --> 01:02:52.850
be in the hands of somebody
who can hold you accountable.

01:02:52.850 --> 01:02:56.330
Wife, training partner,
business partner, whatever.

01:02:56.330 --> 01:02:58.460
So it's like, hey,
Andrew, you promised you

01:02:58.460 --> 01:03:00.080
were going to do x yourself.

01:03:00.080 --> 01:03:01.490
Why are you still here?

01:03:01.490 --> 01:03:02.660
You committed to this.

01:03:02.660 --> 01:03:03.910
You've got to get out of here.

01:03:03.910 --> 01:03:05.420
Someone who will
be like, no, no.

01:03:05.420 --> 01:03:07.040
It's drop everything and read.

01:03:07.040 --> 01:03:07.590
It's 8:00.

01:03:07.590 --> 01:03:08.840
You're supposed to be reading.

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:10.347
You got to go.

01:03:10.347 --> 01:03:11.930
You're going to check
back in on that.

01:03:11.930 --> 01:03:13.212
Check back again every week.

01:03:13.212 --> 01:03:15.170
Check back in-- it doesn't
matter, every month.

01:03:15.170 --> 01:03:16.310
And then, you can adjust.

01:03:16.310 --> 01:03:16.830
That's fine.

01:03:16.830 --> 01:03:17.900
You can always
change the system.

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:19.170
But that has to now change.

01:03:19.170 --> 01:03:20.420
You've got to print a new one.

01:03:20.420 --> 01:03:23.660
And now it's a whole new
promise you've made to yourself.

01:03:23.660 --> 01:03:25.370
So you've got to be
able to hold yourself

01:03:25.370 --> 01:03:26.537
accountable to those things.

01:03:26.537 --> 01:03:30.230
It's got to be flexible enough
to where it's realistic.

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:34.470
It can't be, I'm not going
to work after 6:00 every day.

01:03:34.470 --> 01:03:36.470
Maybe it's just
three days a week.

01:03:36.470 --> 01:03:38.690
Maybe it's, on
Saturdays I promise

01:03:38.690 --> 01:03:41.668
to work for the first two hours
so I don't work Saturday night.

01:03:41.668 --> 01:03:44.210
Or whatever, or the inverse,
I'm not going to work Saturday--

01:03:44.210 --> 01:03:44.510
you get it.

01:03:44.510 --> 01:03:46.468
You could come up with
a million examples here.

01:03:46.468 --> 01:03:48.200
So that's the system
we use to make sure

01:03:48.200 --> 01:03:50.970
that we have now properly
identified where we're going.

01:03:50.970 --> 01:03:52.710
We found a roadmap to that.

01:03:52.710 --> 01:03:57.200
And now we know exactly how
we're going to stay on track.

01:03:57.200 --> 01:03:59.960
I have to take this opportunity
to add one more thing

01:03:59.960 --> 01:04:01.610
to our drop everything list.

01:04:01.610 --> 01:04:04.280
And you gave the
example of DEAR,

01:04:04.280 --> 01:04:06.200
which is maybe drop
everything and read,

01:04:06.200 --> 01:04:07.760
or drop everything and relax.

01:04:07.760 --> 01:04:12.290
Or another example,
I have to add a DEAL,

01:04:12.290 --> 01:04:15.470
which is drop everything
and-- this is for you, Lex--

01:04:15.470 --> 01:04:15.970
love.

01:04:15.970 --> 01:04:17.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Actually,
one of the advantages

01:04:17.970 --> 01:04:19.640
of having a dog
or having children

01:04:19.640 --> 01:04:25.100
is that the drop everything
and love is often

01:04:25.100 --> 01:04:29.457
enforced by the faces
of those that you love.

01:04:29.457 --> 01:04:31.790
They just show up in whatever
space you happen to be in.

01:04:31.790 --> 01:04:33.310
ANDY GALPIN: Especially
if you work from home.

01:04:33.310 --> 01:04:34.685
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I'd like to take

01:04:34.685 --> 01:04:37.270
a brief break to acknowledge
our sponsor, Inside Tracker.

