01:05:34.330 --> 01:05:36.112
to get 20% off.

01:05:36.112 --> 01:05:38.320
ANDY GALPIN: Let's move on
to the next one, shall we?

01:05:38.320 --> 01:05:41.700
Number three here is going to
be what I call calendar or time

01:05:41.700 --> 01:05:42.200
frame.

01:05:42.200 --> 01:05:44.350
So it's going back a
little bit and saying,

01:05:44.350 --> 01:05:47.350
you've decided on
this goal and you've

01:05:47.350 --> 01:05:48.730
identified the defenders.

01:05:48.730 --> 01:05:50.860
Now we need to come up
with a realistic time

01:05:50.860 --> 01:05:54.050
frame for how long it's going
to take to accomplish that goal.

01:05:54.050 --> 01:05:58.630
And when you do that, you need
to look at your life schedule.

01:05:58.630 --> 01:06:01.300
And what I mean by
that is do you have

01:06:01.300 --> 01:06:02.920
important deadlines coming up?

01:06:02.920 --> 01:06:04.180
Do you have a holiday?

01:06:04.180 --> 01:06:05.750
Do you have a trip?

01:06:05.750 --> 01:06:06.970
Do you have travel?

01:06:06.970 --> 01:06:10.430
Do your children have
something coming up?

01:06:10.430 --> 01:06:12.080
You need to take all
that information.

01:06:12.080 --> 01:06:14.350
And I literally
lay out a calendar.

01:06:14.350 --> 01:06:18.080
And I write all those dates
in a physical calendar first.

01:06:18.080 --> 01:06:19.870
And the reason I'm
suggesting this is you

01:06:19.870 --> 01:06:22.930
want to work your training
backwards around that.

01:06:22.930 --> 01:06:25.690
As we've been discussing,
life will win.

01:06:25.690 --> 01:06:28.960
If you try to plan a
training program that

01:06:28.960 --> 01:06:32.208
is five days a week 90 minutes
a day, and all of a sudden,

01:06:32.208 --> 01:06:34.000
you look two weeks from
now and you realize

01:06:34.000 --> 01:06:35.410
you've got a grant deadline.

01:06:35.410 --> 01:06:38.270
And then you've got to take
two days to go to Austin.

01:06:38.270 --> 01:06:39.160
It's just foolish.

01:06:39.160 --> 01:06:40.130
You're going to fail.

01:06:40.130 --> 01:06:40.840
And then you're going to quit.

01:06:40.840 --> 01:06:42.507
And you're going to
be like, man, again,

01:06:42.507 --> 01:06:43.780
my training program failed.

01:06:43.780 --> 01:06:45.490
So you need to
figure out what are

01:06:45.490 --> 01:06:48.790
the non-negotiables are
in that business quadrant,

01:06:48.790 --> 01:06:50.980
and just not be foolish.

01:06:50.980 --> 01:06:54.490
So let's imagine you're
going to plot out, say,

01:06:54.490 --> 01:06:56.470
a 12-week training phase.

01:06:56.470 --> 01:06:59.170
And you want to-- you've
decided on this goal.

01:06:59.170 --> 01:07:01.060
And then you look
and you realize

01:07:01.060 --> 01:07:03.490
in the middle of
this 12-week span,

01:07:03.490 --> 01:07:06.940
week five is really
hectic and chaotic.

01:07:06.940 --> 01:07:10.570
Or you realize that this is
a quarter in which something

01:07:10.570 --> 01:07:11.710
important is due.

01:07:11.710 --> 01:07:13.990
Maybe we want to
either adjust the goal.

01:07:13.990 --> 01:07:17.830
Or what we really do in
this step is going on

01:07:17.830 --> 01:07:20.380
to actually step
number four, which

01:07:20.380 --> 01:07:25.630
is choose the number of days
per week you can exercise

01:07:25.630 --> 01:07:28.960
and the length in
terms of amount of time

01:07:28.960 --> 01:07:31.420
you can truly afford to train.

01:07:31.420 --> 01:07:35.450
I would rather you underestimate
that than overestimate it.

01:07:35.450 --> 01:07:37.750
So you, again, you
look at the calendar.

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:39.400
You put all these
non-negotiables,

01:07:39.400 --> 01:07:42.080
the deadlines you cannot
move in the calendar.

01:07:42.080 --> 01:07:45.400
And then you say, look,
based on this, realistically,

01:07:45.400 --> 01:07:48.040
I can conservatively
train three days a week

01:07:48.040 --> 01:07:49.300
for 60 minutes total.

01:07:49.300 --> 01:07:50.830
And that includes
the time I walk

01:07:50.830 --> 01:07:54.160
into the gym, my warm-up,
my down-regulation breathing

01:07:54.160 --> 01:07:56.450
at the end, and
then me getting back

01:07:56.450 --> 01:07:57.700
either in the shower and back.

01:07:57.700 --> 01:07:59.410
Because really, now
it's maybe 90 minutes

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:01.270
by the time you traveled,
you transitioned,

01:08:01.270 --> 01:08:04.473
you picked back up on work, you
showered, you ate, et cetera.

01:08:04.473 --> 01:08:05.890
That time just
runs away from you.

01:08:05.890 --> 01:08:08.415
And all of sudden it was
a two and 1/2 hour thing

01:08:08.415 --> 01:08:10.040
even though it was
a 45-minute workout.

01:08:10.040 --> 01:08:11.530
So you really need to
figure that thing out.

01:08:11.530 --> 01:08:13.290
If you're a few weeks
in and you realize, ooh,

01:08:13.290 --> 01:08:15.340
I actually have a little
more time than I thought,

01:08:15.340 --> 01:08:16.180
you can always train more.

01:08:16.180 --> 01:08:16.779
You can do another thing.

01:08:16.779 --> 01:08:17.260
You can add up.

01:08:17.260 --> 01:08:18.885
But what you don't
want to do is set up

01:08:18.885 --> 01:08:23.920
a program that is requiring you
to do certain exercises on one

01:08:23.920 --> 01:08:27.279
days or certain styles
of training on another,

01:08:27.279 --> 01:08:30.287
and then you constantly
miss one of those days.

01:08:30.287 --> 01:08:31.870
I thought I could
do four days a week,

01:08:31.870 --> 01:08:34.218
but one day a week something's
getting pulled out.

01:08:34.218 --> 01:08:36.010
That's just going to
keep you off schedule.

01:08:36.010 --> 01:08:37.810
It's going to make you
feel like a failure.

01:08:37.810 --> 01:08:40.185
And you're going to run into
problems with your training.

01:08:40.185 --> 01:08:43.120
So schedule three if you're
sure you can get three.

01:08:43.120 --> 01:08:46.160
And if there's an extra day, we
can always do other fun stuff.

01:08:46.160 --> 01:08:48.279
So that's really
step three and four.

01:08:48.279 --> 01:08:51.550
Figure out your life events
over the course of this time.

01:08:51.550 --> 01:08:53.620
How many days a
week can you train?

01:08:53.620 --> 01:08:56.920
And then how long in terms
of minutes per workout.

01:08:56.920 --> 01:09:00.760
Notice, we haven't selected
a single exercise yet.

01:09:00.760 --> 01:09:02.710
We haven't worried
about how heavy,

01:09:02.710 --> 01:09:04.930
rest intervals, all those
modifiable variables.

01:09:04.930 --> 01:09:06.910
You don't need to
pick those later.

01:09:06.910 --> 01:09:08.920
First, where are we going.

01:09:08.920 --> 01:09:12.310
Second, how are we going to get
there, which is the quadrant

01:09:12.310 --> 01:09:14.080
and identifying of defenders.

01:09:14.080 --> 01:09:16.779
And then the third is,
what are the restrictions

01:09:16.779 --> 01:09:20.340
I need to place on myself
in terms of program design

01:09:20.340 --> 01:09:23.910
based on how often and
how long I can work out.

01:09:23.910 --> 01:09:25.950
That is going to
allow you to go back

01:09:25.950 --> 01:09:28.470
to some of the previous
episodes and go, man,

01:09:28.470 --> 01:09:30.330
you gave us all kinds of ideas.

01:09:30.330 --> 01:09:32.020
How do I know which
one to choose?

01:09:32.020 --> 01:09:33.220
This is your answer.

01:09:33.220 --> 01:09:36.390
You're going to choose based
upon the limitations of time

01:09:36.390 --> 01:09:37.450
and frequency.

01:09:37.450 --> 01:09:40.470
So if you've already said,
we're in-- let's imagine

01:09:40.470 --> 01:09:43.710
we're in bucket A, or
bucket C. It doesn't matter.

01:09:43.710 --> 01:09:45.569
And you go, look,
the most I can afford

01:09:45.569 --> 01:09:48.540
with where I'm at with
what's going on in my life

01:09:48.540 --> 01:09:50.104
is three days a week.

01:09:50.104 --> 01:09:51.479
Well, we automatically
know we're

01:09:51.479 --> 01:09:53.910
going to have to start
training-- choosing a training

01:09:53.910 --> 01:09:56.297
style that's limited
to three days a week.

01:09:56.297 --> 01:09:58.380
Don't even worry about the
four or five day stuff.

01:09:58.380 --> 01:09:59.910
Those are off the table.

01:09:59.910 --> 01:10:02.040
And now we're on-- so
we've placed restrictions.

01:10:02.040 --> 01:10:05.010
It goes back to
that concept of I

01:10:05.010 --> 01:10:08.400
think it's one of your
podcast's guests, Jocko.

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:11.040
It's just like, hey, structure
gives us actually some freedom.

01:10:11.040 --> 01:10:13.282
So by creating some
restrictions here,

01:10:13.282 --> 01:10:15.240
we're a little more free
to go, I only actually

01:10:15.240 --> 01:10:16.740
have to choose between A and B.

01:10:16.740 --> 01:10:19.215
Rather than sitting down and
going, man, there's all--

01:10:19.215 --> 01:10:22.030
I'm 20 hours into this
exercise podcast thing.

01:10:22.030 --> 01:10:23.310
And there's so many options.

01:10:23.310 --> 01:10:23.920
Which one to pick?

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:25.795
Well, you create a little
bit of restriction.

01:10:25.795 --> 01:10:28.770
And now it's easier to go,
oh, my only option is A or B.

01:10:28.770 --> 01:10:30.125
And there we go.

01:10:30.125 --> 01:10:31.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
that's number four.

01:10:31.750 --> 01:10:33.208
ANDY GALPIN: That's
three and four.

01:10:33.208 --> 01:10:35.300
At that point, once
we're good there,

01:10:35.300 --> 01:10:37.720
now what you want to do us
go to step number five, which

01:10:37.720 --> 01:10:41.380
is actually select your
exercises or your movements.

01:10:41.380 --> 01:10:45.490
And this can be as simple as
selecting a kettlebell swing,

01:10:45.490 --> 01:10:48.010
or running, or swimming.

01:10:48.010 --> 01:10:50.630
It could be your
entire exercise mode.

01:10:50.630 --> 01:10:52.450
What you want to do
with exercise selection

01:10:52.450 --> 01:10:56.440
here is make sure that you're
balancing those exercises

01:10:56.440 --> 01:10:59.260
across the whole week.

01:10:59.260 --> 01:11:02.210
Not within necessarily
every workout.

01:11:02.210 --> 01:11:04.630
So if you have four days
a week, five days a week,

01:11:04.630 --> 01:11:06.430
you want to look at
the exercise selection

01:11:06.430 --> 01:11:08.650
and say, OK, I need
to have somewhat

01:11:08.650 --> 01:11:13.870
of a reasonable balance between
movement patterns, or muscle

01:11:13.870 --> 01:11:16.210
groups, or front and
back, side to side,

01:11:16.210 --> 01:11:19.690
however you're thinking of
it, just across that week.

01:11:19.690 --> 01:11:23.410
So again, say we're on
a three-day program.

01:11:23.410 --> 01:11:25.720
And we're in either
of the buckets--

01:11:25.720 --> 01:11:27.910
any of the buckets, really.

01:11:27.910 --> 01:11:29.380
And we say, OK, great.

01:11:29.380 --> 01:11:34.533
Maybe it's not ideal if all I
select is cycling every day.

01:11:34.533 --> 01:11:35.700
That's not a lot of balance.

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:36.360
I don't notice anything.

01:11:36.360 --> 01:11:37.735
There's no upper
body work there.

01:11:37.735 --> 01:11:38.940
There's no torso work.

01:11:38.940 --> 01:11:41.140
There's no other positions.

01:11:41.140 --> 01:11:43.440
So maybe I'm going to
really focus on cycling.

01:11:43.440 --> 01:11:45.300
So I will do only
cycling two days a week.

01:11:45.300 --> 01:11:47.100
But that third day I
need to pick something

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:48.365
for the other movement areas.

01:11:48.365 --> 01:11:49.740
And that's going
to make sure you

01:11:49.740 --> 01:11:51.690
stay in a reasonable balance.

01:11:51.690 --> 01:11:54.240
If you have an exercise
that you like, great.

01:11:54.240 --> 01:11:56.580
If you have exercise
you have access to.

01:11:56.580 --> 01:11:58.980
Again, maybe the gym is
a giant pain in the ass.

01:11:58.980 --> 01:12:01.237
And so you can say, look.

01:12:01.237 --> 01:12:02.070
That's too far away.

01:12:02.070 --> 01:12:03.945
The closest one is 45
minutes there and back.

01:12:03.945 --> 01:12:05.520
So maybe I'm going
to restrict myself

01:12:05.520 --> 01:12:08.280
to only kettlebell, and
bands, and running, because I

01:12:08.280 --> 01:12:10.140
can do those in my house.

01:12:10.140 --> 01:12:10.722
Awesome.

01:12:10.722 --> 01:12:12.180
We've actually
created some freedom

01:12:12.180 --> 01:12:13.960
because we gave ourselves
some restriction.

01:12:13.960 --> 01:12:15.377
And now we just
have to figure out

01:12:15.377 --> 01:12:17.910
how am I going to give some
movement patterns somewhat

01:12:17.910 --> 01:12:20.200
balanced across my three days.

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:24.000
So really, when it comes
to exercise choice,

01:12:24.000 --> 01:12:28.200
it is selecting the patterns
that you know how to execute.

01:12:28.200 --> 01:12:30.750
Giving yourself, again,
somewhat of a balance

01:12:30.750 --> 01:12:34.050
between the muscles, and
the joints, and the movement

01:12:34.050 --> 01:12:35.730
patterns.

01:12:35.730 --> 01:12:37.620
Making sure that
you are specifically

01:12:37.620 --> 01:12:41.580
targeting any muscle group
or movement that you want.

01:12:41.580 --> 01:12:44.640
So making sure you
want to improve muscle

01:12:44.640 --> 01:12:46.380
size in your glutes,
you better make

01:12:46.380 --> 01:12:49.560
sure some of the exercises
you're doing at least one day

01:12:49.560 --> 01:12:52.470
a week you're feeling
in your actual glutes.

01:12:52.470 --> 01:12:53.750
So you can check that box.

01:12:53.750 --> 01:12:55.292
It doesn't have to
be every exercise.

01:12:55.292 --> 01:12:56.370
It probably shouldn't.

01:12:56.370 --> 01:12:59.110
It doesn't even have
to be every single day.

01:12:59.110 --> 01:13:03.020
But make sure it's checked
off somewhere on that list.

01:13:03.020 --> 01:13:05.870
And the last one is,
is there a strategy

01:13:05.870 --> 01:13:07.625
in which you can progress it?

01:13:07.625 --> 01:13:09.000
So if you're like,
I'm just going

01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:10.110
to do bodyweight exercises.

01:13:10.110 --> 01:13:10.610
OK, great.

01:13:10.610 --> 01:13:13.350
Well, how are you going
to progress those?

01:13:13.350 --> 01:13:16.050
In the case of bodyweight,
it's really hard to add load.

01:13:16.050 --> 01:13:17.670
Maybe you can put a weight
vest on or something.

01:13:17.670 --> 01:13:19.320
But then, maybe you don't have
that, or that's an extra thing,

01:13:19.320 --> 01:13:20.410
or that can only go so far.

01:13:20.410 --> 01:13:22.327
So what's my progression
strategy going to be?

01:13:22.327 --> 01:13:25.560
Well, in this case, maybe you
just increase the complexity

01:13:25.560 --> 01:13:29.160
by going from two legs,
like say a bodyweight squat,

01:13:29.160 --> 01:13:33.090
to a single leg squat, or you
just increase repetitions,

01:13:33.090 --> 01:13:35.197
or you increase time
you're going to hold it.

01:13:35.197 --> 01:13:36.780
My point is, your
progression strategy

01:13:36.780 --> 01:13:38.238
will be based upon
the restrictions

01:13:38.238 --> 01:13:40.470
that you place based
on availability

01:13:40.470 --> 01:13:42.100
and things like that.

01:13:42.100 --> 01:13:44.880
The last thing I
always recommend here

01:13:44.880 --> 01:13:46.770
in terms of exercise
progression,

01:13:46.770 --> 01:13:48.600
to make sure that
you can continue

01:13:48.600 --> 01:13:52.050
to do these things while
lowering your risk of injury,

01:13:52.050 --> 01:13:54.660
both in the short-term
and long-term,

01:13:54.660 --> 01:13:59.350
is to progress your exercise
complexity in this fashion.

01:13:59.350 --> 01:14:03.600
So make sure, number one, you
can do the exercise properly

01:14:03.600 --> 01:14:05.430
with assistance.

01:14:05.430 --> 01:14:06.990
So let's imagine
a scenario where

01:14:06.990 --> 01:14:09.390
we're going to try to squat.

01:14:09.390 --> 01:14:13.030
So give yourself-- put your
hands on a bench or something

01:14:13.030 --> 01:14:13.530
like that.

01:14:13.530 --> 01:14:16.680
Now, can you execute that squat
perfectly with assistance?

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:18.130
So you're holding
onto something.

01:14:18.130 --> 01:14:18.630
Great.

01:14:18.630 --> 01:14:21.900
If you can't, then don't
progress past that.

01:14:21.900 --> 01:14:23.550
Don't go now to a
barbell back squat

01:14:23.550 --> 01:14:26.730
if you can't do it correctly
when you have assistance.

01:14:26.730 --> 01:14:28.750
But let's assume most
people can do that.

01:14:28.750 --> 01:14:29.560
OK, great.

01:14:29.560 --> 01:14:31.920
Now you can move on to the
next step, which is can you

01:14:31.920 --> 01:14:34.725
do it well without assistance.

01:14:34.725 --> 01:14:36.960
So this would be
bodyweight only.

01:14:36.960 --> 01:14:38.610
Check, cool.

01:14:38.610 --> 01:14:39.690
We're good there.

01:14:39.690 --> 01:14:41.730
Now you can go
ahead and move on.

01:14:41.730 --> 01:14:46.000
Can you do it well with
an added e-centric load?

01:14:46.000 --> 01:14:49.440
So in this particular case,
if we're learning a squat,

01:14:49.440 --> 01:14:51.975
we can do it well when I hold
on to something, that's great.

01:14:51.975 --> 01:14:53.850
Now I can do it well
with just my bodyweight.

01:14:53.850 --> 01:14:55.380
Now if I put a
little bit of weight

01:14:55.380 --> 01:14:58.350
on, whether it's a kettlebell in
the front like a goblet squat,

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:00.840
or dumbbells to the side,
or whatever you want to do,

01:15:00.840 --> 01:15:02.610
can I lower it and
go all the way down

01:15:02.610 --> 01:15:05.070
and stay in perfect position?

01:15:05.070 --> 01:15:06.378
If you can do that, great.

01:15:06.378 --> 01:15:08.670
You're allowed to go to the
next step, which is can you

01:15:08.670 --> 01:15:11.230
hold it isometrically.

01:15:11.230 --> 01:15:13.080
So can you go all the
way down and then hold

01:15:13.080 --> 01:15:14.190
that bottom position?

01:15:14.190 --> 01:15:17.760
What you don't want to
do is start adding load,

01:15:17.760 --> 01:15:21.397
or speed, or fatigue
if you're going down

01:15:21.397 --> 01:15:22.980
to the bottom position
of the movement

01:15:22.980 --> 01:15:25.170
and you are out of control.

01:15:25.170 --> 01:15:26.410
We really want to avoid this.

01:15:26.410 --> 01:15:28.260
So I want you to show
me you can go down

01:15:28.260 --> 01:15:31.680
and you can lower the
weight under control.

01:15:31.680 --> 01:15:35.280
You can hold it in that
position under control.

01:15:35.280 --> 01:15:39.075
If we're clear there, now we
can add the concentric portion.

01:15:41.620 --> 01:15:43.480
You can now go ahead,
you can lower it,

01:15:43.480 --> 01:15:45.640
you can hold that
position of most danger,

01:15:45.640 --> 01:15:47.890
and now you can move up
at whatever speed we want.

01:15:47.890 --> 01:15:49.090
We are all good there.

01:15:49.090 --> 01:15:50.740
Once you can show
me those things,

01:15:50.740 --> 01:15:53.620
you can add the last two
steps which are now speed--

01:15:53.620 --> 01:15:55.150
if you choose to--

01:15:55.150 --> 01:15:57.760
and the last one,
which is fatigue.

01:15:57.760 --> 01:16:01.210
I would really discourage
people from doing exercises

01:16:01.210 --> 01:16:05.700
to fatigue, especially with
a moderate or high load,

01:16:05.700 --> 01:16:09.390
unless you can promise me you
can do these first six steps.

01:16:09.390 --> 01:16:13.350
If you can, you can basically
go hog wild with your training,

01:16:13.350 --> 01:16:15.540
and your chances of
injury are very low.

01:16:15.540 --> 01:16:19.570
Again, both acute injury,
as well as long-term injury,

01:16:19.570 --> 01:16:21.570
which is just sort of
like my joints ache,

01:16:21.570 --> 01:16:25.140
and all of a sudden my shoulder
hurts, and things like that.

01:16:25.140 --> 01:16:27.100
That's really what
I'm looking for.

01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:30.030
And once you're clear there,
you can train pretty hard.

01:16:30.030 --> 01:16:31.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
really like this.

01:16:31.530 --> 01:16:33.870
Because, recently, I was
showing somebody how to use a--

01:16:33.870 --> 01:16:35.730
in this case, it was
a hack squat machine.

01:16:35.730 --> 01:16:38.940
I notice they were
very timid of getting

01:16:38.940 --> 01:16:41.790
into a deep squat position.

01:16:41.790 --> 01:16:44.730
And they cited a
previous knee injury,

01:16:44.730 --> 01:16:46.398
which has long since healed.

01:16:46.398 --> 01:16:47.190
ANDY GALPIN: Right.

01:16:47.190 --> 01:16:48.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But even
with proper foot placement

01:16:48.900 --> 01:16:50.358
and everything,
you just tell, they

01:16:50.358 --> 01:16:53.190
were getting ginger about
it as they approached

01:16:53.190 --> 01:16:54.840
that bottom position.

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:57.960
But over time, with
pauses at the bottom,

01:16:57.960 --> 01:16:59.590
they've become very comfortable.

01:16:59.590 --> 01:17:03.030
And now actually are going
well below 90 degrees

01:17:03.030 --> 01:17:05.802
angle between femur
and lower leg.

01:17:05.802 --> 01:17:06.760
ANDY GALPIN: Excellent.

01:17:06.760 --> 01:17:10.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So it was clear
that it wasn't something range

01:17:10.650 --> 01:17:12.450
of motion limited
or it was just--

01:17:12.450 --> 01:17:16.860
it was a mental thing, but
a logical one for them.

01:17:16.860 --> 01:17:18.750
Now, after what you
just said, I think

01:17:18.750 --> 01:17:21.960
a better strategy
that I could have used

01:17:21.960 --> 01:17:25.500
would have been to have them
get into that position, just

01:17:25.500 --> 01:17:28.740
no weight at all, maybe
nothing on the sled,

01:17:28.740 --> 01:17:30.600
and then slowly
working up from there

01:17:30.600 --> 01:17:33.810
as opposed to doing what
we did in our case, which

01:17:33.810 --> 01:17:35.700
was to just convince
them that they

01:17:35.700 --> 01:17:37.680
were much stronger than
they thought they were.

01:17:37.680 --> 01:17:38.710
We eventually got there.

01:17:38.710 --> 01:17:41.700
But I'm realizing that there
was far too much mental anguish

01:17:41.700 --> 01:17:42.742
involved in that process.

01:17:42.742 --> 01:17:43.492
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:17:43.492 --> 01:17:45.000
This whole progression,
by the way,

01:17:45.000 --> 01:17:47.790
this can all happen
in one session.

01:17:47.790 --> 01:17:49.170
If you can check the boxes.

01:17:49.170 --> 01:17:51.630
In that example, you may
have been fine to jump there.

01:17:51.630 --> 01:17:53.810
It may have just been a,
hey, you're fine here.

01:17:53.810 --> 01:17:54.540
Get through it.

01:17:54.540 --> 01:17:55.110
Oh, OK.

01:17:55.110 --> 01:17:57.862
This whole progression
might take two years.

01:17:57.862 --> 01:17:59.820
I mean, this really
depends on your background,

01:17:59.820 --> 01:18:02.873
if you actually have injury
history, your comfort,

01:18:02.873 --> 01:18:04.290
your confidence,
all these things.

01:18:04.290 --> 01:18:06.098
So you don't need to
worry about rushing

01:18:06.098 --> 01:18:07.140
through that progression.

01:18:07.140 --> 01:18:08.530
You don't even need to
get all the way to the end

01:18:08.530 --> 01:18:10.740
if you don't want, especially
with speed and things

01:18:10.740 --> 01:18:11.830
like that.

01:18:11.830 --> 01:18:13.150
But again, it can happen.

01:18:13.150 --> 01:18:14.160
It doesn't have
to be like, well,

01:18:14.160 --> 01:18:15.480
it's a month of this, a month--

01:18:15.480 --> 01:18:15.870
well, no.

01:18:15.870 --> 01:18:17.245
If they feel great
and you can go

01:18:17.245 --> 01:18:18.810
through one to seven
in five minutes,

01:18:18.810 --> 01:18:20.460
then you're good to go.

01:18:20.460 --> 01:18:22.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Number six.

01:18:22.280 --> 01:18:24.070
ANDY GALPIN: Number
six now is just order.

01:18:24.070 --> 01:18:26.570
So you know how many days per
week you're going to work out.

01:18:26.570 --> 01:18:28.400
You know how long
they're going to take.

01:18:28.400 --> 01:18:32.418
You've selected all the
exercises you need to get done.

01:18:32.418 --> 01:18:33.960
You've balanced that
across the week.

01:18:33.960 --> 01:18:35.640
Now you just need to
put them in order.

01:18:35.640 --> 01:18:38.340
And the easy answer
here is generally

01:18:38.340 --> 01:18:42.810
do what's most important
first in the workout.

01:18:42.810 --> 01:18:45.920
There is some minor interference
effect of some other things

01:18:45.920 --> 01:18:46.420
there.

01:18:46.420 --> 01:18:50.700
But the reality of it is, if
you do the priority first,

01:18:50.700 --> 01:18:52.830
you're probably going to be OK.

01:18:52.830 --> 01:18:55.750
So whether this priority
is a muscle group.

01:18:55.750 --> 01:18:57.480
So in the example,
you want to make

01:18:57.480 --> 01:18:59.400
sure your glutes get trained.

01:18:59.400 --> 01:19:01.590
Maybe do it first.

01:19:01.590 --> 01:19:04.080
If you're trying to
maximize your back squat,

01:19:04.080 --> 01:19:06.600
you may not want to do a
bunch of glute exercises

01:19:06.600 --> 01:19:07.987
to fatigue first.

01:19:07.987 --> 01:19:09.570
But that's not the
priority we picked.

01:19:09.570 --> 01:19:12.300
We picked a different
one, which is

01:19:12.300 --> 01:19:14.580
buckets A, B, and C. OK, great.

01:19:14.580 --> 01:19:16.650
By doing it first, you
told me the priority

01:19:16.650 --> 01:19:18.810
was to make sure I do
something for my glutes.

01:19:18.810 --> 01:19:21.540
And then I would also like to
get my back squat a little bit

01:19:21.540 --> 01:19:23.510
stronger or whatever.

01:19:23.510 --> 01:19:24.010
Fine.

01:19:24.010 --> 01:19:26.410
The same thing could be done
for your endurance training.

01:19:26.410 --> 01:19:29.590
You could do your endurance
training before your lifting

01:19:29.590 --> 01:19:31.480
if you understand
that that means

01:19:31.480 --> 01:19:34.390
you might be compromising
your lifting quality

01:19:34.390 --> 01:19:36.377
of the workout a little bit.

01:19:36.377 --> 01:19:37.960
But you might be
fine with that if you

01:19:37.960 --> 01:19:40.300
say the endurance work is
more important right now.

01:19:40.300 --> 01:19:40.935
Amazing.

01:19:40.935 --> 01:19:42.310
You don't know
the answer to that

01:19:42.310 --> 01:19:45.702
though if you hadn't gone
through steps one through four.

01:19:45.702 --> 01:19:47.410
And that's why those
things are critical.

01:19:47.410 --> 01:19:50.648
So it makes what we call
chaos management, which

01:19:50.648 --> 01:19:51.940
is things happen in the moment.

01:19:51.940 --> 01:19:52.600
I don't know what to do.

01:19:52.600 --> 01:19:53.710
What should I choose?

01:19:53.710 --> 01:19:55.480
That decision
becomes really clear

01:19:55.480 --> 01:19:58.120
because you can always go back
to the beginning of my priority

01:19:58.120 --> 01:19:59.560
was this.

01:19:59.560 --> 01:20:01.870
Then therefore, that's
my choice today.

01:20:01.870 --> 01:20:05.530
So it provides a very
simple set of instructions

01:20:05.530 --> 01:20:07.780
for when the workout
gets cut short,

01:20:07.780 --> 01:20:09.610
when your workout
has to be in a hotel,

01:20:09.610 --> 01:20:12.950
and any number of things
that pop up in real life,

01:20:12.950 --> 01:20:15.640
whether, again, you're an
athlete or non-athlete.

01:20:15.640 --> 01:20:18.490
Either way, life will get
in the way at some point.

01:20:18.490 --> 01:20:21.760
So you need to have rules
and a system that says,

01:20:21.760 --> 01:20:23.920
when this happens, I
go right back to this.

01:20:23.920 --> 01:20:24.860
And that's my choice.

01:20:24.860 --> 01:20:25.360
Done.

01:20:25.360 --> 01:20:26.440
I'm moving on.

01:20:26.440 --> 01:20:27.700
No decision to make here.

01:20:27.700 --> 01:20:30.760
It's already been determined
a week ago, five weeks ago.

01:20:30.760 --> 01:20:31.960
We're often rolling.

01:20:31.960 --> 01:20:34.240
So the order, again,
is pretty simple.

01:20:34.240 --> 01:20:37.360
Just put the one that
is most important first.

01:20:37.360 --> 01:20:41.560
Now, I know you like
to do legs on Monday.

01:20:41.560 --> 01:20:42.490
That's great.

01:20:42.490 --> 01:20:43.630
I actually love that too.

01:20:43.630 --> 01:20:46.000
I do the same thing
generally, because

01:20:46.000 --> 01:20:48.610
to me, that's almost always
the most important thing.

01:20:48.610 --> 01:20:51.980
If I miss a bicep workout,
I'm probably fine.

01:20:51.980 --> 01:20:55.213
But I really don't like missing
the big movement pattern.

01:20:55.213 --> 01:20:57.130
So I make sure that those
happen on a day that

01:20:57.130 --> 01:20:58.480
tend to be more stable for me.

01:20:58.480 --> 01:21:00.700
Mondays are generally
pretty stable.

01:21:00.700 --> 01:21:04.090
Things get chaotic as the
week moves along for me.

01:21:04.090 --> 01:21:05.470
Others might be the opposite.

01:21:05.470 --> 01:21:07.300
Others might want to
go, hey, I'm actually

01:21:07.300 --> 01:21:10.300
going to keep Monday as
my flexible day or off day

01:21:10.300 --> 01:21:13.250
because I like to get
a lot of my work done,

01:21:13.250 --> 01:21:15.430
get that cleared so I
can have-- oh, sure.

01:21:15.430 --> 01:21:16.330
Work it around you.

01:21:16.330 --> 01:21:17.950
Some people love to
train on Saturdays

01:21:17.950 --> 01:21:20.110
because it's their most
free, some people hate it.

01:21:20.110 --> 01:21:20.800
Sure.

01:21:20.800 --> 01:21:22.707
You tell me, what is
the biggest priority,

01:21:22.707 --> 01:21:24.040
and what are you the most fresh?

01:21:24.040 --> 01:21:26.265
Monday, Tuesday,
it doesn't matter.

01:21:26.265 --> 01:21:27.640
It depends on your
work schedule.

01:21:27.640 --> 01:21:28.807
Maybe you work the weekends.

01:21:28.807 --> 01:21:29.800
I don't know.

01:21:29.800 --> 01:21:32.410
You decide what
day of the week are

01:21:32.410 --> 01:21:35.440
you generally the
most consistent,

01:21:35.440 --> 01:21:38.913
the most consistent schedule,
and the most consistent energy.

01:21:38.913 --> 01:21:41.080
And do the thing that is
most important on that day.

01:21:41.080 --> 01:21:43.678
It doesn't matter Monday,
Tuesday, day one, day three.

01:21:43.678 --> 01:21:45.470
We were sort of talking
about this earlier.

01:21:45.470 --> 01:21:48.250
But you actually don't even
have to do a week schedule.

01:21:48.250 --> 01:21:51.040
Our brains tend to like
to go year, month, week.

01:21:51.040 --> 01:21:52.780
But a lot of folks
will even just

01:21:52.780 --> 01:21:55.390
run this thing in terms of a
seven or nine-day schedule.

01:21:55.390 --> 01:21:59.260
In fact, we ran a
nine-day training schedule

01:21:59.260 --> 01:22:01.480
for one of my Major
League Baseball players.

01:22:01.480 --> 01:22:04.330
And he's eight or so
years into his career.

01:22:04.330 --> 01:22:07.188
And he's hitting
all-time PRs in velocity.

01:22:07.188 --> 01:22:08.230
And he's very, very good.

01:22:08.230 --> 01:22:10.660
And it was a nine-day
training cycle.

01:22:10.660 --> 01:22:13.460
And we ran that for
the entire season.

01:22:13.460 --> 01:22:15.623
So it doesn't have to
be a seven-day split.

01:22:15.623 --> 01:22:17.290
But it tends to work
for a lot of people

01:22:17.290 --> 01:22:20.230
because most people have a
fairly consistent schedule

01:22:20.230 --> 01:22:21.160
across the seven days.

01:22:21.160 --> 01:22:22.858
So pick the thing
that is most important

01:22:22.858 --> 01:22:24.400
and do it first,
and do it on the day

01:22:24.400 --> 01:22:26.650
of the week that is
most consistent for you

01:22:26.650 --> 01:22:28.780
in terms of schedule and energy.

01:22:28.780 --> 01:22:31.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I really
like what you're describing.

01:22:31.030 --> 01:22:33.197
I should just say that one
of the reasons I put legs

01:22:33.197 --> 01:22:34.930
on Monday is because
I tend to get

01:22:34.930 --> 01:22:36.610
enough sleep on the weekends.

01:22:36.610 --> 01:22:39.068
I generally get enough sleep
during the middle of the week.

01:22:39.068 --> 01:22:40.780
But oftentimes,
things will come up.

01:22:40.780 --> 01:22:44.357
I can be pretty sure, however,
that I've, quote unquote,

01:22:44.357 --> 01:22:45.940
caught up on my sleep
on the weekends.

01:22:45.940 --> 01:22:47.170
This notion of
catching up on sleep

01:22:47.170 --> 01:22:48.205
is a little dicey
scientifically.

01:22:48.205 --> 01:22:48.550
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:22:48.550 --> 01:22:50.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But I tend
to be pretty rested by Monday

01:22:50.260 --> 01:22:51.307
morning.
ANDY GALPIN: Right.

01:22:51.307 --> 01:22:54.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And actually,
my week begins on Sunday.

01:22:54.310 --> 01:22:56.290
And Sundays are when I
get my long form cardio.

01:22:56.290 --> 01:22:56.860
ANDY GALPIN: There you go.

01:22:56.860 --> 01:22:59.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So those two
are really non-negotiable.

01:22:59.260 --> 01:23:01.870
And the reason that long
form cardio is on Sunday

01:23:01.870 --> 01:23:04.160
is that it can take
many different forms.

01:23:04.160 --> 01:23:06.220
It can take a hike
with a weighted vest.

01:23:06.220 --> 01:23:08.305
It can take the form of a jog.

01:23:08.305 --> 01:23:09.680
It can be done
with other people.

01:23:09.680 --> 01:23:10.597
It can be family time.

01:23:10.597 --> 01:23:13.130
It can be time with
friends and so on.

01:23:13.130 --> 01:23:15.670
And that's pretty hard to do
during the middle of the week

01:23:15.670 --> 01:23:17.830
or pretty hard to
ensure, at least for me.

01:23:17.830 --> 01:23:19.960
I also find that by
bookending the week

01:23:19.960 --> 01:23:24.760
with some non-negotiable days
of training on Sunday, Monday,

01:23:24.760 --> 01:23:26.860
then if the week
gets busy Tuesday,

01:23:26.860 --> 01:23:29.200
Wednesday or even sometimes
Thursday with travel

01:23:29.200 --> 01:23:32.230
and things like that, one can
catch up toward the weekend.

01:23:32.230 --> 01:23:32.905
It's not ideal.

01:23:32.905 --> 01:23:33.160
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:23:33.160 --> 01:23:35.243
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I mean,
ideally, it's spaced out.

01:23:35.243 --> 01:23:36.975
But really, this
isn't about what I do.

01:23:36.975 --> 01:23:38.350
This is really
just to underscore

01:23:38.350 --> 01:23:39.767
the principle you
described, which

01:23:39.767 --> 01:23:43.600
is, I have a very clear
sense now over three decades

01:23:43.600 --> 01:23:46.390
or so of training and
three decades or so

01:23:46.390 --> 01:23:51.040
of being involved in academics,
and science, and work, of when

01:23:51.040 --> 01:23:53.110
I tend to be most
rested, when I tend

01:23:53.110 --> 01:23:55.030
to have some flexibility
in my schedule.

01:23:55.030 --> 01:23:57.550
And also, when I'm
trying to combine fitness

01:23:57.550 --> 01:24:00.040
with some of my
social engagements,

01:24:00.040 --> 01:24:01.240
which is actually quite fun.

01:24:01.240 --> 01:24:04.180
One thing I note is
that the four boxes

01:24:04.180 --> 01:24:06.610
that you mentioned before,
work, relationships, fitness,

01:24:06.610 --> 01:24:07.330
and recovery.

01:24:07.330 --> 01:24:09.010
Some of them do
have some crossover.

01:24:09.010 --> 01:24:09.460
ANDY GALPIN: They all do.

01:24:09.460 --> 01:24:11.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A hike
with family or friends

01:24:11.380 --> 01:24:15.310
is both relationship
and fitness, and so on.

01:24:15.310 --> 01:24:17.980
But I love the principle.

01:24:17.980 --> 01:24:20.050
Because anything that
can add consistency,

01:24:20.050 --> 01:24:22.823
as you pointed it out, is
going to greatly increase

01:24:22.823 --> 01:24:24.490
the probability of
reaching one's goals.

01:24:24.490 --> 01:24:25.970
That's sort of an obvious one.

01:24:25.970 --> 01:24:29.380
But in an earlier episode
you also said something

01:24:29.380 --> 01:24:30.940
that I wrote down
and is really still

01:24:30.940 --> 01:24:33.970
ringing in my mind, especially
now, which is that consistency

01:24:33.970 --> 01:24:35.770
always beats intensity.

01:24:35.770 --> 01:24:36.910
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

01:24:36.910 --> 01:24:38.350
Yeah, absolutely.

01:24:38.350 --> 01:24:41.380
We used to do a thing--

01:24:41.380 --> 01:24:44.320
when I was training NFL players
for the combine many years

01:24:44.320 --> 01:24:45.040
ago--

01:24:45.040 --> 01:24:49.300
where Saturdays were supposed
to be the day they came in

01:24:49.300 --> 01:24:51.290
and we did the
most regeneration.

01:24:51.290 --> 01:24:53.290
So this is when they
get body work done.

01:24:53.290 --> 01:24:56.620
And we do hot cold contrast
and sort of all these things.

01:24:56.620 --> 01:25:00.220
And our attendance was like 1%.

01:25:00.220 --> 01:25:01.008
Nobody showed up.

01:25:01.008 --> 01:25:02.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN: For a massage?

01:25:02.300 --> 01:25:03.385
ANDY GALPIN: Nobody showed up.

01:25:03.385 --> 01:25:04.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.
I'm surprised.

01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:05.230
ANDY GALPIN: Seems right.
But remember--

01:25:05.230 --> 01:25:06.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
love a good massage.

01:25:06.400 --> 01:25:07.358
ANDY GALPIN: Of course.

01:25:07.358 --> 01:25:09.610
But remember,
you're 18 years old.

01:25:09.610 --> 01:25:11.440
You're likely to
be getting millions

01:25:11.440 --> 01:25:15.070
of dollars handed to you in
the next few weeks or months.

01:25:15.070 --> 01:25:15.910
And--

01:25:15.910 --> 01:25:17.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's not
referring to me, by the way.

01:25:17.200 --> 01:25:18.033
ANDY GALPIN: No, no.

01:25:18.033 --> 01:25:20.698
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm actually
quite a bit older than 18.

01:25:20.698 --> 01:25:22.990
[LAUGHS] And I'm not getting
handed millions of dollars

01:25:22.990 --> 01:25:23.490
each week.

01:25:23.490 --> 01:25:25.050
ANDY GALPIN: Right.

01:25:25.050 --> 01:25:26.820
So I would think that.

01:25:26.820 --> 01:25:28.990
But those folks, they
recover super fast.

01:25:28.990 --> 01:25:30.240
They've never really had that.

01:25:30.240 --> 01:25:34.950
And also, Friday night,
kind of enticing.

01:25:34.950 --> 01:25:36.300
And so, nothing was there.

01:25:36.300 --> 01:25:38.580
And the strategy then
was, what if we instead

01:25:38.580 --> 01:25:43.570
of having a important hard
training day on Saturday

01:25:43.570 --> 01:25:47.340
we transition and it is
only things they want to do.

01:25:47.340 --> 01:25:49.140
So we basically
identified, what are

01:25:49.140 --> 01:25:51.060
the things in training
you love the most,

01:25:51.060 --> 01:25:52.560
and let's do those Saturdays.

01:25:52.560 --> 01:25:55.050
And it turns out for those
folks, no surprise here,

01:25:55.050 --> 01:25:56.760
it was what we
call the gun show.

01:25:56.760 --> 01:25:58.380
So they would come into the gym.

01:25:58.380 --> 01:26:01.740
And we would literally do
nothing but biceps and triceps.

01:26:01.740 --> 01:26:02.730
They'd just get a pump.

01:26:02.730 --> 01:26:04.920
And then the deal was
though, you come in--

01:26:04.920 --> 01:26:07.560
and literally, it would come in
is, we would pick three guys.

01:26:07.560 --> 01:26:08.745
Say you, you, and you.

01:26:08.745 --> 01:26:10.500
You pick your favorite
bicep exercise.

01:26:10.500 --> 01:26:11.430
You pick your favorite one.

01:26:11.430 --> 01:26:12.390
You pick your favorite one.

01:26:12.390 --> 01:26:14.190
You three over there, you
pick your favorite tricep,

01:26:14.190 --> 01:26:14.910
tricep, tricep.

01:26:14.910 --> 01:26:16.160
And we just run a big circuit.

01:26:16.160 --> 01:26:17.498
Like this is, how many reps?

01:26:17.498 --> 01:26:18.040
I don't know.

01:26:18.040 --> 01:26:18.735
I don't care.
How many sets?

01:26:18.735 --> 01:26:19.485
I don't even care.

01:26:19.485 --> 01:26:20.610
Just pump away.

01:26:20.610 --> 01:26:22.140
I don't even care.

01:26:22.140 --> 01:26:24.073
We chose small muscle groups.

01:26:24.073 --> 01:26:25.740
Not really going to
interfere with much.

01:26:25.740 --> 01:26:27.990
We're training them for
the NFL combine, which is--

01:26:27.990 --> 01:26:30.840
it's not a-- it's a legs
performance, basically.

01:26:30.840 --> 01:26:33.150
So it's like, if they smash
their biceps and triceps

01:26:33.150 --> 01:26:34.900
on a Saturday, it's
not going to influence

01:26:34.900 --> 01:26:36.210
what we did on Monday.

01:26:36.210 --> 01:26:38.190
So recovery wasn't an issue.

01:26:38.190 --> 01:26:40.980
Once we finish the
gun show though,

01:26:40.980 --> 01:26:42.870
now you have to go
do your regen stuff.

01:26:42.870 --> 01:26:45.363
So if you need chiro work,
you need physical therapy,

01:26:45.363 --> 01:26:46.530
whatever you're going to do.

01:26:46.530 --> 01:26:48.720
So we would get
them in the building

01:26:48.720 --> 01:26:50.262
with the low-hanging fruit.

01:26:50.262 --> 01:26:52.470
And then we would actually
get them to do their work.

01:26:52.470 --> 01:26:53.580
You can do the same thing.

01:26:53.580 --> 01:26:55.710
And I honestly do
the same thing.

01:26:55.710 --> 01:26:57.788
I tend to do either--

01:26:57.788 --> 01:26:59.580
if I'm going to do an
upper or lower split,

01:26:59.580 --> 01:27:01.590
I'm going to do that
stuff either Friday

01:27:01.590 --> 01:27:03.840
night or Saturday.

01:27:03.840 --> 01:27:05.760
Because it's very
difficult for me

01:27:05.760 --> 01:27:10.040
to do a hard long workout
Friday night or even Friday

01:27:10.040 --> 01:27:11.040
morning for that matter.

01:27:11.040 --> 01:27:11.790
The same thing Saturday.

01:27:11.790 --> 01:27:12.330
I wake up.

01:27:12.330 --> 01:27:14.550
And now it's like,
it's family mode.

01:27:14.550 --> 01:27:15.240
It's kid things.

01:27:15.240 --> 01:27:16.050
I want to do stuff.

01:27:16.050 --> 01:27:17.340
I want-- man.

01:27:17.340 --> 01:27:19.740
But I can usually convince
myself to be like, all right,

01:27:19.740 --> 01:27:21.060
just go in there
and go 20 minutes

01:27:21.060 --> 01:27:22.530
and get your upper
body stuff done.

01:27:22.530 --> 01:27:23.030
All right.

01:27:23.030 --> 01:27:25.020
I can walk myself
into that mentally.

01:27:25.020 --> 01:27:27.300
It's harder to walk
yourself into your five

01:27:27.300 --> 01:27:28.650
sets of five Deadlift.

01:27:28.650 --> 01:27:32.070
It's sort of just like, whoa, I
ain't got that in me right now.

01:27:32.070 --> 01:27:34.740
My high intensity
intervals, the max stuff,

01:27:34.740 --> 01:27:36.000
I don't have that right now.

01:27:36.000 --> 01:27:38.580
So I'll either go for my long
steady state stuff, which

01:27:38.580 --> 01:27:40.590
is like, I'm going on the bike.

01:27:40.590 --> 01:27:43.680
I'm riding down to the beach,
or coming back, nasal only,

01:27:43.680 --> 01:27:46.740
I can get myself to go
for a bike ride, whatever.

01:27:46.740 --> 01:27:50.430
So I picked the thing that
I'm likely to do on the days

01:27:50.430 --> 01:27:53.280
where I'm probably going to
be my weakest, quote unquote.

01:27:53.280 --> 01:27:56.310
Not physically, but
motivation-wise.

01:27:56.310 --> 01:28:00.000
For a long time I try to--
it just got stuck in a way

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:02.125
where my harder stuff
was Friday nights.

01:28:02.125 --> 01:28:03.750
And I'm just like,
why am I doing this?

01:28:03.750 --> 01:28:06.060
I was having like
a 50% success rate.

01:28:06.060 --> 01:28:08.550
Just like we were having
a no percent success rate

01:28:08.550 --> 01:28:09.960
with the NFL guys on Saturday.

01:28:09.960 --> 01:28:12.600
So you have to be
a little bit tough.

01:28:12.600 --> 01:28:14.550
You have to grind sometimes.

01:28:14.550 --> 01:28:16.860
You have to get some
motivation and go after it.

01:28:16.860 --> 01:28:18.330
But you also have
to be like, well,

01:28:18.330 --> 01:28:20.790
this is just stupid planning.

01:28:20.790 --> 01:28:22.290
Why put yourself
in a position where

01:28:22.290 --> 01:28:24.180
you're just failing
over, and over,

01:28:24.180 --> 01:28:27.150
and over, when I could
move it and go, look,

01:28:27.150 --> 01:28:32.190
those sessions are going to
be things that are easier.

01:28:32.190 --> 01:28:36.810
They don't require as much gusto
to get up and get them done.

01:28:36.810 --> 01:28:38.660
I get those things
done 90% of the time.

01:28:38.660 --> 01:28:40.660
Because the worst case I
can be like, all right,

01:28:40.660 --> 01:28:41.580
we're going to go
do a family thing.

01:28:41.580 --> 01:28:42.372
Give me 20 minutes.

01:28:42.372 --> 01:28:45.130
I'm just going to run up
there and smash upper body.

01:28:45.130 --> 01:28:46.060
And you don't need--

01:28:46.060 --> 01:28:47.310
I need a 20 minute warm-up.

01:28:47.310 --> 01:28:49.710
[LAUGHS] It's like, I can
just jump into those things

01:28:49.710 --> 01:28:50.700
if I had to.

01:28:50.700 --> 01:28:53.800
If I feel great, then I can
still go do something else,

01:28:53.800 --> 01:28:55.800
or I could do more, I
could do a longer session.

01:28:55.800 --> 01:28:58.840
But you're sort of
immune to any situation.

01:28:58.840 --> 01:29:01.790
So I would book-end those,
I guess, is what I'm saying.

01:29:01.790 --> 01:29:04.290
What's the day you're going to
have the best day, and what's

01:29:04.290 --> 01:29:05.832
the day you generally
have the worst?

01:29:05.832 --> 01:29:09.373
And put the programs
around those situations.

01:29:09.373 --> 01:29:10.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:29:10.290 --> 01:29:12.340
I love the idea of
identifying the friction

01:29:12.340 --> 01:29:14.340
points, the high friction
and low friction days.

01:29:14.340 --> 01:29:14.730
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:29:14.730 --> 01:29:15.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Friction meaning anything

01:29:15.360 --> 01:29:18.010
that impedes you from
training consistently or well.

01:29:18.010 --> 01:29:21.840
And there are so many factors
that ratchet into that-- sleep,

01:29:21.840 --> 01:29:23.850
other social engagements, work.

01:29:23.850 --> 01:29:28.015
Friday night, I also
find it tough to do

01:29:28.015 --> 01:29:28.890
any kind of training.

01:29:28.890 --> 01:29:31.020
I do cardiovascular training.

01:29:31.020 --> 01:29:34.830
I do interval type training
on Fridays typically.

01:29:34.830 --> 01:29:37.867
But there's a lot of
cumulative fatigue and stress

01:29:37.867 --> 01:29:39.075
that happens across the week.

01:29:39.075 --> 01:29:39.390
ANDY GALPIN: Right on.

01:29:39.390 --> 01:29:41.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
usually, for a long time,

01:29:41.320 --> 01:29:42.905
gosh, more than a
decade now, I've

01:29:42.905 --> 01:29:44.280
been telling myself
that Saturday

01:29:44.280 --> 01:29:48.840
is the day that I try to
reduce my cortisol as much as

01:29:48.840 --> 01:29:49.920
possible from the week.

01:29:49.920 --> 01:29:50.280
ANDY GALPIN: There you go.

01:29:50.280 --> 01:29:51.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And then
Sunday is the day that I

01:29:51.450 --> 01:29:53.010
enjoy that low cortisol state.

01:29:53.010 --> 01:29:55.705
And that's actually what opened
up into the long, slow run.

01:29:55.705 --> 01:29:56.580
ANDY GALPIN: Amazing.

01:29:56.580 --> 01:29:58.288
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
actually like to think

01:29:58.288 --> 01:30:00.480
of myself as a bit of a
mule during those long runs.

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:01.230
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:30:01.230 --> 01:30:04.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I actually have
a shirt that has a sloth on it

01:30:04.200 --> 01:30:07.050
that I wear to remind myself
to go slowly on those runs.

01:30:07.050 --> 01:30:09.180
Not that I ever run that fast.

01:30:09.180 --> 01:30:11.670
But there's the
whole mindset around

01:30:11.670 --> 01:30:15.960
it is to be a bit of a mule,
just kind of moving through it.

01:30:15.960 --> 01:30:18.120
And the fatigue
factor is more one

01:30:18.120 --> 01:30:21.780
of at first there's just
a little bit of boredom.

01:30:21.780 --> 01:30:24.450
But then, I've noticed,
there's a whole different set

01:30:24.450 --> 01:30:26.993
of mental scapes that open up
under different training types.

01:30:26.993 --> 01:30:28.410
And this is maybe
something we get

01:30:28.410 --> 01:30:31.208
into a little bit in a
future episode or discussion.

01:30:31.208 --> 01:30:33.750
When you train really intensely
for short periods of time one

01:30:33.750 --> 01:30:36.540
way, your mind goes
into a particular state.

01:30:36.540 --> 01:30:38.400
When you do long
duration training,

01:30:38.400 --> 01:30:41.160
you're thinking and
indeed even the way

01:30:41.160 --> 01:30:43.320
it affects sleep patterns
is also very different.

01:30:43.320 --> 01:30:46.080
I think one of the great futures
for neuroscience and exercise

01:30:46.080 --> 01:30:48.480
science in collaboration
is to identify

01:30:48.480 --> 01:30:50.670
how different patterns
of physical movement

01:30:50.670 --> 01:30:53.003
relate to different patterns
of thinking and vice versa.

01:30:53.003 --> 01:30:53.878
ANDY GALPIN: Amazing.

01:30:53.878 --> 01:30:56.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Anyway,
something maybe to just earmark

01:30:56.340 --> 01:30:57.720
for a future conversation.

01:30:57.720 --> 01:30:59.580
But there's clearly
a relationship there.

01:30:59.580 --> 01:31:00.660
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:31:00.660 --> 01:31:04.650
Well, when we certainly know
of a pretty clear relationship

01:31:04.650 --> 01:31:08.580
between even what we would
classify as zone five exercise

01:31:08.580 --> 01:31:09.790
and deep sleep.

01:31:09.790 --> 01:31:12.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So a zone
five again being you're

01:31:12.000 --> 01:31:13.417
breathing a lot
through your mouth

01:31:13.417 --> 01:31:15.960
because you have to in order
to bring in enough oxygen

01:31:15.960 --> 01:31:17.940
to offset the
acidity created by--

01:31:17.940 --> 01:31:18.440
[LAUGHTER]

01:31:18.440 --> 01:31:19.200
ANDY GALPIN: The carbon.

01:31:19.200 --> 01:31:19.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Exactly.

01:31:19.800 --> 01:31:21.370
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.
This is the high heart rate.

01:31:21.370 --> 01:31:23.220
So I mean, if you're
going to look at it

01:31:23.220 --> 01:31:28.350
and hit a number, looking for
something like 30-plus minutes

01:31:28.350 --> 01:31:32.372
a week being in the top
10% of your heart rate.

01:31:32.372 --> 01:31:34.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That
impacts deep sleep.

01:31:34.080 --> 01:31:35.830
ANDY GALPIN: Is going
to positively impact

01:31:35.830 --> 01:31:38.740
deep sleep, as long as it's done
very far away from deep sleep.

01:31:38.740 --> 01:31:40.380
So you don't want
to do that at night.

01:31:40.380 --> 01:31:43.000
So you want-- in terms of time.

01:31:43.000 --> 01:31:45.750
So if you hit those
numbers earlier in the day,

01:31:45.750 --> 01:31:47.880
oftentimes that will
enhance deep sleep.

01:31:47.880 --> 01:31:49.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I
was looking at some papers

01:31:49.380 --> 01:31:49.880
recently.

01:31:49.880 --> 01:31:52.830
And the number that
emerged from those papers

01:31:52.830 --> 01:31:56.640
was that unless it's
low intensity exercise,

01:31:56.640 --> 01:32:01.250
trying to exercise about
six hours or more away

01:32:01.250 --> 01:32:02.858
from your sleep
time would be ideal.

01:32:02.858 --> 01:32:03.650
ANDY GALPIN: Bingo.

01:32:03.650 --> 01:32:05.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Now, that
said, for those of you that

01:32:05.420 --> 01:32:07.522
have to hit the gym or go
for a run in the evening

01:32:07.522 --> 01:32:09.980
and then are trying to fall
asleep four to six hours later,

01:32:09.980 --> 01:32:11.900
I wouldn't want that
statement to impede

01:32:11.900 --> 01:32:15.890
your regular exercising.

01:32:15.890 --> 01:32:16.640
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:32:16.640 --> 01:32:17.930
there's an easy trick to that.

01:32:17.930 --> 01:32:20.360
Just finish it with
down-regulation breathing.

01:32:20.360 --> 01:32:23.660
So that's one of our things,
that if you-- because that

01:32:23.660 --> 01:32:24.710
is a realistic situation.

01:32:24.710 --> 01:32:25.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:32:25.010 --> 01:32:27.800
Finish work at 5:00, or even
6:00, and then by the time

01:32:27.800 --> 01:32:30.590
you're training or running or
whatever you want to call it,

01:32:30.590 --> 01:32:32.533
it's a 7:30, 8:00,
you're home at 9:00.

01:32:32.533 --> 01:32:33.950
You're eating, and
then everyone's

01:32:33.950 --> 01:32:35.480
like, you can't eat
two hours before bed.

01:32:35.480 --> 01:32:37.790
Pretty soon you run into a
number of different collision

01:32:37.790 --> 01:32:39.440
points that make you
wonder whether or not

01:32:39.440 --> 01:32:40.820
you're doing everything
wrong or if it's

01:32:40.820 --> 01:32:41.900
really worth training at all.

01:32:41.900 --> 01:32:42.230
ANDY GALPIN: 100%.

01:32:42.230 --> 01:32:43.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Now, I
would argue it's better

01:32:43.280 --> 01:32:44.360
to train than not to train.

01:32:44.360 --> 01:32:44.660
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:32:44.660 --> 01:32:46.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
provided that you can still

01:32:46.280 --> 01:32:46.940
get to sleep.

01:32:46.940 --> 01:32:47.690
ANDY GALPIN: 100%.

01:32:47.690 --> 01:32:49.482
So you have to walk a
little bit of a game.

01:32:49.482 --> 01:32:51.320
We run into this issue
with the NBA players.

01:32:51.320 --> 01:32:53.362
You're playing games at
6:00 at night that start.

01:32:53.362 --> 01:32:56.130
Major League Baseball is
a 7:05, 7:10 pitch, right?

01:32:56.130 --> 01:32:57.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's right.

01:32:57.380 --> 01:32:58.797
ANDY GALPIN: And
also, by the way,

01:32:58.797 --> 01:33:00.890
we're changing time
zones every five days.

01:33:00.890 --> 01:33:04.440
UFC fighters and such, were
usually training twice a day.

01:33:04.440 --> 01:33:06.440
There is no option
to train, or I mean,

01:33:06.440 --> 01:33:08.400
we are training
twice a day always.

01:33:08.400 --> 01:33:10.830
So we have to come up
with strategies for that.

01:33:10.830 --> 01:33:12.830
And there's other
non-athlete scenarios,

01:33:12.830 --> 01:33:14.690
of course, where it's like,
there is no other option here.

01:33:14.690 --> 01:33:15.200
Cool.

01:33:15.200 --> 01:33:17.700
So what we do is a
couple of things.

01:33:17.700 --> 01:33:20.210
Number one, the further
away you can make it

01:33:20.210 --> 01:33:22.560
from sleep, the
better if possible.

01:33:22.560 --> 01:33:25.520
We do need to train, though,
around the same time you're

01:33:25.520 --> 01:33:27.230
going to be playing.

01:33:27.230 --> 01:33:28.290
That has to happen.

01:33:28.290 --> 01:33:31.490
So the harder and longer
we go in the training

01:33:31.490 --> 01:33:33.350
session, the harder
and longer we

01:33:33.350 --> 01:33:35.700
go in our down-regulation
post-exercise.

01:33:35.700 --> 01:33:40.280
And that is, in my estimation,
the number one lever

01:33:40.280 --> 01:33:42.800
you can pull that can help.

01:33:42.800 --> 01:33:46.220
Now, if it really does
start crushing sleep,

01:33:46.220 --> 01:33:49.220
you're going to have to make
a critical decision there.

01:33:49.220 --> 01:33:53.300
In general, it's not a good
reason to not exercise.

01:33:53.300 --> 01:33:57.140
But maybe you restrict to
only a couple of days a week

01:33:57.140 --> 01:33:59.000
you go all the way
up in intensity.

01:33:59.000 --> 01:34:01.310
And the rest of
the days maybe 70%,

01:34:01.310 --> 01:34:04.220
you stay in this kind
of a working zone.

01:34:04.220 --> 01:34:05.420
Awesome.

01:34:05.420 --> 01:34:07.190
Maybe it's a longer
down-regulation.

01:34:07.190 --> 01:34:10.250
Maybe there's other
strategies you can do.

01:34:10.250 --> 01:34:10.790
But yeah.

01:34:10.790 --> 01:34:12.560
You want to be
careful of-- and we've

01:34:12.560 --> 01:34:15.680
had this situation a number
of times where it's just

01:34:15.680 --> 01:34:18.200
sleep complaints, sleep
complaints, sleep complaints.

01:34:18.200 --> 01:34:21.020
We run full sleep studies
on them in their house.

01:34:21.020 --> 01:34:23.270
We do the whole thing
with absolute rest.

01:34:23.270 --> 01:34:23.930
We come in.

01:34:23.930 --> 01:34:25.880
We do the whole
thing eye tracking,

01:34:25.880 --> 01:34:27.770
biomarkers, the whole thing.

01:34:27.770 --> 01:34:30.500
And it's like, oh,
you just need to stop

01:34:30.500 --> 01:34:31.772
doing intervals at 8:00 PM.

01:34:31.772 --> 01:34:33.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And I would add to

01:34:33.230 --> 01:34:35.720
that, another
incentive for being

01:34:35.720 --> 01:34:38.480
able to train with
or without caffeine

01:34:38.480 --> 01:34:41.060
is that it's very clear that
even if you can fall asleep

01:34:41.060 --> 01:34:44.540
after ingesting caffeine
in the preceding hours that

01:34:44.540 --> 01:34:48.950
caffeine consumed in the,
gosh, even 12 but really eight

01:34:48.950 --> 01:34:51.410
to 10 hours, four hours
prior to bedtime really

01:34:51.410 --> 01:34:53.100
disrupts the
architecture of sleep.

01:34:53.100 --> 01:34:56.510
So if you critically rely on
caffeine in order to train,

01:34:56.510 --> 01:34:58.690
whatever your training
might be, and you

01:34:58.690 --> 01:35:00.440
know that sleep is
important for recovery,

01:35:00.440 --> 01:35:02.450
well, then it's pretty obvious
where I'm going with this.

01:35:02.450 --> 01:35:03.200
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:35:03.200 --> 01:35:05.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So having
that flexibility is vitally

01:35:05.540 --> 01:35:06.170
important.

01:35:06.170 --> 01:35:06.320
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:35:06.320 --> 01:35:07.737
You've probably
also covered this.

01:35:07.737 --> 01:35:09.840
But you can actually
measure that directly.

01:35:09.840 --> 01:35:13.580
So by eye tracking
patterns, you can actually

01:35:13.580 --> 01:35:15.470
identify the effects
that caffeine

01:35:15.470 --> 01:35:18.472
has on sleep independent
of sleep time or not.

01:35:18.472 --> 01:35:19.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:35:19.430 --> 01:35:20.870
There are never
positive effects.

01:35:20.870 --> 01:35:22.047
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

01:35:22.047 --> 01:35:24.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That said,
I am a proponent of caffeine

01:35:24.380 --> 01:35:26.030
early in the day.

01:35:26.030 --> 01:35:29.090
And caffeine does
have a lot of--

01:35:29.090 --> 01:35:32.990
well, it's
anti-neurodegenerative.

01:35:32.990 --> 01:35:34.700
As long as you're
not getting anxiety,

01:35:34.700 --> 01:35:37.160
it's pro-performance,
both mental and physical

01:35:37.160 --> 01:35:37.760
performance.

01:35:37.760 --> 01:35:39.597
But, of course, if you
do not need caffeine,

01:35:39.597 --> 01:35:41.930
if you're one of these mutants
that do not need caffeine

01:35:41.930 --> 01:35:44.510
in order to go about
your daily living

01:35:44.510 --> 01:35:47.040
with focus and intensity,
then, by all means,

01:35:47.040 --> 01:35:48.350
don't start taking caffeine.

01:35:48.350 --> 01:35:49.850
ANDY GALPIN: I'm
not the hugest fan.

01:35:49.850 --> 01:35:51.080
I am scientifically.

01:35:51.080 --> 01:35:51.890
100% or more.

01:35:51.890 --> 01:35:53.840
Personally, it does--
I don't do well on it.

01:35:53.840 --> 01:35:56.423
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, you seem
to ride a little bit more what

01:35:56.423 --> 01:35:59.150
we would call a
sympathetic tone, shifted

01:35:59.150 --> 01:36:00.380
towards more alert.

01:36:00.380 --> 01:36:03.440
I tend to be
naturally a bit more

01:36:03.440 --> 01:36:06.170
like my bulldog Costello
was, a little bit more

01:36:06.170 --> 01:36:07.490
on the mellow sleepy side.

01:36:07.490 --> 01:36:12.290
And caffeine just puts me right
at that alert but calm place.

01:36:12.290 --> 01:36:14.733
And I can get away
with drinking it--

01:36:14.733 --> 01:36:17.150
I wouldn't say a ridiculous,
but a fair amount of caffeine

01:36:17.150 --> 01:36:17.858
and remain there.

01:36:17.858 --> 01:36:21.080
But I do restrict it until the
time right up about 2:00 PM

01:36:21.080 --> 01:36:23.990
at the latest is really when I
were trying to drink caffeine.

01:36:23.990 --> 01:36:24.758
Number seven.

01:36:24.758 --> 01:36:25.550
ANDY GALPIN: Great.

01:36:25.550 --> 01:36:27.660
So number seven and
eight are pretty simple.

01:36:27.660 --> 01:36:31.250
This is now choose the
intensity and the volume.

01:36:31.250 --> 01:36:33.110
So we've discussed
those at length

01:36:33.110 --> 01:36:35.020
in the previous episodes.

01:36:35.020 --> 01:36:36.770
We probably don't have
the time to go back

01:36:36.770 --> 01:36:38.000
over all those details.

01:36:38.000 --> 01:36:40.130
So remember the adaptation
you're training for

01:36:40.130 --> 01:36:44.960
and pick the appropriate rep
range, total amount of sets,

01:36:44.960 --> 01:36:47.690
as well as the
intensity to then get

01:36:47.690 --> 01:36:49.880
the corresponding adaptation.

01:36:49.880 --> 01:36:51.920
All you have to do is
select those things.

01:36:51.920 --> 01:36:54.920
In terms of progression
through a week,

01:36:54.920 --> 01:36:57.140
the rule of thumb
we say for intensity

01:36:57.140 --> 01:37:01.440
is something around
three 3% per week.

01:37:01.440 --> 01:37:04.950
For volume, it will depend on
what you're doing a little bit,

01:37:04.950 --> 01:37:09.727
but any time you cross
more than 10% per week,

01:37:09.727 --> 01:37:11.560
you're going to start
running into problems.

01:37:11.560 --> 01:37:13.350
So I like 5% better.

01:37:13.350 --> 01:37:15.540
It doesn't need to
be as low as three.

01:37:15.540 --> 01:37:17.280
You can jump up
much more than that.

01:37:17.280 --> 01:37:18.790
5% to 7% is better.

01:37:18.790 --> 01:37:22.560
So if you are
doing, say, running,

01:37:22.560 --> 01:37:24.960
because the numbers
make it easy,

01:37:24.960 --> 01:37:28.600
and you're doing 10
miles per week total.

01:37:28.600 --> 01:37:32.580
And if you were to go up to
11 miles the next week, great.

01:37:32.580 --> 01:37:33.730
You're right around 10%.

01:37:33.730 --> 01:37:36.030
But what you wouldn't
want to do is say,

01:37:36.030 --> 01:37:37.860
I'm running 10 miles this week.

01:37:37.860 --> 01:37:43.150
And I did maybe four Monday,
three Wednesday, three Friday.

01:37:43.150 --> 01:37:45.292
So four, three, and
three, you got your 10.

01:37:45.292 --> 01:37:47.250
Then you wouldn't want
to add a mile every day.

01:37:47.250 --> 01:37:49.860
So Monday, instead of
doing four, I did five.

01:37:49.860 --> 01:37:51.872
Wednesday instead of
doing three I did four.

01:37:51.872 --> 01:37:53.580
Friday instead of
doing three I did four.

01:37:53.580 --> 01:37:56.070
What you actually did is
you went from 10 to 13,

01:37:56.070 --> 01:37:59.730
which is a much higher jump
than the 10% prescribed.

01:37:59.730 --> 01:38:02.490
So the same thing would be
true for lifting weights.

01:38:02.490 --> 01:38:05.340
The same thing is actually
true for calories and trying

01:38:05.340 --> 01:38:06.300
to add them, et cetera.

01:38:06.300 --> 01:38:08.850
So the body tends to
not handle those things

01:38:08.850 --> 01:38:11.677
as well jumping more
than 10% per week.

01:38:11.677 --> 01:38:14.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So keeping
with this idea of increasing

01:38:14.010 --> 01:38:20.070
progressive overload, being 10%
more over some period of time,

01:38:20.070 --> 01:38:24.390
am I correct in assuming that
I want to identify one, maybe

01:38:24.390 --> 01:38:27.780
two meaningful variables
and progressing

01:38:27.780 --> 01:38:29.220
that or those variables?

01:38:29.220 --> 01:38:30.720
ANDY GALPIN: So
progressive overload

01:38:30.720 --> 01:38:33.850
can come in the form of any
of the modifiable variables.

01:38:33.850 --> 01:38:36.990
So you could increase the
complexity of the movement.

01:38:36.990 --> 01:38:39.660
You could increase the
intensity or the load.

01:38:39.660 --> 01:38:44.130
You can increase the volume by
either more sets, more reps,

01:38:44.130 --> 01:38:45.962
or more total
exercises in a day.

01:38:45.962 --> 01:38:47.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
about time under tension?

01:38:47.920 --> 01:38:49.950
ANDY GALPIN: You can
also manipulate how--

01:38:49.950 --> 01:38:51.750
the tempo of each repetition.

01:38:51.750 --> 01:38:55.020
You can also manipulate how
many times per day you train.

01:38:55.020 --> 01:38:56.880
So you can manipulate frequency.

01:38:56.880 --> 01:38:59.820
You can also manipulate
rest intervals.

01:38:59.820 --> 01:39:03.630
So you can progressively
load any of these things.

01:39:03.630 --> 01:39:06.200
Increase intensity.

01:39:06.200 --> 01:39:07.400
Run a little bit faster.

01:39:07.400 --> 01:39:11.030
Complete the same amount
of work slightly faster.

01:39:11.030 --> 01:39:14.093
Put 5% more on the barbell,
or the load, or the handle,

01:39:14.093 --> 01:39:15.260
or whatever you going to do.

01:39:15.260 --> 01:39:16.370
That's the simple way.

01:39:16.370 --> 01:39:18.320
If you want to think
about volume, in the case

01:39:18.320 --> 01:39:19.940
of endurance work is simple--

01:39:19.940 --> 01:39:22.470
mileage, time, whatever.

01:39:22.470 --> 01:39:24.680
In the case of lifting,
all you have to do

01:39:24.680 --> 01:39:27.230
is take the amount
of repetitions

01:39:27.230 --> 01:39:31.010
you're doing per set,
multiply it by the sets,

01:39:31.010 --> 01:39:32.100
add that all up.

01:39:32.100 --> 01:39:35.150
So if you're doing three sets
of 10, that's 30 repetitions.

01:39:35.150 --> 01:39:37.880
If you did three exercises,
you just did 90 repetitions.

01:39:37.880 --> 01:39:39.332
Put that number down for Monday.

01:39:39.332 --> 01:39:40.790
Put that number
down for Wednesday.

01:39:40.790 --> 01:39:42.123
Put that number down for Friday.

01:39:42.123 --> 01:39:43.400
Add that total up.

01:39:43.400 --> 01:39:45.770
So say you did 90, 90, 90.

01:39:45.770 --> 01:39:49.160
You would look and say, my total
number of repetitions this week

01:39:49.160 --> 01:39:50.900
is 270.

01:39:50.900 --> 01:39:53.540
If I want to go
up 5%, then I need

01:39:53.540 --> 01:39:58.488
to go up another 15 or
so total repetitions.

01:39:58.488 --> 01:39:59.780
Great that's all we have to do.

01:39:59.780 --> 01:40:00.613
That's the increase.

01:40:00.613 --> 01:40:03.770
You may keep the load exactly
the same, keep the exercises

01:40:03.770 --> 01:40:05.100
the same, change nothing else.

01:40:05.100 --> 01:40:08.870
But you want to add 15 more
total reps for your week.

01:40:08.870 --> 01:40:10.820
And you may choose
to do that by adding

01:40:10.820 --> 01:40:13.370
one more repetition per set.

01:40:13.370 --> 01:40:13.970
Close enough.

01:40:13.970 --> 01:40:15.650
So last week I did
three sets of 10.

01:40:15.650 --> 01:40:18.330
This week I'm going
to three sets of 11.

01:40:18.330 --> 01:40:19.910
It can be as simple as that.

01:40:19.910 --> 01:40:21.230
Again, it can be complex.

01:40:21.230 --> 01:40:22.730
I walked you through--
it can be any

01:40:22.730 --> 01:40:23.930
of the modifiable variables.

01:40:23.930 --> 01:40:26.300
But the progression I just
laid out is fairly simple,

01:40:26.300 --> 01:40:28.550
and it's honestly the one I
recommend for most people,

01:40:28.550 --> 01:40:31.040
just because it
will avoid confusion

01:40:31.040 --> 01:40:34.280
and it will avoid people
taking massive leaps in volume.

01:40:34.280 --> 01:40:37.940
So the typical strategy
I would recommend here

01:40:37.940 --> 01:40:41.810
is increasing load or
intensity, or a little bit

01:40:41.810 --> 01:40:48.260
of a combination, slowly
for about six or so weeks

01:40:48.260 --> 01:40:50.630
and then taking what we
generally call a de-load.

01:40:50.630 --> 01:40:53.540
So back down to maybe 70%.

01:40:53.540 --> 01:40:55.700
Whatever that number
is you've been doing.

01:40:55.700 --> 01:40:58.310
So you did three sets of
10 and you worked yourself

01:40:58.310 --> 01:41:01.130
all the way up to
three sets of 15.

01:41:01.130 --> 01:41:04.450
Back that down and maybe
we'll do two sets of eight

01:41:04.450 --> 01:41:05.410
for the week.

01:41:05.410 --> 01:41:07.660
And then we'll come
back the following week

01:41:07.660 --> 01:41:09.850
and go back and do the
highest we've done.

01:41:09.850 --> 01:41:12.370
Now we're all of a sudden we're
going to do four sets of 12

01:41:12.370 --> 01:41:13.460
or something like that.

01:41:13.460 --> 01:41:17.200
So if you get these
little de-loads every--

01:41:17.200 --> 01:41:19.838
depending on what you're
doing, four weeks or so--

01:41:19.838 --> 01:41:21.880
you should be in a spot
where you can continually

01:41:21.880 --> 01:41:24.640
progress for a very long
time without either burning

01:41:24.640 --> 01:41:27.698
out or overloading,
and over-stressing,

01:41:27.698 --> 01:41:28.490
and injury pattern.

01:41:28.490 --> 01:41:32.560
So the simple way, pick
intensity or volume.

01:41:32.560 --> 01:41:35.830
And just go up slightly every
week for a short span of time,

01:41:35.830 --> 01:41:37.442
generally around six weeks.

01:41:37.442 --> 01:41:39.400
And then you come back
and change your strategy

01:41:39.400 --> 01:41:40.092
if you'd like.

01:41:40.092 --> 01:41:42.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Because you
mentioned sets and repetitions

01:41:42.550 --> 01:41:44.200
here, I just wanted
to remind folks

01:41:44.200 --> 01:41:48.700
that in the episode that we did
on strength and hypertrophy--

01:41:48.700 --> 01:41:50.500
and that also included speed--

01:41:50.500 --> 01:41:53.510
there was a description of a
terrific program for strength,

01:41:53.510 --> 01:41:55.090
which is the three
by five program,

01:41:55.090 --> 01:41:57.500
or three to five
program, as it's called,

01:41:57.500 --> 01:42:01.990
which is to select three
to five exercises performed

01:42:01.990 --> 01:42:05.290
for three to five repetitions
three to five times

01:42:05.290 --> 01:42:07.810
per week with three
to five minutes rest

01:42:07.810 --> 01:42:09.100
in between those exercises.

01:42:09.100 --> 01:42:10.330
ANDY GALPIN: For
three to five sets.

01:42:10.330 --> 01:42:12.122
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
if I recall correctly,

01:42:12.122 --> 01:42:15.460
the protocol for generating
hypertrophy, muscle growth,

01:42:15.460 --> 01:42:19.480
is to perform a minimum of 10
and probably more like 15 to 20

01:42:19.480 --> 01:42:22.100
sets per muscle group per week.

01:42:22.100 --> 01:42:26.720
And that can be done in a single
session per muscle per week.

01:42:26.720 --> 01:42:28.930
So one could train,
for instance,

01:42:28.930 --> 01:42:30.970
quadriceps one day
per week, as long

01:42:30.970 --> 01:42:34.360
as you're getting that
volume of sets per week.

01:42:34.360 --> 01:42:38.530
Or it could be divided up across
two or three different sessions

01:42:38.530 --> 01:42:40.030
for that individual
muscle group.

01:42:40.030 --> 01:42:43.330
Of course, people are going to
target all their major muscle

01:42:43.330 --> 01:42:46.070
groups and hopefully some of
the minor muscle groups as well.

01:42:46.070 --> 01:42:48.070
And as I recall, the
number of repetitions

01:42:48.070 --> 01:42:50.347
that can generate
hypertrophy is quite broad,

01:42:50.347 --> 01:42:52.180
anywhere from six
repetitions all the way up

01:42:52.180 --> 01:42:53.200
to 30 repetitions.

01:42:53.200 --> 01:42:56.900
But by the end of the set,
it should be to failure

01:42:56.900 --> 01:42:58.843
or close to failure
with good form.

01:42:58.843 --> 01:42:59.510
Is that correct?

01:42:59.510 --> 01:43:00.260
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:43:00.260 --> 01:43:03.560
And we would say,
close to failure

01:43:03.560 --> 01:43:05.510
is probably most appropriate.

01:43:05.510 --> 01:43:08.270
You can actually
reach failure maybe

01:43:08.270 --> 01:43:10.460
on a few of the
sets, maybe the end.

01:43:10.460 --> 01:43:14.870
And probably best to choose
that with the exercises that

01:43:14.870 --> 01:43:19.850
are safer, not that any exercise
is particularly unsafe if you

01:43:19.850 --> 01:43:20.870
do it appropriately.

01:43:20.870 --> 01:43:23.930
But you may not want
to go to true failure

01:43:23.930 --> 01:43:29.090
on every set for the
more complex, larger,

01:43:29.090 --> 01:43:31.100
riskier exercises.

01:43:31.100 --> 01:43:35.185
So hedge pretty close to
failure, but not all the way.

01:43:35.185 --> 01:43:36.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And I realized I

01:43:36.560 --> 01:43:39.110
forgot to mention rest
intervals between sets.

01:43:39.110 --> 01:43:43.100
It follows that if a
large range of repetitions

01:43:43.100 --> 01:43:47.880
are performed that a large range
of rest intervals are allowed,

01:43:47.880 --> 01:43:50.360
meaning that there could be
rest intervals between sets

01:43:50.360 --> 01:43:52.160
of as low as 30
seconds between sets

01:43:52.160 --> 01:43:55.100
or as high of two or three
minutes, depending on the loads

01:43:55.100 --> 01:43:56.150
that one is using.

01:43:56.150 --> 01:43:59.392
And that, of course, will scale
with the number of repetitions.

01:43:59.392 --> 01:44:00.350
ANDY GALPIN: Excellent.

01:44:00.350 --> 01:44:03.110
In fact, that leads
me into step number

01:44:03.110 --> 01:44:06.440
nine of designing your own
training program, which

01:44:06.440 --> 01:44:08.610
is you've decided on a goal.

01:44:08.610 --> 01:44:09.980
We've worked our calendar out.

01:44:09.980 --> 01:44:11.900
We've figured out how
many days per week

01:44:11.900 --> 01:44:14.450
and how long we're going to
work out in those sessions.

01:44:14.450 --> 01:44:16.430
We then went and
selected our exercises.

01:44:16.430 --> 01:44:18.710
We balance them across
the movement patterns

01:44:18.710 --> 01:44:20.520
and the muscle
groups that we need

01:44:20.520 --> 01:44:24.500
so we're not causing excessive
stress on the same exact joint

01:44:24.500 --> 01:44:26.610
or muscle group over time.

01:44:26.610 --> 01:44:31.010
We then ordered our
exercises based on priority.

01:44:31.010 --> 01:44:33.050
Because of that, we've
identified our goal.

01:44:33.050 --> 01:44:35.730
We went back and we
selected the volume,

01:44:35.730 --> 01:44:38.840
which is the repetitions per
set, the total amount of sets,

01:44:38.840 --> 01:44:42.110
and the load per set that
matched the goal that we wanted

01:44:42.110 --> 01:44:43.620
to get or the adaptation.

01:44:43.620 --> 01:44:46.670
Now, all we have to do is fill
in the rest intervals, which

01:44:46.670 --> 01:44:49.040
reflect back, again, the goal.

01:44:49.040 --> 01:44:52.070
So generally higher rest
intervals, which means

01:44:52.070 --> 01:44:54.980
time that you rest
between your sets.

01:44:54.980 --> 01:44:59.390
Higher somewhere between two
to five minutes for things

01:44:59.390 --> 01:45:01.880
like speed, power, and strength.

01:45:01.880 --> 01:45:04.130
Perhaps a little bit lower,
although as you mentioned,

01:45:04.130 --> 01:45:06.170
it could also stay
high for hypertrophy.

01:45:06.170 --> 01:45:08.450
And then for endurance, you
follow the rest interval

01:45:08.450 --> 01:45:10.460
that reflects the type
of endurance training

01:45:10.460 --> 01:45:12.140
that you'd like to get.

01:45:12.140 --> 01:45:14.330
That's walked us through
one through nine.

01:45:14.330 --> 01:45:15.210
We're almost done.

01:45:15.210 --> 01:45:17.690
We've put together a pretty
nice little protocol.

01:45:17.690 --> 01:45:19.250
It should be well rounded.

01:45:19.250 --> 01:45:20.250
It should be effective.

01:45:20.250 --> 01:45:23.390
We've also talked about how to
progress it from week to week,

01:45:23.390 --> 01:45:27.440
keeping it within, again, four
to six weeks, maybe up to eight

01:45:27.440 --> 01:45:29.000
before we then take a back-off.

01:45:29.000 --> 01:45:30.800
The very last
thing we have to do

01:45:30.800 --> 01:45:34.970
to make sure this training
program is customized to you,

01:45:34.970 --> 01:45:38.930
your goal, and your situation,
which is then going to enhance

01:45:38.930 --> 01:45:41.480
your likelihood of
adherence and consistency,

01:45:41.480 --> 01:45:44.938
as well as increase the
likelihood of effectiveness

01:45:44.938 --> 01:45:46.730
is, we just have to do
a little bit of what

01:45:46.730 --> 01:45:48.140
we call chaos management.

01:45:48.140 --> 01:45:51.260
Which is take a quick
moment to think through,

01:45:51.260 --> 01:45:53.190
this program looks great.

01:45:53.190 --> 01:45:56.300
But if I had to
nitpick it, where

01:45:56.300 --> 01:45:58.520
are the possible
chances of failure?

01:45:58.520 --> 01:46:03.320
And you just want to
think about where would I

01:46:03.320 --> 01:46:04.890
predict things would go wrong?

01:46:04.890 --> 01:46:07.400
And if anything pops out to you.

01:46:07.400 --> 01:46:10.460
Try to come up with your
solution at the beginning.

01:46:10.460 --> 01:46:13.200
And this could be
a number of things.

01:46:13.200 --> 01:46:14.655
So maybe you've
picked an exercise

01:46:14.655 --> 01:46:16.280
and you realize, man,
I really actually

01:46:16.280 --> 01:46:17.540
don't like that exercise.

01:46:17.540 --> 01:46:18.920
Or maybe you look
at the schedule

01:46:18.920 --> 01:46:20.570
now that it's laid
out in front of you.

01:46:20.570 --> 01:46:21.862
You look at your work schedule.

01:46:21.862 --> 01:46:25.068
And you're like, ah, maybe
that's a bit aggressive.

01:46:25.068 --> 01:46:25.610
I don't know.

01:46:25.610 --> 01:46:27.510
It could be any
number of things.

01:46:27.510 --> 01:46:30.380
But it is a useful exercise to
just think through everything

01:46:30.380 --> 01:46:31.310
realistically.

01:46:31.310 --> 01:46:34.310
I typically just-- it's the
adage I teach my graduate

01:46:34.310 --> 01:46:35.240
students--

01:46:35.240 --> 01:46:38.010
before we hit Submit,
we're going to sleep.

01:46:38.010 --> 01:46:38.850
So it's there.

01:46:38.850 --> 01:46:39.350
It's ready.

01:46:39.350 --> 01:46:40.520
We're going to take 12 hours.

01:46:40.520 --> 01:46:41.978
We're going to wake
up the next day

01:46:41.978 --> 01:46:44.720
and look at it again and go,
are we sure we're good here?

01:46:44.720 --> 01:46:45.650
Yep.

01:46:45.650 --> 01:46:46.920
Make adjustments if you need.

01:46:46.920 --> 01:46:48.380
If not, if you feel
confident, then

01:46:48.380 --> 01:46:50.073
hit send and cross your fingers.

01:46:50.073 --> 01:46:50.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:46:50.990 --> 01:46:53.440
We were referring to
submitting the manuscript.

01:46:53.440 --> 01:46:54.690
It's interesting you say that.

01:46:54.690 --> 01:46:58.680
I have a statement that I
always make to people in my lab.

01:46:58.680 --> 01:47:00.253
They hit Submit,
and they always say

01:47:00.253 --> 01:47:02.670
that you realize you're going
to wake up tomorrow morning.

01:47:02.670 --> 01:47:04.440
There's going to be
an email in your inbox

01:47:04.440 --> 01:47:06.120
that something was
formatted incorrectly

01:47:06.120 --> 01:47:08.430
and you're going to spend
tomorrow reformatting

01:47:08.430 --> 01:47:09.690
and submitting again.

01:47:09.690 --> 01:47:13.700
So I've also learned that
every project is actually

01:47:13.700 --> 01:47:14.436
two projects--

01:47:14.436 --> 01:47:14.680
ANDY GALPIN: I'm sorry.

01:47:14.680 --> 01:47:16.570
I'm trying to not die over
here and interrupt you.

01:47:16.570 --> 01:47:17.130
[LAUGHS] It's true.

01:47:17.130 --> 01:47:17.550
You have no--

01:47:17.550 --> 01:47:17.742
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.
It's true.

01:47:17.742 --> 01:47:18.930
I've done this enough times.

01:47:18.930 --> 01:47:21.330
I've done this many
dozens of times.

01:47:21.330 --> 01:47:25.260
And then there's also
another truism of science

01:47:25.260 --> 01:47:27.317
which is that
there's the project,

01:47:27.317 --> 01:47:28.650
there's the scientific question.

01:47:28.650 --> 01:47:30.630
And then the paper is
yet another project.

01:47:30.630 --> 01:47:33.450
And I actually think this is
an analogy that carries over

01:47:33.450 --> 01:47:34.980
to other domains of life.

01:47:34.980 --> 01:47:37.020
I think that any time
we take on something,

01:47:37.020 --> 01:47:39.540
if we want to write a book,
or we want to get a degree,

01:47:39.540 --> 01:47:41.670
or we want to do
a fitness program,

01:47:41.670 --> 01:47:44.130
I think it's worth thinking
about those decisions

01:47:44.130 --> 01:47:47.190
as actually to taking
on two major things.

01:47:47.190 --> 01:47:50.230
Because one is the planning and
organization around that thing.

01:47:50.230 --> 01:47:51.940
And the other is the actual
performance of the thing.

01:47:51.940 --> 01:47:51.960
ANDY GALPIN: Wonderful.

01:47:51.960 --> 01:47:54.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And so, I
say that, because here what

01:47:54.990 --> 01:47:58.890
you just described, this 10
steps to consider in designing

01:47:58.890 --> 01:48:02.640
a program, I think some people
who are real list makers

01:48:02.640 --> 01:48:05.910
and love the precision
and the thoroughness--

01:48:05.910 --> 01:48:07.130
and I'm one of these people--

01:48:07.130 --> 01:48:08.130
thinking, this is great.

01:48:08.130 --> 01:48:10.860
I just want to check off each
one of these things on the list

01:48:10.860 --> 01:48:12.780
and figure out the
ideal program for me

01:48:12.780 --> 01:48:14.732
for a given period
of time et cetera.

01:48:14.732 --> 01:48:17.190
And then other folks might be
thinking, well, that's a lot.

01:48:17.190 --> 01:48:18.490
That's just a lot to do.

01:48:18.490 --> 01:48:20.430
But what I know
with certainty is

01:48:20.430 --> 01:48:24.540
that performing those sorts
of, let's just call them

01:48:24.540 --> 01:48:26.610
what they are, those
tasks of figuring out

01:48:26.610 --> 01:48:28.260
what's what, where
the defenders are,

01:48:28.260 --> 01:48:32.400
et cetera, without question
makes everything go so much

01:48:32.400 --> 01:48:36.300
more smoothly once you are
into the actual performance,

01:48:36.300 --> 01:48:39.540
the action of doing the
exercise program, or the book,

01:48:39.540 --> 01:48:42.040
or the podcast, or whatever
it is that you happen to do.

01:48:42.040 --> 01:48:47.460
So I'm grateful that you
brought up both the things that

01:48:47.460 --> 01:48:52.440
act as conduits for getting
good work done and this notion

01:48:52.440 --> 01:48:54.780
of defenders and bottlenecks.

01:48:54.780 --> 01:48:56.820
Because we don't
consider those, I

01:48:56.820 --> 01:48:59.348
would argue that it's
a very low probability

01:48:59.348 --> 01:49:00.390
that anyone will succeed.

01:49:00.390 --> 01:49:03.490
But when one does consider
those, even just a few of them,

01:49:03.490 --> 01:49:05.830
I think the probability
of success goes way,

01:49:05.830 --> 01:49:06.872
way up immediately.

01:49:06.872 --> 01:49:08.830
ANDY GALPIN: That's
actually a very good point.

01:49:08.830 --> 01:49:10.680
That is a lot of work
for a lot of people.

01:49:10.680 --> 01:49:14.580
And I know when I'm
consuming information,

01:49:14.580 --> 01:49:18.700
it is helpful to hear structure,
and systems, and design.

01:49:18.700 --> 01:49:21.640
It's also helpful to hear
actual real-life examples.

01:49:21.640 --> 01:49:24.690
So maybe the next
thing we can do here

01:49:24.690 --> 01:49:27.780
is I can just walk you
through an entire setup

01:49:27.780 --> 01:49:32.670
and a program considering folks
that are in bucket A, B, and C.

01:49:32.670 --> 01:49:36.420
And maybe I'll save a little
bit of the explanation.

01:49:36.420 --> 01:49:38.340
And we'll eliminate
maybe some background.

01:49:38.340 --> 01:49:41.170
And I'll just walk you through
what this could look like.

01:49:41.170 --> 01:49:41.670
All right.

01:49:41.670 --> 01:49:46.710
So I created a program which
should run about a year.

01:49:46.710 --> 01:49:50.680
And the idea here is that this
could be an evergreen system.

01:49:50.680 --> 01:49:55.930
So one could check off all the
boxes that we've talked about.

01:49:55.930 --> 01:49:59.250
So in general we want to
have three primary goals

01:49:59.250 --> 01:50:00.250
with exercise.

01:50:00.250 --> 01:50:03.332
We want to look a certain way,
whatever that means to you.

01:50:03.332 --> 01:50:05.790
We want to be able to perform
a certain way, whether that's

01:50:05.790 --> 01:50:09.120
for life goals, like hiking
and energy, or sport goals,

01:50:09.120 --> 01:50:10.260
or whatever.

01:50:10.260 --> 01:50:13.830
And then we want to be able to
do that across our lifespan.

01:50:13.830 --> 01:50:18.390
So a program that gives you
all the goals we talked about,

01:50:18.390 --> 01:50:22.680
and a program that covers that
health combine that we referred

01:50:22.680 --> 01:50:24.870
to way back in some of
our earlier discussions,

01:50:24.870 --> 01:50:27.510
which as a little
bit of a recap is,

01:50:27.510 --> 01:50:30.180
what are the physical
fitness parameters that we

01:50:30.180 --> 01:50:33.750
know are critical to maintaining
both lifespan and wellness

01:50:33.750 --> 01:50:34.560
span?

01:50:34.560 --> 01:50:36.210
And as a bit of
a reminder, those

01:50:36.210 --> 01:50:40.680
are things like your grip
strength, your leg strength,

01:50:40.680 --> 01:50:42.930
your total amount
of muscle mass,

01:50:42.930 --> 01:50:46.050
your actual speed and power
so that you can catch yourself

01:50:46.050 --> 01:50:52.450
from a fall, your VO2 max,
and your physical fitness.

01:50:52.450 --> 01:50:54.810
So I want a program that does
a little bit of all that.

01:50:54.810 --> 01:50:56.190
It's similar,
actually, and we're

01:50:56.190 --> 01:50:59.190
kind of crossing barriers
between our three buckets.

01:50:59.190 --> 01:51:01.093
So I need to be able
to control my fat.

01:51:01.093 --> 01:51:02.760
I need to be able to
have enough muscle.

01:51:02.760 --> 01:51:05.340
That muscle needs to
have enough function.

01:51:05.340 --> 01:51:09.010
And I need to be able to
maintain range of motion

01:51:09.010 --> 01:51:11.370
so that I don't lose
flexibility and get hurt.

01:51:11.370 --> 01:51:13.890
And then I need to
have a good VO2 max

01:51:13.890 --> 01:51:16.900
as well as to be able to
sustain energy over time.

01:51:16.900 --> 01:51:20.210
So that was the
goal of my progress.

01:51:20.210 --> 01:51:22.710
Now, a couple of other things
that we haven't chatted about,

01:51:22.710 --> 01:51:24.780
which are very important.

01:51:24.780 --> 01:51:27.360
You have mentioned, I
think, on a previous podcast

01:51:27.360 --> 01:51:28.945
about the importance
of seeing light?

01:51:28.945 --> 01:51:31.320
Is this something you've
covered at one point or another?

01:51:31.320 --> 01:51:32.237
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:51:32.237 --> 01:51:33.948
I joked that I'll be
going into the grave

01:51:33.948 --> 01:51:35.490
and they'll be
shoveling dirt onto me

01:51:35.490 --> 01:51:38.380
and I'll be telling people
what I'll tell you again now,

01:51:38.380 --> 01:51:42.090
which is to get five to 30
minutes of sunlight viewing

01:51:42.090 --> 01:51:45.825
as early in the day as
possible, ideally from sunlight.

01:51:45.825 --> 01:51:47.850
But that's why it's
called sunlight.

01:51:47.850 --> 01:51:50.250
Or from bright lights of
another kind if you cannot get

01:51:50.250 --> 01:51:50.820
sunlight.

01:51:50.820 --> 01:51:52.290
And also get that
in the evening.

01:51:52.290 --> 01:51:54.190
And then avoid bright lights
between the hours of 10:00

01:51:54.190 --> 01:51:56.130
PM and 4:00 AM, unless you
do shift work, in which case,

01:51:56.130 --> 01:51:57.630
check out our episode
on shift work.

01:51:57.630 --> 01:51:58.880
ANDY GALPIN: [LAUGHS] Amazing.

01:51:58.880 --> 01:52:00.780
So we've got a little
bit of a juxtaposition

01:52:00.780 --> 01:52:02.520
where people are like, I
need to work out and do

01:52:02.520 --> 01:52:03.270
all this training.

01:52:03.270 --> 01:52:05.610
But then I'm also
supposed to be outside.

01:52:05.610 --> 01:52:08.620
How do I blend those two things
into my training program?

01:52:08.620 --> 01:52:09.120
Cool.

01:52:09.120 --> 01:52:10.620
So I checked that box as well.

01:52:10.620 --> 01:52:11.700
I built that in.

01:52:11.700 --> 01:52:13.950
The last thing here
is we've talked

01:52:13.950 --> 01:52:15.240
about structured exercise.

01:52:15.240 --> 01:52:17.850
And just in this episode,
we've really opened up

01:52:17.850 --> 01:52:20.772
and do non-structured
exercise-- hiking, sports,

01:52:20.772 --> 01:52:21.480
things like that.

01:52:21.480 --> 01:52:23.520
Well, one thing that
is incredibly clear--

01:52:23.520 --> 01:52:26.070
and my colleague and
friend, Tommy Wood,

01:52:26.070 --> 01:52:30.120
at the University of Washington,
published a fantastic paper

01:52:30.120 --> 01:52:34.740
very recently on the
importance of proprioception

01:52:34.740 --> 01:52:39.330
in maintaining and staving
off late onset dementia

01:52:39.330 --> 01:52:39.997
and Parkinson's.

01:52:39.997 --> 01:52:41.538
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Maybe just to remind

01:52:41.538 --> 01:52:42.970
people what proprioception is.

01:52:42.970 --> 01:52:44.220
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, absolutely.

01:52:44.220 --> 01:52:45.660
So there's structured exercise.

01:52:45.660 --> 01:52:47.020
And that's very important.

01:52:47.020 --> 01:52:50.820
But then there's also things
like balance, and coordination.

01:52:50.820 --> 01:52:52.470
And proprioception
meaning you're

01:52:52.470 --> 01:52:55.180
adjusting to stimuli coming
in from the outside world.

01:52:55.180 --> 01:52:57.310
So this stimuli could be sound.

01:52:57.310 --> 01:52:58.320
It could be light.

01:52:58.320 --> 01:52:59.340
It could be smell.

01:52:59.340 --> 01:53:01.030
Or in the physical
case of the body,

01:53:01.030 --> 01:53:03.190
it is where you are at in space.

01:53:03.190 --> 01:53:05.407
So I'm feeling like I'm
falling to the left.

01:53:05.407 --> 01:53:07.740
Therefore, I need to correct
and move back to the right.

01:53:07.740 --> 01:53:09.900
So you don't get this
with doing things

01:53:09.900 --> 01:53:12.090
like a hack squat on a machine.

01:53:12.090 --> 01:53:15.370
You get this typically
from being outside.

01:53:15.370 --> 01:53:17.130
So now you're smelling
and seeing things.

01:53:17.130 --> 01:53:18.900
And you're also not
landing with your foot

01:53:18.900 --> 01:53:21.900
in the exact same position
on an even platform.

01:53:21.900 --> 01:53:24.750
We get this from
things like sport.

01:53:24.750 --> 01:53:26.700
Now I'm not only
exercising, but I'm

01:53:26.700 --> 01:53:28.230
reacting to the outside world.

01:53:28.230 --> 01:53:30.750
The ball is going over here,
my opponent's going over there.

01:53:30.750 --> 01:53:33.820
So it's very important,
in my opinion,

01:53:33.820 --> 01:53:37.650
to have at least one
session per week of exercise

01:53:37.650 --> 01:53:41.820
in which you are doing something
that challenges proprioception.

01:53:41.820 --> 01:53:44.970
So how do I fold all
of these best practices

01:53:44.970 --> 01:53:49.180
into one training program that's
not 200 hours a week seven days

01:53:49.180 --> 01:53:49.680
a week?

01:53:49.680 --> 01:53:51.115
That's what I've
laid out to you.

01:53:51.115 --> 01:53:51.615
Make sense?

01:53:51.615 --> 01:53:52.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Makes sense.

01:53:52.450 --> 01:53:53.880
ANDY GALPIN: Cool.
Let me walk you through it.

01:53:53.880 --> 01:53:58.350
And then, maybe we'll come back
into each individual category

01:53:58.350 --> 01:54:00.340
and you can ask
questions about them.

01:54:00.340 --> 01:54:05.730
So the way that I think
is best is to have a goal.

01:54:05.730 --> 01:54:09.960
And have that goal be around
eight to 10 weeks long,

01:54:09.960 --> 01:54:11.290
like we've been talking about.

01:54:11.290 --> 01:54:14.610
So what I gave you is, let's
start off with quarter number

01:54:14.610 --> 01:54:15.990
one of the year.

01:54:15.990 --> 01:54:18.360
So perhaps January
through March or so--

01:54:18.360 --> 01:54:21.240
and it doesn't have to be this
long, but just as an example.

01:54:21.240 --> 01:54:23.910
You decide your goal is going to
be to put some muscle mass on.

01:54:23.910 --> 01:54:27.450
So we're going to
prioritize adding muscle.

01:54:27.450 --> 01:54:29.820
Now, within that, you're
going to be bulking up,

01:54:29.820 --> 01:54:32.880
adding some muscle, but we're
also going to be sleeping more.

01:54:32.880 --> 01:54:35.310
We know we need extra
recovery in this session.

01:54:35.310 --> 01:54:37.140
And we need to go
up in calories.

01:54:37.140 --> 01:54:39.490
Now, this happens to work
nice for a couple of reasons.

01:54:39.490 --> 01:54:41.940
But in that
protocol, maybe we're

01:54:41.940 --> 01:54:44.520
going to do seven
days or seven sessions

01:54:44.520 --> 01:54:46.230
a week of physical activity.

01:54:46.230 --> 01:54:47.460
Doesn't mean seven days.

01:54:47.460 --> 01:54:50.670
But maybe those sessions
are something like I

01:54:50.670 --> 01:54:54.180
will do one indoor sport.

01:54:54.180 --> 01:54:57.810
This could be basketball,
could be any number of things.

01:54:57.810 --> 01:54:59.880
So I got my sport ticked
off, and it's indoors.

01:54:59.880 --> 01:55:00.750
Why?

01:55:00.750 --> 01:55:02.430
I'm in January to March.

01:55:02.430 --> 01:55:04.960
The weather's probably not
great for most of the world.

01:55:04.960 --> 01:55:07.500
So I'm not going to do
as much outside activity.

01:55:07.500 --> 01:55:10.080
I'm going to do weights maybe
three or four times a week,

01:55:10.080 --> 01:55:13.743
and then maybe two days a
week I'll go for a long walk.

01:55:13.743 --> 01:55:15.660
Again, we'll come back
and I'll explain to you

01:55:15.660 --> 01:55:17.927
why I made all these
individual choices

01:55:17.927 --> 01:55:20.010
so you're going to run
that for the first quarter.

01:55:20.010 --> 01:55:21.385
At the end of this
quarter you're

01:55:21.385 --> 01:55:22.710
going to take a de-load week.

01:55:22.710 --> 01:55:24.150
Now, this could be fully off.

01:55:24.150 --> 01:55:26.190
Maybe this is when you
schedule a vacation.

01:55:26.190 --> 01:55:27.610
Maybe this is backing off.

01:55:27.610 --> 01:55:29.130
Maybe you just
keep your walks in

01:55:29.130 --> 01:55:32.250
and you spend the extra time
on your family, or work,

01:55:32.250 --> 01:55:35.170
or whatever else we need to do.

01:55:35.170 --> 01:55:36.600
So we've bulked up a little bit.

01:55:36.600 --> 01:55:39.210
We spent 12 weeks
adding some mass.

01:55:39.210 --> 01:55:41.310
Now we're going to
transition into quarter two,

01:55:41.310 --> 01:55:43.590
which is where we start
to actually get lean.

01:55:43.590 --> 01:55:47.260
This is actually a pretty
standard bodybuilding template,

01:55:47.260 --> 01:55:49.410
which is put on some
mass first, and then you

01:55:49.410 --> 01:55:50.800
get lean after that.

01:55:50.800 --> 01:55:54.240
So now we're going to get
lean from April to June.

01:55:54.240 --> 01:55:56.260
We're going to bring
calories down a little bit.

01:55:56.260 --> 01:55:57.718
So now we're actually
going to play

01:55:57.718 --> 01:55:59.910
in a hypocaloric
caloric state somewhat.

01:55:59.910 --> 01:56:02.620
The days tend to get longer.

01:56:02.620 --> 01:56:04.870
So we're going to have more
time to spend in the sun.

01:56:04.870 --> 01:56:07.680
So we're going to shift a
little bit from an indoor sport

01:56:07.680 --> 01:56:10.650
activity, like the example I
said earlier was basketball,

01:56:10.650 --> 01:56:13.470
to maybe stand-up
paddleboarding,

01:56:13.470 --> 01:56:15.360
or some other thing
where you're actually

01:56:15.360 --> 01:56:17.108
getting your sport
done, you're reacting,

01:56:17.108 --> 01:56:18.900
you're using proprioception,
but now you're

01:56:18.900 --> 01:56:21.400
getting that sun in there as
well because you have a greater

01:56:21.400 --> 01:56:24.120
opportunity to actually do
so, and the weather probably

01:56:24.120 --> 01:56:26.820
is going to cooperate with
you more often than it would

01:56:26.820 --> 01:56:29.070
in, say, February.

01:56:29.070 --> 01:56:34.050
You then maybe going to pick a
fitness or an exercise class.

01:56:34.050 --> 01:56:38.400
Any number of routines where
you're with multiple people.

01:56:38.400 --> 01:56:41.260
And then, two days a
week in addition to that,

01:56:41.260 --> 01:56:44.070
you're going to maybe
lift some weights.

01:56:44.070 --> 01:56:46.800
So now, we've added some muscle.

01:56:46.800 --> 01:56:48.222
Now we've got lean.

01:56:48.222 --> 01:56:49.680
And all of a sudden,
we're actually

01:56:49.680 --> 01:56:52.114
looking pretty good
for the summertime.

01:56:52.114 --> 01:56:52.900
Hmm.

01:56:52.900 --> 01:56:54.100
Interesting.

01:56:54.100 --> 01:56:57.640
Quarter three, July to
September, we'll transition,

01:56:57.640 --> 01:57:00.610
and we'll try to get into
great cardiovascular shape.

01:57:00.610 --> 01:57:03.250
So we'll transition more into
some high intensity interval

01:57:03.250 --> 01:57:05.620
type of stuff more frequently.

01:57:05.620 --> 01:57:08.170
We're going to maybe stay
at maintenance calories now.

01:57:08.170 --> 01:57:10.720
We spend a little bit of time
hyper, then we went hypo,

01:57:10.720 --> 01:57:13.600
and now we're going to go back
to maintenance and keep along.

01:57:13.600 --> 01:57:15.880
We're going to continue to
choose some outdoor sports,

01:57:15.880 --> 01:57:17.080
but maybe you change it up.

01:57:17.080 --> 01:57:19.150
Maybe you keep the same one.

01:57:19.150 --> 01:57:21.340
Maybe now we switch
it out on the golf,

01:57:21.340 --> 01:57:23.530
or now we pick a pickleball,
or we play basketball,

01:57:23.530 --> 01:57:25.180
but now we just do it outside.

01:57:25.180 --> 01:57:27.520
Any number of things you can do.

01:57:27.520 --> 01:57:29.050
So maybe even we do a couple.

01:57:29.050 --> 01:57:29.620
Change it up.

01:57:29.620 --> 01:57:31.120
You do that twice a week.

01:57:31.120 --> 01:57:33.495
You're spending more
time in the sun now.

01:57:33.495 --> 01:57:34.870
You're looking
outside and you're

01:57:34.870 --> 01:57:37.390
seeing this great weather and
you're not cooped up in a gym

01:57:37.390 --> 01:57:39.640
but you're getting your
physical fitness in.

01:57:39.640 --> 01:57:41.950
That's also going to be
aiding in your high intensity

01:57:41.950 --> 01:57:43.450
or your interval,
your conditioning,

01:57:43.450 --> 01:57:45.158
because you're doing
more stuff like that

01:57:45.158 --> 01:57:46.738
rather than lifting in a gym.

01:57:46.738 --> 01:57:48.280
And then, maybe
you're actually going

01:57:48.280 --> 01:57:50.140
to do some track workouts.

01:57:50.140 --> 01:57:52.330
Maybe we'll do this on a
bike, or we'll do some hill

01:57:52.330 --> 01:57:55.820
sprints outside, any
number of things.

01:57:55.820 --> 01:57:57.760
And then we'll do that
maybe twice a week.

01:57:57.760 --> 01:58:00.700
And then we'll still
lift weights twice a week

01:58:00.700 --> 01:58:01.900
in our gym.

01:58:01.900 --> 01:58:05.302
The last quarter then is going
to be October to December.

01:58:05.302 --> 01:58:06.760
And we're going to
transition there

01:58:06.760 --> 01:58:10.962
into more pure
cardiovascular fitness.

01:58:10.962 --> 01:58:13.420
Because we're doing that, we're
going to be working harder.

01:58:13.420 --> 01:58:15.760
And remember,
cardiovascular training

01:58:15.760 --> 01:58:20.750
is generally expelling much
more calories than lifting.

01:58:20.750 --> 01:58:22.660
So we're going to actually
go up in calories.

01:58:22.660 --> 01:58:23.910
We're going to return to that.

01:58:23.910 --> 01:58:27.370
And that works out kind of well,
because I don't know if or not,

01:58:27.370 --> 01:58:29.500
but people tend to eat
a little more calories

01:58:29.500 --> 01:58:32.605
from the months of, say,
November through December.

01:58:32.605 --> 01:58:34.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
holidays, and at least

01:58:34.480 --> 01:58:36.490
in northern hemisphere,
colder temperatures.

01:58:36.490 --> 01:58:37.900
ANDY GALPIN: Totally.

01:58:37.900 --> 01:58:40.390
Maybe even we play with
two workouts a day here.

01:58:40.390 --> 01:58:42.040
We're trying to get
really in shape.

01:58:42.040 --> 01:58:44.770
We're trying to improve our
conditioning and our endurance

01:58:44.770 --> 01:58:46.180
in multiple areas.

01:58:46.180 --> 01:58:47.950
We're going to actually
transition back

01:58:47.950 --> 01:58:49.300
into an indoor sport.

01:58:49.300 --> 01:58:51.790
So maybe we're going
to do some kickboxing,

01:58:51.790 --> 01:58:54.713
or a jiu jitsu class,
or something like that.

01:58:54.713 --> 01:58:57.130
We're going to maybe hit the
cardio machine once or twice.

01:58:57.130 --> 01:59:00.040
Now we're hopping on a
StairMaster, a VersaClimber,

01:59:00.040 --> 01:59:03.160
more maybe get an assault bike
going, something like that.

01:59:03.160 --> 01:59:05.638
Maybe hit some machines
and do our lifting there.

01:59:05.638 --> 01:59:07.930
Maybe we spent the rest of
the earlier part of the year

01:59:07.930 --> 01:59:09.013
on barbells and dumbbells.

01:59:09.013 --> 01:59:10.750
We'll transition
to some machines.

01:59:10.750 --> 01:59:15.430
And then we'll still try to get
outside and walk twice a week.

01:59:15.430 --> 01:59:18.160
And that gets us our
outside activity,

01:59:18.160 --> 01:59:21.200
but it's not necessarily
a structured program.

01:59:21.200 --> 01:59:23.583
So we've got 15 minutes
where the weather's

01:59:23.583 --> 01:59:24.500
breaking a little bit.

01:59:24.500 --> 01:59:27.010
So let's walk, get
outside, and get a walk in.

01:59:27.010 --> 01:59:29.770
So that's the overall
structure of everything.

01:59:29.770 --> 01:59:31.820
I would like to actually
go back to the beginning

01:59:31.820 --> 01:59:35.230
now and walk through each
one of these things in detail

01:59:35.230 --> 01:59:37.810
and explain why I
chose certain things.

01:59:37.810 --> 01:59:39.460
I've kind of given
some hints already.

01:59:39.460 --> 01:59:41.835
But I think it'd be helpful
to walk back to the beginning

01:59:41.835 --> 01:59:42.610
and start there.

01:59:42.610 --> 01:59:43.568
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

01:59:43.568 --> 01:59:44.980
I of the overall structure.

01:59:44.980 --> 01:59:47.680
I have just a
couple of questions.

01:59:47.680 --> 01:59:51.700
The idea of training mostly
for hypertrophy January

01:59:51.700 --> 01:59:53.860
through March makes sense.

01:59:53.860 --> 01:59:59.140
Followed by a period from April
through June focusing primarily

01:59:59.140 --> 02:00:01.180
on fat loss.

02:00:01.180 --> 02:00:06.220
And then from July to September,
speed and interval type work.

02:00:06.220 --> 02:00:10.240
And then October
to December, you

02:00:10.240 --> 02:00:13.300
put to emphasize
endurance type training.

02:00:13.300 --> 02:00:16.870
I thought for a moment that when
we got to October, December,

02:00:16.870 --> 02:00:20.220
you were going to
emphasize strength.

02:00:20.220 --> 02:00:22.650
And I'm wondering
whether or not there's

02:00:22.650 --> 02:00:25.740
any incentive for training for
strength October, December,

02:00:25.740 --> 02:00:28.230
so that when one arrives
at the hypertrophy training

02:00:28.230 --> 02:00:32.190
January through March,
we're that much stronger.

02:00:32.190 --> 02:00:37.020
The idea being then there's
more muscle to hold on to as one

02:00:37.020 --> 02:00:39.210
then tries to lose fat
from April through June.

02:00:39.210 --> 02:00:41.760
And then July through
September is the speed work.

02:00:41.760 --> 02:00:44.070
Or is July through
September the speed

02:00:44.070 --> 02:00:46.465
slash power phase
of the program?

02:00:46.465 --> 02:00:48.090
ANDY GALPIN: The July
through September

02:00:48.090 --> 02:00:52.590
would be more like
your higher heart rate,

02:00:52.590 --> 02:00:55.620
learning to get all the way
up, maximum exertion, and then

02:00:55.620 --> 02:00:56.185
recovering.

02:00:56.185 --> 02:00:57.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: An
October to December

02:00:57.810 --> 02:00:59.520
is long-form endurance?

02:00:59.520 --> 02:01:01.140
ANDY GALPIN: Moderate
to long-form.

02:01:01.140 --> 02:01:04.170
So it's closer to that
aerobic capacity stuff.

02:01:04.170 --> 02:01:06.750
It is closer to longer
duration and moving

02:01:06.750 --> 02:01:08.100
through that spectrum.

02:01:08.100 --> 02:01:10.530
You are astute in pointing
out that I didn't have

02:01:10.530 --> 02:01:12.660
pure strength really in there.

02:01:12.660 --> 02:01:14.880
You certainly could fold it in.

02:01:14.880 --> 02:01:19.440
But quite literally, if you
spent three months bulking up

02:01:19.440 --> 02:01:21.240
in January to
March, that's going

02:01:21.240 --> 02:01:23.113
to bring some strength
along the way.

02:01:23.113 --> 02:01:24.280
So you should be fine there.

02:01:24.280 --> 02:01:27.948
But you absolutely could
alter any of these variables

02:01:27.948 --> 02:01:30.240
if you want to emphasize
something more than other one.

02:01:30.240 --> 02:01:32.615
So say you actually felt like
you ran through the fitness

02:01:32.615 --> 02:01:33.150
testing.

02:01:33.150 --> 02:01:36.120
And you identified, actually,
your endurance is pretty good.

02:01:36.120 --> 02:01:39.270
But you're struggling maybe
with a little bit of strength

02:01:39.270 --> 02:01:41.640
and maybe a little bit
of lower muscle mass.

02:01:41.640 --> 02:01:44.550
You could substitute quarter
three or quarter four

02:01:44.550 --> 02:01:46.945
and say, one of those
quarters will be strength.

02:01:46.945 --> 02:01:48.570
And then I'll do all
of my conditioning

02:01:48.570 --> 02:01:49.440
in another quarter.

02:01:49.440 --> 02:01:52.170
And what you've really
done is the programming

02:01:52.170 --> 02:01:53.340
is still fairly simple.

02:01:53.340 --> 02:01:55.620
You've just altered the
priorities a little bit.

02:01:55.620 --> 02:01:58.450
And therefore, you've altered
the adaptation across the year.

02:01:58.450 --> 02:01:59.910
And why this is
really important,

02:01:59.910 --> 02:02:02.850
this template is meant to be
something you can just run back

02:02:02.850 --> 02:02:05.160
year after year after year.

02:02:05.160 --> 02:02:07.560
And you make a subtle
change like that.

02:02:07.560 --> 02:02:11.070
And now, over the course
of five, 10, 20 years,

02:02:11.070 --> 02:02:14.220
you're going to be in a
fantastic spot at the end.

02:02:14.220 --> 02:02:16.450
So you can make easy
adjustments along the way

02:02:16.450 --> 02:02:19.560
as priorities pop
up, as goals pop up.

02:02:19.560 --> 02:02:21.060
But you're going
to be in a position

02:02:21.060 --> 02:02:23.505
where everything is--
there's nothing that's

02:02:23.505 --> 02:02:24.630
going to be lagging behind.

02:02:24.630 --> 02:02:25.672
You'll be in a good spot.

02:02:25.672 --> 02:02:29.490
Most of your bases are
covered to be pretty lean,

02:02:29.490 --> 02:02:33.438
have a good amount of muscle,
and to be in great shape.

02:02:33.438 --> 02:02:34.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Two other questions.

02:02:34.980 --> 02:02:36.500
One just a quick question.

02:02:36.500 --> 02:02:40.340
For sake of generating
proprioceptive feedback--

02:02:40.340 --> 02:02:45.040
during the endurance phase, is
trail-running a good option?

02:02:45.040 --> 02:02:46.040
ANDY GALPIN: Absolutely.

02:02:46.040 --> 02:02:46.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

02:02:46.730 --> 02:02:48.830
Thinking back to the days
running cross-country.

02:02:48.830 --> 02:02:50.750
It's October, December,
you're trail-running.

02:02:50.750 --> 02:02:51.140
ANDY GALPIN: Totally.

02:02:51.140 --> 02:02:52.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Training for endurance.

02:02:52.160 --> 02:02:53.990
ANDY GALPIN: The ground
is not super solid,

02:02:53.990 --> 02:02:55.880
which is even
better in this case.

02:02:55.880 --> 02:02:58.190
So you're making more
choices and trying

02:02:58.190 --> 02:02:59.430
to not fall on your face.

02:02:59.430 --> 02:03:00.245
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Absolutely.

02:03:00.245 --> 02:03:01.730
And then you
mentioned bulking up.

02:03:01.730 --> 02:03:06.930
And I just wanted to highlight
that there are some folks,

02:03:06.930 --> 02:03:10.220
myself included, while I'd like
to add a little bit of muscle

02:03:10.220 --> 02:03:12.928
here or there, I'm not
interested in overeating

02:03:12.928 --> 02:03:14.720
to the point where I
lay down a lot of body

02:03:14.720 --> 02:03:16.020
fat stores along with that.

02:03:16.020 --> 02:03:16.120
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

02:03:16.120 --> 02:03:17.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And I think
a lot of people out there

02:03:17.840 --> 02:03:20.910
are not necessarily interested
in, quote unquote, bulking up.

02:03:20.910 --> 02:03:24.710
I also-- my understanding
of the literature--

02:03:24.710 --> 02:03:26.690
and tell me if I'm wrong--

02:03:26.690 --> 02:03:29.570
is that while there
does need to be

02:03:29.570 --> 02:03:33.197
some sort of caloric
surplus above what

02:03:33.197 --> 02:03:35.030
is required to maintain
body weight in order

02:03:35.030 --> 02:03:37.970
to build muscle,
that many people who

02:03:37.970 --> 02:03:40.850
try and, quote unquote,
bulk up basically just

02:03:40.850 --> 02:03:43.370
end up expanding the
size of their cheeks

02:03:43.370 --> 02:03:46.980
and face along with
their limbs and torso.

02:03:46.980 --> 02:03:47.480
[LAUGHS]

02:03:47.480 --> 02:03:48.290
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:03:48.290 --> 02:03:49.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm not
trying to poke fun at them.

02:03:49.620 --> 02:03:51.830
But the idea of
deliberately overeating

02:03:51.830 --> 02:03:54.202
to the point where a lot of
body fat stores come along,

02:03:54.202 --> 02:03:56.660
I would imagine that would just
make the April through June

02:03:56.660 --> 02:03:57.755
phase that much harder.

02:03:57.755 --> 02:03:58.630
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

02:03:58.630 --> 02:04:00.513
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
I'm not sure it's ever

02:04:00.513 --> 02:04:01.430
been studied directly.

02:04:01.430 --> 02:04:03.470
But I can't imagine
it's all that--

02:04:03.470 --> 02:04:06.110
excuse me, all that
healthy, to bring along

02:04:06.110 --> 02:04:11.000
a lot of adipose tissue in
one's pursuit of hypertrophy.

02:04:11.000 --> 02:04:12.780
ANDY GALPIN: You're
absolutely correct.

02:04:12.780 --> 02:04:15.750
We have not gotten into the
nutritional details there.

02:04:15.750 --> 02:04:16.700
But yeah, thank you.

02:04:16.700 --> 02:04:17.840
Good clarification point.

02:04:17.840 --> 02:04:18.770
A couple of things.

02:04:18.770 --> 02:04:21.410
You're not going to be
doing this very long.

02:04:21.410 --> 02:04:22.177
It's 12 weeks.

02:04:22.177 --> 02:04:24.260
We're not going to be six,
seven, or eight months.

02:04:24.260 --> 02:04:25.353
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
haven't seen me eat.

02:04:25.353 --> 02:04:26.180
[LAUGHTER]

02:04:26.180 --> 02:04:29.750
ANDY GALPIN: Number two, just
since we're here to clarify,

02:04:29.750 --> 02:04:32.930
the literature is
ongoing in this area.

02:04:32.930 --> 02:04:34.970
And there's actually
a handful of studies

02:04:34.970 --> 02:04:36.630
that I know are coming soon.

02:04:36.630 --> 02:04:39.210
But in general, when I
say hypercaloric here,

02:04:39.210 --> 02:04:43.280
I'm referring to an increasing
caloric intake above baseline

02:04:43.280 --> 02:04:46.950
by something like 10% to 15%.

02:04:46.950 --> 02:04:50.840
So if you normally eat 2,500
calories throughout the day,

02:04:50.840 --> 02:04:54.050
you might add
another 250 to 400.

02:04:54.050 --> 02:04:56.000
I'm not doubling calories.

02:04:56.000 --> 02:04:58.400
I don't want you to
be stuffing your face,

02:04:58.400 --> 02:05:01.490
hating food, feeling
awful all day,

02:05:01.490 --> 02:05:03.470
and then putting on half
of your weight as fat

02:05:03.470 --> 02:05:05.090
and half your weight as muscle.

02:05:05.090 --> 02:05:08.090
It is just technically
hypercaloric

02:05:08.090 --> 02:05:09.620
because you're
eating more, which

02:05:09.620 --> 02:05:14.030
is an absolute requirement
for most people to add muscle.

02:05:14.030 --> 02:05:17.240
Some folks who have a high
percentage of body fat

02:05:17.240 --> 02:05:19.700
and a low level of
fitness training

02:05:19.700 --> 02:05:22.820
can actually get
away with just being

02:05:22.820 --> 02:05:25.430
either isocaloric, technically,
or even a little bit lower,

02:05:25.430 --> 02:05:27.710
and still adding some muscle
while losing some fat.

02:05:27.710 --> 02:05:29.880
But for most folks, that's
going to be challenging.

02:05:29.880 --> 02:05:33.230
So you're going to want to
be in a hypercaloric state.

02:05:33.230 --> 02:05:37.130
Another reason I put it in
here is because, remember,

02:05:37.130 --> 02:05:40.190
people tend to make
these extra calorie

02:05:40.190 --> 02:05:43.160
choices during this part
of the year anyways.

02:05:43.160 --> 02:05:46.910
And so, you're playing into
life is why I chose that.

02:05:46.910 --> 02:05:50.960
It's like, hey, you can't
restrict calories all the time.

02:05:50.960 --> 02:05:52.140
It's really, really hard.

02:05:52.140 --> 02:05:54.945
So maybe if we can put
it calorie restriction

02:05:54.945 --> 02:05:57.320
during the phases of the year
that's a little bit easier,

02:05:57.320 --> 02:05:59.830
and give you the freedom
to have a little bit

02:05:59.830 --> 02:06:01.580
more calories during
the phase of the year

02:06:01.580 --> 02:06:03.830
when you're probably going
to want to do that anyways.

02:06:03.830 --> 02:06:06.740
Just make sure you're doing
a style of training that

02:06:06.740 --> 02:06:07.800
supports that.

02:06:07.800 --> 02:06:09.560
So you're going to be
trying to add muscle

02:06:09.560 --> 02:06:11.600
when you know you're going
to be adding more calories.

02:06:11.600 --> 02:06:13.370
We're going to be
trying to really push

02:06:13.370 --> 02:06:15.020
the pace on our
conditioning when

02:06:15.020 --> 02:06:17.480
we know we're going to be
eating more calories anyways.

02:06:17.480 --> 02:06:19.550
And so, that is
actually, in fact,

02:06:19.550 --> 02:06:23.810
exactly why I chose those goals
for those times of the year.

02:06:23.810 --> 02:06:27.320
It's because we're now playing
into life a little bit more.

02:06:27.320 --> 02:06:31.670
But we, again, certainly do not
want to be eating to an excess

02:06:31.670 --> 02:06:34.550
or where it's causing some of
the problems you mentioned.

02:06:34.550 --> 02:06:37.310
We just need to be
eating a little bit more.

02:06:37.310 --> 02:06:40.820
The last point here is, the
next phase, April to June,

02:06:40.820 --> 02:06:42.960
we know we're going hypocaloric.

02:06:42.960 --> 02:06:44.650
So it's always kind
of nice to go, yeah,

02:06:44.650 --> 02:06:47.150
we're going to go in a little
bit of a calorie deficit here.

02:06:47.150 --> 02:06:49.520
But it's really just
these few months.

02:06:49.520 --> 02:06:52.822
And it's OK, because I spent
the last six months where

02:06:52.822 --> 02:06:55.280
I wasn't restricting that much,
and then one actually where

02:06:55.280 --> 02:06:57.360
I got to eat a little bit more.

02:06:57.360 --> 02:06:59.768
And now, cool, not hard
for me to convince somebody

02:06:59.768 --> 02:07:02.060
that to go we're going to
bring the calories down right

02:07:02.060 --> 02:07:04.345
now, or in a month,
in two months.

02:07:04.345 --> 02:07:06.470
And it's just going to be
this 12 or 16-week phase,

02:07:06.470 --> 02:07:08.053
or whatever you end
up being in there.

02:07:08.053 --> 02:07:09.838
So those were some
of the rationale

02:07:09.838 --> 02:07:11.880
that I was thinking of
when I decided to do that.

02:07:11.880 --> 02:07:12.330
But thank you.

02:07:12.330 --> 02:07:14.747
That's a very important point
in terms of the hypocaloric.

02:07:14.747 --> 02:07:17.030
It's not the dirty bulk.

02:07:17.030 --> 02:07:19.910
It's not the excess that
a lot of folks will do.

02:07:19.910 --> 02:07:21.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And just a final

02:07:21.830 --> 02:07:26.040
point for folks in the
Southern hemisphere, Australia,

02:07:26.040 --> 02:07:27.170
and South Americans.

02:07:27.170 --> 02:07:27.290
ANDY GALPIN: Ah,
flip everything.

02:07:27.290 --> 02:07:29.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We actually
have a large listenership

02:07:29.900 --> 02:07:31.350
in the Southern hemisphere.

02:07:31.350 --> 02:07:32.600
Of course, adjust accordingly.

02:07:32.600 --> 02:07:34.820
Even though the holiday
months are still

02:07:34.820 --> 02:07:37.130
in November, December,
there are, of course,

02:07:37.130 --> 02:07:38.540
holidays all year long.

02:07:38.540 --> 02:07:39.080
ANDY GALPIN: Of course.
You got Fourth of July.

02:07:39.080 --> 02:07:40.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Many,
many of the major holidays

02:07:40.910 --> 02:07:42.320
are around November, December.

02:07:42.320 --> 02:07:45.740
But it's summer down there.

02:07:45.740 --> 02:07:47.130
Just adjust accordingly.

02:07:47.130 --> 02:07:48.837
There's nothing
holy about trying

02:07:48.837 --> 02:07:51.170
to achieve certain adaptations
at certain times of year.

02:07:51.170 --> 02:07:54.170
It's more about trying
to eliminate bottlenecks,

02:07:54.170 --> 02:07:56.030
defenders, as you mentioned.

02:07:56.030 --> 02:07:57.870
And it's really
about the sequence.

02:07:57.870 --> 02:08:00.560
ANDY GALPIN: So if we go
back to that first quarter,

02:08:00.560 --> 02:08:03.320
we're going to try to add
some mass, for the reasons

02:08:03.320 --> 02:08:05.600
I just described.

02:08:05.600 --> 02:08:07.490
It's also tends to
be pretty motivating.

02:08:07.490 --> 02:08:08.660
You're going to
start the year off.

02:08:08.660 --> 02:08:11.035
You're going to want to train
and get all excited because

02:08:11.035 --> 02:08:12.650
of your New Year's resolution.

02:08:12.650 --> 02:08:14.565
And you're going to see
results immediately.

02:08:14.565 --> 02:08:16.940
We've talked about this in
some of the previous episodes.

02:08:16.940 --> 02:08:18.607
The nice part about
hypertrophy training

02:08:18.607 --> 02:08:20.930
is you see your muscles
growing right now.

02:08:20.930 --> 02:08:23.960
Where the endurance stuff
tends to have a little bit more

02:08:23.960 --> 02:08:25.530
of a delayed gratification.

02:08:25.530 --> 02:08:28.100
So I'm going to give
you a win early.

02:08:28.100 --> 02:08:30.830
Now, we're also going
to be sleeping more.

02:08:30.830 --> 02:08:33.020
Because we know--
and maybe we'll

02:08:33.020 --> 02:08:34.820
get into this in
a future episode--

02:08:34.820 --> 02:08:39.230
that sleep is absolutely
critical to recovery

02:08:39.230 --> 02:08:40.820
and critical to
growing muscle mass.

02:08:40.820 --> 02:08:42.470
So you're going
to emphasize sleep

02:08:42.470 --> 02:08:44.480
more during this
part of the year

02:08:44.480 --> 02:08:46.560
also because the
sun is very low.

02:08:46.560 --> 02:08:48.770
It's harder to sleep
for a lot of folks

02:08:48.770 --> 02:08:50.507
longer when the sun
is out for longer,

02:08:50.507 --> 02:08:52.840
especially if you don't have
a perfect blackout curtain.

02:08:52.840 --> 02:08:54.560
And so, you're
just trying to play

02:08:54.560 --> 02:08:58.320
with the restrictions life gives
you and optimize your scenario.

02:08:58.320 --> 02:09:00.680
So the sun's probably
not out very often.

02:09:00.680 --> 02:09:02.240
And especially,
depending on where

02:09:02.240 --> 02:09:04.820
you live, if you're anywhere
like where I grew up

02:09:04.820 --> 02:09:06.340
in the Pacific
Northwest, [LAUGHS]

02:09:06.340 --> 02:09:08.840
it's going to be dark, and gray,
and gloomy most of the day.

02:09:08.840 --> 02:09:11.480
So it's not hard to convince you
to go sleep a little bit more

02:09:11.480 --> 02:09:12.590
often.

02:09:12.590 --> 02:09:14.120
So we'll do that.

02:09:14.120 --> 02:09:16.880
That's also, again, why
I chose an indoor sport.

02:09:16.880 --> 02:09:21.200
That activity, you're going to
not shoot yourself in the foot.

02:09:21.200 --> 02:09:23.900
Being in the gym when
it's cold and crappy

02:09:23.900 --> 02:09:26.152
outside is not that hard
to convince yourself to do.

02:09:26.152 --> 02:09:28.610
So you're going to be lifting
your weights, say, four times

02:09:28.610 --> 02:09:29.510
a week.

02:09:29.510 --> 02:09:32.840
And then, again, getting
some outside time

02:09:32.840 --> 02:09:34.988
in the form of a walk
so that you can do it

02:09:34.988 --> 02:09:36.530
in the middle of
work if you have to,

02:09:36.530 --> 02:09:40.700
or catching 20 minutes here or
there, whatever it needs to be.

02:09:40.700 --> 02:09:43.910
The chances of you missing
that walk are little.

02:09:43.910 --> 02:09:46.280
And you'll still get
some outside time.

02:09:46.280 --> 02:09:49.660
You've talked about the
importance of getting sunlight

02:09:49.660 --> 02:09:51.220
in even if it is overcast.

02:09:51.220 --> 02:09:54.070
So you can still
nail all those boxes

02:09:54.070 --> 02:09:56.620
and be in a pretty good spot
at the end of that quarter.

02:09:56.620 --> 02:10:00.070
So moving on to quarter
two then, April to June.

02:10:00.070 --> 02:10:03.820
A lot of people want to look
good during the summer months.

02:10:03.820 --> 02:10:05.350
You're more likely
to be outside.

02:10:05.350 --> 02:10:07.570
You're more likely to have your
shirt off because it's hot,

02:10:07.570 --> 02:10:08.830
because you're
either on vacation,

02:10:08.830 --> 02:10:09.820
or going to the beach.

02:10:09.820 --> 02:10:11.710
So let's play into
that a little bit.

02:10:11.710 --> 02:10:15.730
Let's let people look
a little bit better,

02:10:15.730 --> 02:10:18.610
if that's what they determine
to be looking better,

02:10:18.610 --> 02:10:21.100
during the months when they're
more likely to have that.

02:10:21.100 --> 02:10:24.580
You're also more likely to
have things like weddings

02:10:24.580 --> 02:10:25.540
over the summertime.

02:10:25.540 --> 02:10:27.430
People don't get married
often in the winter.

02:10:27.430 --> 02:10:29.513
And so, people want to
look good for these events.

02:10:29.513 --> 02:10:32.170
So let's play into what a
lot of people already want.

02:10:32.170 --> 02:10:34.060
And let's help you get leaner.

02:10:34.060 --> 02:10:37.300
Not a lot of
holidays that involve

02:10:37.300 --> 02:10:38.657
eating during that phase.

02:10:38.657 --> 02:10:40.990
And so, you're not going to
feel like you're missing out

02:10:40.990 --> 02:10:44.980
on a ton of life outside of
maybe a few smaller holidays

02:10:44.980 --> 02:10:46.360
in that phase.

02:10:46.360 --> 02:10:47.562
The days are getting longer.

02:10:47.562 --> 02:10:50.020
And so, we're going to choose
to get in the sun more often.

02:10:50.020 --> 02:10:51.610
We can start getting
a tan better.

02:10:51.610 --> 02:10:53.780
We can start getting
ready for summer.

02:10:53.780 --> 02:10:57.670
And so, that's is why we
exchanged our indoor sport

02:10:57.670 --> 02:10:59.140
for an outdoor sport--

02:10:59.140 --> 02:11:02.320
surfing, hiking,
cycling outside,

02:11:02.320 --> 02:11:04.325
whatever the thing
is you want to do.

02:11:04.325 --> 02:11:05.200
There's tons of them.

02:11:05.200 --> 02:11:07.533
Kiteboarding, like I said,
skateboarding-- there you go.

02:11:07.533 --> 02:11:10.150
Get out and start skating
a little bit, whatever

02:11:10.150 --> 02:11:11.290
it needs to be.

02:11:11.290 --> 02:11:13.510
So we'll do that
once a week or so.

02:11:13.510 --> 02:11:16.930
And then I actually threw
in a fitness class here.

02:11:16.930 --> 02:11:18.950
And there's a couple of reasons.

02:11:18.950 --> 02:11:22.810
One, now it's sort of nice to
take the pressure programming

02:11:22.810 --> 02:11:24.710
off.

02:11:24.710 --> 02:11:26.200
It's also nice
to, if you've been

02:11:26.200 --> 02:11:28.480
lifting by yourself,
to get in there

02:11:28.480 --> 02:11:30.400
and lift with somebody else.

02:11:30.400 --> 02:11:35.170
It's also nice to now have
some social interaction.

02:11:35.170 --> 02:11:38.410
The gamification, the
group, the scoring stuff

02:11:38.410 --> 02:11:41.480
that happens in fitness
classes is very, very powerful.

02:11:41.480 --> 02:11:43.720
It tends to be
somewhat fleeting.

02:11:43.720 --> 02:11:45.530
So it won't last
for a long time,

02:11:45.530 --> 02:11:47.152
for some people, others it does.

02:11:47.152 --> 02:11:48.610
And so, if you
pepper this thing in

02:11:48.610 --> 02:11:51.520
and you know you're going
to join this activity class,

02:11:51.520 --> 02:11:56.120
even if it's not great and the
program design isn't perfect,

02:11:56.120 --> 02:11:58.220
it's fine for 10 weeks.

02:11:58.220 --> 02:12:00.130
In fact, you may
really, really enjoy it.

02:12:00.130 --> 02:12:04.030
And also, again, it gives you
something new to think about.

02:12:04.030 --> 02:12:05.195
Music is on.

02:12:05.195 --> 02:12:07.570
You're out of your house if
you're lifting at your house.

02:12:07.570 --> 02:12:09.153
You're in a different
part of the gym.

02:12:09.153 --> 02:12:10.670
The schedule is
a little tighter.

02:12:10.670 --> 02:12:12.670
So you can't just go work
out whenever you want.

02:12:12.670 --> 02:12:14.680
You've got to show up
when the class is going.

02:12:14.680 --> 02:12:17.830
And you'll probably find
that you just love it.

02:12:17.830 --> 02:12:20.178
You also get some
social interaction,

02:12:20.178 --> 02:12:22.720
which is something that's also
very important that we haven't

02:12:22.720 --> 02:12:24.970
really discussed yet, if
you're out playing basketball

02:12:24.970 --> 02:12:26.300
by yourself or whatnot.

02:12:26.300 --> 02:12:28.360
So this is just
another thing I'm

02:12:28.360 --> 02:12:31.210
trying to fold in that still
allows you to check off

02:12:31.210 --> 02:12:35.093
multiple boxes of things
that are healthy for you.

02:12:35.093 --> 02:12:37.510
You've had episodes on the
importance of social connection

02:12:37.510 --> 02:12:38.080
interaction.

02:12:38.080 --> 02:12:39.880
We talked about that
in the quad breakdown

02:12:39.880 --> 02:12:41.620
of making sure you
have relationship time

02:12:41.620 --> 02:12:42.495
and things like that.

02:12:42.495 --> 02:12:44.770
So throwing in a fitness
class and just doing,

02:12:44.770 --> 02:12:48.370
honestly, something quite
different is pretty fun.

02:12:48.370 --> 02:12:50.307
But then, still,
keeping two days a week

02:12:50.307 --> 02:12:52.390
where you're doing a
traditional strength training

02:12:52.390 --> 02:12:56.330
thing so you have some
quality control there.

02:12:56.330 --> 02:12:59.560
Lastly, you can also
then make sure you're

02:12:59.560 --> 02:13:02.710
hitting any specific movements
or muscle groups that

02:13:02.710 --> 02:13:03.800
are very important to you.

02:13:03.800 --> 02:13:06.382
So you don't get to control
that in your fitness class.

02:13:06.382 --> 02:13:07.840
But now you can at
least do the gym

02:13:07.840 --> 02:13:10.060
and make sure you hit
that muscle group that you

02:13:10.060 --> 02:13:11.510
have an interest in it.

02:13:11.510 --> 02:13:13.000
So now we're
feeling pretty good.

02:13:13.000 --> 02:13:14.950
We're rolling into the summer.

02:13:14.950 --> 02:13:16.720
We're pretty lean.

02:13:16.720 --> 02:13:19.210
We're getting out
in the sun a lot.

02:13:19.210 --> 02:13:21.040
We're bringing calories
down a little bit.

02:13:21.040 --> 02:13:23.248
And we probably are
feeling pretty happy.

02:13:23.248 --> 02:13:24.290
We're also not burnt out.

02:13:24.290 --> 02:13:26.200
We've done a lot of fun things.

02:13:26.200 --> 02:13:27.910
And we've checked
a lot of the boxes

02:13:27.910 --> 02:13:30.100
off for long-term development.

02:13:30.100 --> 02:13:35.120
We had a combination
of specificity

02:13:35.120 --> 02:13:36.380
with exercise selection.

02:13:36.380 --> 02:13:38.648
But we also folded in just
a little bit of variation

02:13:38.648 --> 02:13:40.940
so we don't have to worry
about overuse injury of doing

02:13:40.940 --> 02:13:44.270
the same machines, the same
lift, months, and months,

02:13:44.270 --> 02:13:47.210
and months, after months, and
slowly wearing down something

02:13:47.210 --> 02:13:49.942
if our technique isn't perfect.

02:13:49.942 --> 02:13:51.650
So now we're going to
go into our quarter

02:13:51.650 --> 02:13:53.858
three, which is the summer
months, basically, up here

02:13:53.858 --> 02:13:56.120
at least, July to September.

02:13:56.120 --> 02:13:57.710
We'll transition.

02:13:57.710 --> 02:14:01.280
It's been a while since
we've done some conditioning.

02:14:01.280 --> 02:14:04.310
So we may have lost a little
cardiorespiratory fitness.

02:14:04.310 --> 02:14:06.465
We may have-- not
feeling great anymore,

02:14:06.465 --> 02:14:09.090
maybe energy throughout the day
stuff is starting to leak down.

02:14:09.090 --> 02:14:09.890
So we're going to get in shape.

02:14:09.890 --> 02:14:11.630
We're going to push
our heart rate high.

02:14:11.630 --> 02:14:14.180
And we're going to bring
the calories back up.

02:14:14.180 --> 02:14:19.160
The summertime, 4th of July,
other holidays like this where

02:14:19.160 --> 02:14:21.020
eating is involved,
maybe you're going

02:14:21.020 --> 02:14:24.330
to sporting events
and things like that.

02:14:24.330 --> 02:14:29.940
Our sport choices is often
going to be outdoors.

02:14:29.940 --> 02:14:31.980
But in fact, what you'll
notice here is, is I've

02:14:31.980 --> 02:14:34.873
ramped the sport choice
up to twice a week.

02:14:34.873 --> 02:14:36.290
And in fact, I
would encourage you

02:14:36.290 --> 02:14:38.550
to do two different
types of exercise.

02:14:38.550 --> 02:14:40.340
And one of the primary
reasons for that

02:14:40.340 --> 02:14:43.070
is to spend more time outside.

02:14:43.070 --> 02:14:45.020
A challenge we often
see with people with

02:14:45.020 --> 02:14:47.390
exercises going, man,
it's so nice outside.

02:14:47.390 --> 02:14:50.240
I can't go sit in the
gym for 45 minutes.

02:14:50.240 --> 02:14:51.652
I don't have that
much free time.

02:14:51.652 --> 02:14:53.360
And then, Huberman's
over here telling me

02:14:53.360 --> 02:14:55.050
I need to get direct
sunlight more.

02:14:55.050 --> 02:14:56.300
How am I going to fit this in?

02:14:56.300 --> 02:14:57.920
Well, do your exercise outside.

02:14:57.920 --> 02:14:59.420
Enjoy it.

02:14:59.420 --> 02:15:01.040
Now, if you live
down here like us,

02:15:01.040 --> 02:15:03.130
you take sunshine for granted.

02:15:03.130 --> 02:15:04.880
But a lot of people I
know it's like, it's

02:15:04.880 --> 02:15:07.760
only nice for two and
1/2 months of the year.

02:15:07.760 --> 02:15:08.850
Get outside.

02:15:08.850 --> 02:15:11.330
So let's push more of
our fitness training

02:15:11.330 --> 02:15:13.040
to outdoor activities.

02:15:13.040 --> 02:15:16.250
These sports can
be intense or not.

02:15:16.250 --> 02:15:18.152
It could be go out
there and swim hard.

02:15:18.152 --> 02:15:18.860
Get in the ocean.

02:15:18.860 --> 02:15:21.277
You're going to do open ocean
swimming instead of swimming

02:15:21.277 --> 02:15:23.880
in the pool indoors, or
whatever the case may be.

02:15:23.880 --> 02:15:26.870
So we're going to give ourselves
more of a priority of being

02:15:26.870 --> 02:15:28.708
outside, looking--

02:15:28.708 --> 02:15:29.750
we've looked pretty good.

02:15:29.750 --> 02:15:31.010
We're a little tan.

02:15:31.010 --> 02:15:33.680
And we're enjoying all the
benefits of training outside

02:15:33.680 --> 02:15:35.360
and the lack of structure.

02:15:35.360 --> 02:15:38.210
Still, we have structured,
but not so specific

02:15:38.210 --> 02:15:40.970
like the machines and
the weights give us.

02:15:40.970 --> 02:15:43.220
Maybe even now we're
doing some track workouts.

02:15:43.220 --> 02:15:46.340
So now we can do something
like sprint the straightaways,

02:15:46.340 --> 02:15:47.520
walk the corners.

02:15:47.520 --> 02:15:49.520
And we don't have to,
again, do our conditioning

02:15:49.520 --> 02:15:53.180
on the same StairMaster, or
machine, or whatever we're on.

02:15:53.180 --> 02:15:55.160
So we're going to enjoy
some stuff like that.

02:15:55.160 --> 02:15:56.150
We're going to be athletic.

02:15:56.150 --> 02:15:56.870
We're going to run.

02:15:56.870 --> 02:15:57.703
We're going to move.

02:15:57.703 --> 02:15:59.180
We haven't talked
about that yet.

02:15:59.180 --> 02:16:02.195
Everything has really been
about structured exercise.

02:16:02.195 --> 02:16:04.070
Well, now we're going
to do some sprint work.

02:16:04.070 --> 02:16:05.820
We're going to get out
and see that, which

02:16:05.820 --> 02:16:08.107
is a really important human
quality that, I think,

02:16:08.107 --> 02:16:10.440
is important to not lose, is
actually ability to sprint.

02:16:10.440 --> 02:16:11.870
So we'll do that.

02:16:11.870 --> 02:16:14.840
And then we'll still make
sure we lift twice a week

02:16:14.840 --> 02:16:18.200
for the same reasons I talked
about in the previous phase.

02:16:18.200 --> 02:16:20.480
So we make sure we have
some quality control there.

02:16:20.480 --> 02:16:23.120
We maintain some of the muscle
that we built in the quarter

02:16:23.120 --> 02:16:23.870
before.

02:16:23.870 --> 02:16:25.730
We don't lose too much strength.

02:16:25.730 --> 02:16:29.690
There is very good literature
to suggest strength maintenance

02:16:29.690 --> 02:16:32.809
can be done in as little
as five sets per week

02:16:32.809 --> 02:16:36.351
for a very long time, really
up to eight-plus weeks

02:16:36.351 --> 02:16:37.309
if you do a little bit.

02:16:37.309 --> 02:16:39.080
So you're touching it enough
where you're not going

02:16:39.080 --> 02:16:40.205
to get really, really weak.

02:16:40.205 --> 02:16:43.850
But what you wouldn't want
to do is go 12 or 16 weeks

02:16:43.850 --> 02:16:45.145
where you lifted no weights.

02:16:45.145 --> 02:16:46.520
And maybe you got
in great shape,

02:16:46.520 --> 02:16:48.437
but you're going to feel
very weak after that.

02:16:48.437 --> 02:16:50.990
So maybe that number could
come down to one time a week

02:16:50.990 --> 02:16:52.605
if you really wanted it to.

02:16:52.605 --> 02:16:54.230
But one to two days
a week where you're

02:16:54.230 --> 02:16:56.629
lifting the big exercises,
the muscle groups

02:16:56.629 --> 02:16:59.180
and movements of interest,
and you're good to go.

02:16:59.180 --> 02:17:01.250
Then, lastly, we roll
into our final quarter,

02:17:01.250 --> 02:17:03.530
which is October to December.

02:17:03.530 --> 02:17:05.730
And we're going to really
get in great shape.

02:17:05.730 --> 02:17:08.270
The sun is starting
to come down.

02:17:08.270 --> 02:17:09.860
We're rolling into the holidays.

02:17:09.860 --> 02:17:11.120
Weather's getting worse.

02:17:11.120 --> 02:17:13.190
We may have other
outdoor activities

02:17:13.190 --> 02:17:16.340
we want to do, like, in my case,
you're going on a hunting trip,

02:17:16.340 --> 02:17:18.200
you have some
travel, conferences,

02:17:18.200 --> 02:17:19.709
whatever the case may be.

02:17:19.709 --> 02:17:22.400
And so, we're going to
choose an indoor sport.

02:17:22.400 --> 02:17:24.620
And I love combat sports.

02:17:24.620 --> 02:17:28.010
So the example I gave
earlier was jujitsu.

02:17:28.010 --> 02:17:30.469
Or maybe you just
transition your basketball

02:17:30.469 --> 02:17:32.480
to inside, or your
pickleball comes inside,

02:17:32.480 --> 02:17:34.730
or whatever it happens to be.

02:17:34.730 --> 02:17:37.430
And you're still going to
have that twice a week.

02:17:37.430 --> 02:17:40.070
And then, maybe instead of
the track workout outside,

02:17:40.070 --> 02:17:41.750
you do that same
workout indoors,

02:17:41.750 --> 02:17:46.010
now back on some sort of
machine or something like that.

02:17:46.010 --> 02:17:49.190
Our weights are actually
now down to once a week

02:17:49.190 --> 02:17:52.340
because we're really pushing
the pace on cardiovascular.

02:17:52.340 --> 02:17:55.010
We're doing once a week to
maintain it, to not lose,

02:17:55.010 --> 02:17:56.450
and get too far behind.

02:17:56.450 --> 02:17:59.000
But we really want to
bring up our VO2 max.

02:17:59.000 --> 02:18:02.600
We want to bring up our
efficiency, our cardiac output,

02:18:02.600 --> 02:18:03.770
and everything like that.

02:18:03.770 --> 02:18:06.260
And we're still going to
now walk twice a week so

02:18:06.260 --> 02:18:07.820
that we get something outside.

02:18:07.820 --> 02:18:09.320
And I talked about
why, again, it's

02:18:09.320 --> 02:18:12.968
nice to have that flexibility
of not having to train outside,

02:18:12.968 --> 02:18:15.260
because now you got to warm
up and do all those things.

02:18:15.260 --> 02:18:16.958
We just get out
and get a walk in.

02:18:16.958 --> 02:18:18.500
You still get the
outdoor experience.

02:18:18.500 --> 02:18:21.620
So we run through
that entire thing.

02:18:21.620 --> 02:18:23.420
And then you just start
back the next year.

02:18:23.420 --> 02:18:26.870
Ideally, again, at the
end of every quarter,

02:18:26.870 --> 02:18:28.490
you take a week off.

02:18:28.490 --> 02:18:30.299
Whether that is a
true full week off,

02:18:30.299 --> 02:18:31.703
which I'm fully supportive.

02:18:31.703 --> 02:18:33.620
I mean, friends, we're
only talking about four

02:18:33.620 --> 02:18:34.639
off weeks a year.

02:18:34.639 --> 02:18:37.430
That's absolutely fine.

02:18:37.430 --> 02:18:40.040
Or it could even be
a slight de-load week

02:18:40.040 --> 02:18:41.910
if you want it to be.

02:18:41.910 --> 02:18:44.510
So we shouldn't run into
too many issues of overuse.

02:18:44.510 --> 02:18:45.809
We have a lot of variety.

02:18:45.809 --> 02:18:47.389
We get a lot of
movement patterns

02:18:47.389 --> 02:18:52.020
in because we're mixing in sport
with machines and dumbbells.

02:18:52.020 --> 02:18:53.660
We're mixing in
social interaction.

02:18:53.660 --> 02:18:54.740
We're mixing in the sun.

02:18:54.740 --> 02:18:56.180
We're mixing in enjoyment.

02:18:56.180 --> 02:18:59.209
We're mixing in fat loss,
strength, hypertrophy,

02:18:59.209 --> 02:19:00.709
some cardiovascular endurance.

02:19:00.709 --> 02:19:03.049
We're mixing in
calories in high,

02:19:03.049 --> 02:19:05.150
we're mixing a little
bit of calories low.

02:19:05.150 --> 02:19:08.480
And we're trying to hit as many
of these nodes as possible.

02:19:08.480 --> 02:19:12.200
If you also wanted to cut each
one of these a little bit short

02:19:12.200 --> 02:19:15.559
and repeat your fitness testing
at the end of every quarter,

02:19:15.559 --> 02:19:16.520
you could.

02:19:16.520 --> 02:19:20.150
I would probably recommend
doing it at least once a year.

02:19:20.150 --> 02:19:24.620
Perhaps doing it maybe the
third week of December or so.

02:19:24.620 --> 02:19:25.993
So you run that testing.

02:19:25.993 --> 02:19:27.410
That's your last
week of training.

02:19:27.410 --> 02:19:29.840
Then you get to go on
your vacation break.

02:19:29.840 --> 02:19:31.740
You come back at the
beginning of the year.

02:19:31.740 --> 02:19:34.853
You've got new goals,
new targets and you go.

02:19:34.853 --> 02:19:37.520
If you want to repeat it twice a
year, do the same sort of thing

02:19:37.520 --> 02:19:38.990
at the end of June.

02:19:38.990 --> 02:19:39.709
It's fine.

02:19:39.709 --> 02:19:42.799
I know I laid these out as
quarters, which is generally 12

02:19:42.799 --> 02:19:45.292
weeks, with one back-off week.

02:19:45.292 --> 02:19:47.750
But if you wanted to make it
nine weeks and a testing week,

02:19:47.750 --> 02:19:50.180
and then a back-off,
or 10 weeks, it's fine.

02:19:50.180 --> 02:19:51.080
It's close enough.

02:19:51.080 --> 02:19:53.960
So the last little
thing I want to say

02:19:53.960 --> 02:19:57.483
is, let's assume you're
doing the 12 weeks.

02:19:57.483 --> 02:19:59.150
And you're going to
have a back-off week

02:19:59.150 --> 02:20:00.800
at the end of the 12 weeks.

02:20:00.800 --> 02:20:03.620
I would actually still then
recommend having at least one

02:20:03.620 --> 02:20:06.770
back-off week halfway through.

02:20:06.770 --> 02:20:07.970
So it would look like this.

02:20:07.970 --> 02:20:11.100
Five weeks hard where
you're progressing.

02:20:11.100 --> 02:20:13.453
You're going up, up, up, up, up.

02:20:13.453 --> 02:20:15.620
Every week you're either
increasing volume intensity

02:20:15.620 --> 02:20:17.780
like we talked about
a few minutes ago.

02:20:17.780 --> 02:20:20.750
Then week six, de-load.

02:20:20.750 --> 02:20:23.180
Go down to 70% volume intensity.

02:20:23.180 --> 02:20:24.440
Come back.

02:20:24.440 --> 02:20:26.540
Go hard for five more weeks.

02:20:26.540 --> 02:20:31.400
And now, week 12 is your true
off week where you, again,

02:20:31.400 --> 02:20:32.750
take the whole thing off.

02:20:32.750 --> 02:20:35.540
If you do that, you now
have four weeks a year

02:20:35.540 --> 02:20:37.520
where you're totally off.

02:20:37.520 --> 02:20:41.090
You have four weeks a year where
you're really backing down.

02:20:41.090 --> 02:20:43.497
And you just have
five-week segments

02:20:43.497 --> 02:20:45.080
all year round where
you're just going

02:20:45.080 --> 02:20:46.950
to push it hard for five weeks.

02:20:46.950 --> 02:20:48.440
You're going to get a break.

02:20:48.440 --> 02:20:49.400
You're going to
reset, and you're

02:20:49.400 --> 02:20:50.775
going to transition
a little bit.

02:20:50.775 --> 02:20:53.150
Now, as I started this
conversation off with,

02:20:53.150 --> 02:20:56.930
there are many ways you could
structure your training program

02:20:56.930 --> 02:20:59.510
throughout the year and hit
those primary goals we talked

02:20:59.510 --> 02:21:03.470
about of looking fantastic,
feeling amazing, and being

02:21:03.470 --> 02:21:06.110
able to do that
your entire life.

02:21:06.110 --> 02:21:10.010
All I can tell you, though,
is I know this model works.

02:21:10.010 --> 02:21:12.410
Because we've done this
a lot with our clients

02:21:12.410 --> 02:21:14.330
in our rapid health
optimization program.

02:21:14.330 --> 02:21:17.210
And this spans everything
from 25-year-old folks

02:21:17.210 --> 02:21:19.940
who are competing in
the Boston Marathon

02:21:19.940 --> 02:21:23.270
to a lot of individuals who
have never exercised before,

02:21:23.270 --> 02:21:25.700
who maybe have done a
little bit of exercise.

02:21:25.700 --> 02:21:28.460
In fact, it's quite literally
all three of the buckets

02:21:28.460 --> 02:21:29.480
you laid out.

02:21:29.480 --> 02:21:31.850
We've had clients in
all of those areas,

02:21:31.850 --> 02:21:35.000
both men and women,
young and old.

02:21:35.000 --> 02:21:38.450
And we've had a tremendous
amount of success transforming

02:21:38.450 --> 02:21:42.593
their lives using a very similar
model to what I just laid out.

02:21:42.593 --> 02:21:44.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I find
that overall structure

02:21:44.510 --> 02:21:47.240
to be immensely informative.

02:21:47.240 --> 02:21:50.970
And I'll tell you why in the
context of a number of examples

02:21:50.970 --> 02:21:54.120
with myself, although that's
the least important of them,

02:21:54.120 --> 02:21:58.250
frankly, but examples of family
members of mine and friends

02:21:58.250 --> 02:22:00.320
of mine who've
undertaken consistent

02:22:00.320 --> 02:22:03.380
exercise training
programs but that haven't

02:22:03.380 --> 02:22:05.300
varied the program so much.

02:22:05.300 --> 02:22:08.330
And here, again, I
think of the person

02:22:08.330 --> 02:22:10.350
who really loves to swim.

02:22:10.350 --> 02:22:13.220
They have a low barrier of entry
to the pool or to the ocean.

02:22:13.220 --> 02:22:14.660
They love being in the water.

02:22:14.660 --> 02:22:15.747
I am not one such person.

02:22:15.747 --> 02:22:17.330
I like being in the
water, but I don't

02:22:17.330 --> 02:22:19.580
motivate to drive to the
pool or to bike to the pool

02:22:19.580 --> 02:22:21.080
or to get into the
ocean that often.

02:22:21.080 --> 02:22:22.640
Once I do it, I enjoy it.

02:22:22.640 --> 02:22:24.980
But for me it's running
and lifting weights.

02:22:24.980 --> 02:22:27.530
And it has been for
a very long time.

02:22:27.530 --> 02:22:29.620
I have a family member,
close family member,

02:22:29.620 --> 02:22:32.260
who doesn't really like,
quote unquote, exercise,

02:22:32.260 --> 02:22:33.970
but loves dance and dancing.

02:22:33.970 --> 02:22:36.935
Going out dancing, yes, but
dance classes in particular.

02:22:36.935 --> 02:22:37.810
ANDY GALPIN: Amazing.

02:22:37.810 --> 02:22:39.227
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Really enjoys it.

02:22:39.227 --> 02:22:42.520
Loves to be distracted from the
fact that she's doing exercise

02:22:42.520 --> 02:22:43.660
and just really enjoys it.

02:22:43.660 --> 02:22:45.400
And actually is a
very good dancer

02:22:45.400 --> 02:22:48.330
despite the fact that
she's related to me.

02:22:48.330 --> 02:22:49.180
And on and on.

02:22:49.180 --> 02:22:51.240
There are many
examples, I think,

02:22:51.240 --> 02:22:54.300
of folks that fall into the
different bins that we talked

02:22:54.300 --> 02:22:57.480
about earlier but that also
tend to default towards a given

02:22:57.480 --> 02:23:00.690
structure of training
one way and doing that

02:23:00.690 --> 02:23:01.920
throughout the year.

02:23:01.920 --> 02:23:04.200
I can tell you right
now that I'm personally

02:23:04.200 --> 02:23:06.360
going to modify my
schedule according

02:23:06.360 --> 02:23:09.258
to this four quarters per year.

02:23:09.258 --> 02:23:10.800
It actually works
because I've mostly

02:23:10.800 --> 02:23:13.200
been on the quarter system in
academics for a very long time.

02:23:13.200 --> 02:23:15.570
I was at a university that
had a semester system once.

02:23:15.570 --> 02:23:17.460
But this quarter system
is actually the one

02:23:17.460 --> 02:23:19.480
that we follow academically.

02:23:19.480 --> 02:23:22.480
So that's one reason why
it's a natural fit for me.

02:23:22.480 --> 02:23:25.680
I confess that I
typically don't vary up

02:23:25.680 --> 02:23:31.230
the proportions of endurance
to resistance training.

02:23:31.230 --> 02:23:33.870
I tend to keep those about
three and three across the week.

02:23:33.870 --> 02:23:36.180
Three resistance
training sessions, three,

02:23:36.180 --> 02:23:37.650
let's call them cardio sessions.

02:23:37.650 --> 02:23:40.800
But each one designed to
achieve a different adaptation.

02:23:40.800 --> 02:23:43.260
And I've now altered
those even further

02:23:43.260 --> 02:23:46.470
based on your recommendations
in this episode

02:23:46.470 --> 02:23:47.760
and previous episodes.

02:23:47.760 --> 02:23:54.140
But what I have not done is
to really think about de-load

02:23:54.140 --> 02:23:57.050
and to really stick
to the structure

02:23:57.050 --> 02:24:00.140
that I set out to
accomplish across the year.

02:24:00.140 --> 02:24:02.900
On the topic of de-load,
for me, the de-load

02:24:02.900 --> 02:24:06.950
has been when I get overwhelmed
with work, or I've gotten sick.

02:24:06.950 --> 02:24:08.450
I don't tend to get
sick that often.

02:24:08.450 --> 02:24:10.880
But every once in a
while I get knocked back

02:24:10.880 --> 02:24:12.620
with a cold or a flu.

02:24:12.620 --> 02:24:14.540
Once every three or
four years I seem

02:24:14.540 --> 02:24:17.010
to really get hammered with
a fever-inducing something

02:24:17.010 --> 02:24:17.510
or other.

02:24:17.510 --> 02:24:19.340
And then I'm bedridden
for a couple of days.

02:24:19.340 --> 02:24:20.340
And then I'm back at it.

02:24:20.340 --> 02:24:22.310
And I tend to come
back rather slowly.

02:24:22.310 --> 02:24:24.530
And that tends to
be my week off.

02:24:24.530 --> 02:24:27.470
But I'm beginning to
wonder whether or not

02:24:27.470 --> 02:24:31.370
part of the reason I hit those
streaks of being overwhelmed

02:24:31.370 --> 02:24:35.010
by sickness or by stress
is that I have not

02:24:35.010 --> 02:24:36.130
done a de-load period.

02:24:36.130 --> 02:24:38.760
So one of the things that I'm
going to immediately implement

02:24:38.760 --> 02:24:42.240
is a periodic de-load
according to the program

02:24:42.240 --> 02:24:43.200
that you described.

02:24:43.200 --> 02:24:47.730
And I'm also going to start
matching my specific goals

02:24:47.730 --> 02:24:50.130
for each quarter
with time of year.

02:24:50.130 --> 02:24:51.580
I don't think I've done that.

02:24:51.580 --> 02:24:54.048
And it's not because
I live in California.

02:24:54.048 --> 02:24:56.340
And by the way, folks, there
are temperature variations

02:24:56.340 --> 02:24:59.750
and amount of light across the
day variations in California

02:24:59.750 --> 02:25:01.500
as well, although they
are not as dramatic

02:25:01.500 --> 02:25:02.700
as they would be near the
North Pole, for instance.

02:25:02.700 --> 02:25:03.420
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.
They're pretty moderate.

02:25:03.420 --> 02:25:03.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

02:25:03.960 --> 02:25:06.293
But of course, some of the
listeners are at the equator.

02:25:06.293 --> 02:25:08.580
So they have the opposite issue.

02:25:08.580 --> 02:25:11.100
In any event, I'm
definitely going to do that.

02:25:11.100 --> 02:25:14.640
I'm going to start incorporating
regular de-load periods.

02:25:14.640 --> 02:25:20.460
And I am going to be
very dedicated, very

02:25:20.460 --> 02:25:23.490
disciplined about sticking
to a program for three

02:25:23.490 --> 02:25:25.200
months devoted mainly
to hypertrophy,

02:25:25.200 --> 02:25:27.540
then a three-month program
devoted to fat loss,

02:25:27.540 --> 02:25:31.860
then a program devoted
to aerobic output,

02:25:31.860 --> 02:25:34.470
and then one devoted
to endurance.

02:25:34.470 --> 02:25:36.810
Although I must say,
it's very tempting for me

02:25:36.810 --> 02:25:39.150
to do a very specific
strength-dedicated portion.

02:25:39.150 --> 02:25:39.900
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

02:25:39.900 --> 02:25:42.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Because I don't
tend to be particularly strong.

02:25:42.060 --> 02:25:43.977
I'm not weak, but I'm
not particularly strong.

02:25:43.977 --> 02:25:46.590
So I might consult with
you as to how I could

02:25:46.590 --> 02:25:48.960
vary endurance and strength.

02:25:48.960 --> 02:25:52.440
In any event, I love the
idea of a macro-structure.

02:25:52.440 --> 02:25:56.610
And I love the idea of
de-loads in anticipation

02:25:56.610 --> 02:26:00.090
of being able to go further
in the long-run in terms

02:26:00.090 --> 02:26:01.500
of results.

02:26:01.500 --> 02:26:03.600
I'm hoping this next
year, because we're just

02:26:03.600 --> 02:26:06.510
on the cusp of a new year, will
be the first year in which I

02:26:06.510 --> 02:26:10.230
don't find myself getting
some bug, or virus,

02:26:10.230 --> 02:26:12.750
or whatever it happens
to be from time to time

02:26:12.750 --> 02:26:16.320
and having to back off on
training for that reason.

02:26:16.320 --> 02:26:18.390
And that prompts a question.

02:26:18.390 --> 02:26:20.550
And it's something
that I want to get

02:26:20.550 --> 02:26:24.510
into in more detail with you
when you describe recovery--

02:26:24.510 --> 02:26:26.070
an upcoming episode.

02:26:26.070 --> 02:26:28.440
But a couple of quick questions.

02:26:28.440 --> 02:26:30.540
Maybe there are some
short or short-ish answers

02:26:30.540 --> 02:26:32.450
you could provide.

02:26:32.450 --> 02:26:35.360
If I'm not feeling
well, like I really

02:26:35.360 --> 02:26:36.770
had a poor night's sleep.

02:26:36.770 --> 02:26:41.500
Maybe just two to four hours
of sleep for whatever reason.

02:26:41.500 --> 02:26:42.610
Train or don't train?

02:26:42.610 --> 02:26:44.630
That's the first question.

02:26:44.630 --> 02:26:46.420
Second question
is, if I'm starting

02:26:46.420 --> 02:26:48.988
to feel a little bit
of a throat tickle,

02:26:48.988 --> 02:26:51.280
and I'm in that phase of
denial, like I don't get sick,

02:26:51.280 --> 02:26:52.155
I'm not getting sick.

02:26:52.155 --> 02:26:56.230
And would I be better off
bundling up some hot liquids,

02:26:56.230 --> 02:26:59.530
getting into bed, sleeping
in a little bit more,

02:26:59.530 --> 02:27:02.050
et cetera, and protecting
myself against that,

02:27:02.050 --> 02:27:04.000
or would I be
better off training?

02:27:04.000 --> 02:27:06.340
And if I've-- and then
the third question is--

02:27:06.340 --> 02:27:10.360
if I've already succumbed to a
bug, but it's not a severe bug.

02:27:10.360 --> 02:27:12.860
I don't have elevated
body temperature.

02:27:12.860 --> 02:27:13.870
So no fever.

02:27:13.870 --> 02:27:16.180
I'm not hacking up,
not productive cough

02:27:16.180 --> 02:27:17.230
or anything like that.

02:27:17.230 --> 02:27:19.540
But I'm feeling just
kind of not well,

02:27:19.540 --> 02:27:22.600
head cold-ish sort of
thing comes to mind,

02:27:22.600 --> 02:27:26.590
and it's not seasonal
allergies, train or don't train,

02:27:26.590 --> 02:27:30.010
leaving aside the
point of whether or not

02:27:30.010 --> 02:27:32.170
I'm in a position to
get anyone else sick.

02:27:32.170 --> 02:27:33.670
Because obviously
that's a bad idea.

02:27:33.670 --> 02:27:34.628
ANDY GALPIN: Of course.

02:27:34.628 --> 02:27:38.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So lack of
sleep, I would say, 30% to 40%

02:27:38.770 --> 02:27:41.290
of one's typical sleep
the previous night,

02:27:41.290 --> 02:27:42.520
train or no train?

02:27:42.520 --> 02:27:45.920
Starting to feel like
one might be getting ill.

02:27:45.920 --> 02:27:49.870
And then the third category is
coming back from being sick.

02:27:49.870 --> 02:27:50.620
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:27:50.620 --> 02:27:51.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Thanks.

02:27:51.160 --> 02:27:52.510
And sorry for the
extended question,

02:27:52.510 --> 02:27:54.070
but I want to make sure there
was enough detail there,

02:27:54.070 --> 02:27:55.870
because I think these are
three common scenarios.

02:27:55.870 --> 02:27:57.287
ANDY GALPIN: We
are going to cover

02:27:57.287 --> 02:27:59.980
that in the recovery
conversation

02:27:59.980 --> 02:28:02.170
that's next in detail.

02:28:02.170 --> 02:28:04.450
And I will give you very
specific guidelines.

02:28:04.450 --> 02:28:06.430
And we'll have plenty
of time to go into that.

02:28:06.430 --> 02:28:09.700
The quick answer
is, it comes back

02:28:09.700 --> 02:28:11.560
to what phase of
training you're in.

02:28:11.560 --> 02:28:14.290
Now, to walk through
each scenario.

02:28:14.290 --> 02:28:16.990
If it is a crummy
night of sleep,

02:28:16.990 --> 02:28:20.860
and I am in a phase of
training in which we are trying

02:28:20.860 --> 02:28:25.210
to cause adaptation, I have a
lot of space in my schedule,

02:28:25.210 --> 02:28:30.100
and I'm really using this
time to make progress

02:28:30.100 --> 02:28:33.490
because I know coming up
soon my schedule will change

02:28:33.490 --> 02:28:36.250
and my time to
train will go down.

02:28:36.250 --> 02:28:37.780
I'm still training.

02:28:37.780 --> 02:28:39.880
I might use a bunch
of tricks that we

02:28:39.880 --> 02:28:42.190
have for feeling
better instantaneously.

02:28:42.190 --> 02:28:43.630
We call these little hacks.

02:28:43.630 --> 02:28:45.340
These are acute hacks.

02:28:45.340 --> 02:28:47.230
These are not chronic hacks.

02:28:47.230 --> 02:28:49.130
I'm going to push the pace.

02:28:49.130 --> 02:28:52.090
If it is really close
to a de-load week.

02:28:52.090 --> 02:28:55.720
Say it's Wednesday and I
start my de-load next week.

02:28:55.720 --> 02:28:59.650
Or this is not one
bad night of sleep,

02:28:59.650 --> 02:29:03.730
this has been four bad nights
of sleep in the last five days.

02:29:03.730 --> 02:29:05.710
This has been six
kind of crummy nights

02:29:05.710 --> 02:29:07.930
over the course
of the last nine,

02:29:07.930 --> 02:29:10.000
and you're starting to
see a larger pattern,

02:29:10.000 --> 02:29:11.407
then that's a different answer.

02:29:11.407 --> 02:29:13.240
So the question we're
going to ask ourselves

02:29:13.240 --> 02:29:14.950
is, is this acute?

02:29:14.950 --> 02:29:20.200
Or is this a tendency, or
actually a chronic thing?

02:29:20.200 --> 02:29:23.440
If it's acute, and
we're close, we're

02:29:23.440 --> 02:29:24.760
going to train through it.

02:29:24.760 --> 02:29:29.440
If it's acute and this is
not a phase of training

02:29:29.440 --> 02:29:32.020
when we're trying to
really push, then maybe we

02:29:32.020 --> 02:29:33.220
back off a little bit.

02:29:33.220 --> 02:29:36.230
If it's the opposite
though, we need

02:29:36.230 --> 02:29:38.540
to probably make some
changes and give ourselves

02:29:38.540 --> 02:29:39.500
some recovery.

02:29:39.500 --> 02:29:44.570
This may include anything from
a moderate training session,

02:29:44.570 --> 02:29:48.440
maybe I'm going to go in the
sauna and sit through that,

02:29:48.440 --> 02:29:51.750
and then do some breathing
drills and some mobility stuff.

02:29:51.750 --> 02:29:52.250
Great.

02:29:52.250 --> 02:29:57.540
Maybe I'm going to go to the gym
and ride the bike at 50% heart

02:29:57.540 --> 02:29:58.040
rate.

02:29:58.040 --> 02:29:59.420
Something restorative like that.

02:29:59.420 --> 02:30:02.240
Gives you a little bit of energy
but doesn't beat you down.

02:30:02.240 --> 02:30:04.040
That's probably
where we're learning.

02:30:04.040 --> 02:30:07.460
If you're feeling sick
and you think it's coming,

02:30:07.460 --> 02:30:09.080
I'm probably going
to do option two

02:30:09.080 --> 02:30:11.720
as well, which is some sort
of restorative training.

02:30:11.720 --> 02:30:13.220
So again, this tends
to be moderate.

02:30:13.220 --> 02:30:14.780
Could be weights.

02:30:14.780 --> 02:30:15.920
Could be any of the stuff.

02:30:15.920 --> 02:30:18.212
Maybe you're going to go out
for your swim or whatever.

02:30:18.212 --> 02:30:20.850
But we're not going to push
past probably about 70%.

02:30:20.850 --> 02:30:24.350
We can absolutely
induce immunosuppression

02:30:24.350 --> 02:30:25.580
with excessive training.

02:30:25.580 --> 02:30:28.940
And so, you may want
to walk out of that.

02:30:28.940 --> 02:30:32.390
The last case, which was, I
think, phase number three,

02:30:32.390 --> 02:30:35.630
you said there, which is, I got
a pretty gnarly cold right now.

02:30:35.630 --> 02:30:36.667
Am I going to train?

02:30:36.667 --> 02:30:39.000
Most of the time for most
people, I'm just going to say,

02:30:39.000 --> 02:30:40.500
just shut it down.

02:30:40.500 --> 02:30:41.600
Get out of there.

02:30:41.600 --> 02:30:44.630
If you're not going to be able
to get productivity done there,

02:30:44.630 --> 02:30:47.930
you may be better off either
going and sleeping, catching up

02:30:47.930 --> 02:30:49.342
on work doing other stuff.

02:30:49.342 --> 02:30:51.800
So that the next time you go
to train you don't feel behind

02:30:51.800 --> 02:30:53.870
and we can give a good
solid effort for it.

02:30:53.870 --> 02:30:54.370
I

02:30:54.370 --> 02:30:56.910
Know other people who will
train right through it.

02:30:56.910 --> 02:30:59.380
I tend to not, to
be totally frank.

02:30:59.380 --> 02:31:01.880
If I'm feeling kind of junky,
I'm really not going to train.

02:31:01.880 --> 02:31:06.680
I may actually probably do
some hot water immersion.

02:31:06.680 --> 02:31:08.990
So bath, Jacuzzi,
things like that.

02:31:08.990 --> 02:31:11.508
I actually like those
better than I like sauna.

02:31:11.508 --> 02:31:12.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If one is ill?

02:31:12.800 --> 02:31:14.600
Or you just like them better
than the sauna generally?

02:31:14.600 --> 02:31:15.650
ANDY GALPIN: Oh, both, actually.

02:31:15.650 --> 02:31:16.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh my.

02:31:16.190 --> 02:31:16.940
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:31:16.940 --> 02:31:20.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: First
person I've ever met or come

02:31:20.000 --> 02:31:22.490
on this podcast to say
you like baths and Jacuzzi

02:31:22.490 --> 02:31:23.210
more than sauna.

02:31:23.210 --> 02:31:25.550
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, absolutely.

02:31:25.550 --> 02:31:26.840
I may even do some ice.

02:31:26.840 --> 02:31:28.010
Probably not a ton though.

02:31:28.010 --> 02:31:29.635
Because you've got
to be careful there.

02:31:29.635 --> 02:31:30.740
That's a big stressor.

02:31:30.740 --> 02:31:33.240
And if you're already
over the line,

02:31:33.240 --> 02:31:34.850
you may be adding a pass there.

02:31:34.850 --> 02:31:36.950
Or I may go sleep.

02:31:36.950 --> 02:31:39.740
If I'm feeling very,
very, very sleepy,

02:31:39.740 --> 02:31:41.990
and sometimes depending on
what kind of a bug you get,

02:31:41.990 --> 02:31:42.657
that can happen.

02:31:42.657 --> 02:31:43.700
I will just sleep.

02:31:43.700 --> 02:31:45.710
And that might be the
best choice you have.

02:31:45.710 --> 02:31:51.070
If that means you kick the
cold half a day earlier,

02:31:51.070 --> 02:31:53.440
then you just won
in the aggregate.

02:31:53.440 --> 02:31:57.345
So those are probably--

02:31:57.345 --> 02:31:59.220
it's a little bit of
insight of the algorithm

02:31:59.220 --> 02:32:00.900
that I'm running
with those things.

02:32:00.900 --> 02:32:02.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Those are
highly informative answers.

02:32:02.830 --> 02:32:03.360
Thank you.

02:32:03.360 --> 02:32:06.810
And I look forward to our
discussion about recovery

02:32:06.810 --> 02:32:10.737
so that we can go into even
more depth on how to recover.

02:32:10.737 --> 02:32:12.570
ANDY GALPIN: The last
thing I do want to say

02:32:12.570 --> 02:32:17.040
here is going back to
our quarter system.

02:32:17.040 --> 02:32:19.620
The examples I gave
with the bulking up,

02:32:19.620 --> 02:32:23.160
losing fat, and then
getting into better fitness

02:32:23.160 --> 02:32:24.900
and cardiovascular
fitness at the end,

02:32:24.900 --> 02:32:27.240
those were just samples.

02:32:27.240 --> 02:32:29.970
Friends, please don't
take that literally.

02:32:29.970 --> 02:32:32.070
If you want to
emphasize strength more,

02:32:32.070 --> 02:32:33.280
put in some more strength.

02:32:33.280 --> 02:32:35.730
If you want to emphasize a
different one of our nine

02:32:35.730 --> 02:32:37.080
adaptations, great.

02:32:37.080 --> 02:32:37.680
Do that too.

02:32:37.680 --> 02:32:42.840
If you're somebody who has
a lot of body fat to lose,

02:32:42.840 --> 02:32:45.450
then maybe put that for
two consecutive sessions,

02:32:45.450 --> 02:32:46.530
or every other.

02:32:46.530 --> 02:32:47.970
You can modify them.

02:32:47.970 --> 02:32:51.570
We've talked about nine very
specific training adaptations

02:32:51.570 --> 02:32:53.490
as well as in fat loss.

02:32:53.490 --> 02:32:55.890
I only gave you
four, which is just

02:32:55.890 --> 02:32:58.830
meant to be a sample that
you can roll in or out.

02:32:58.830 --> 02:33:03.300
But use those priorities to
adjust that system according

02:33:03.300 --> 02:33:06.780
to what is important
for you now, five years,

02:33:06.780 --> 02:33:10.000
and then 45 years down the
line for whatever that may be.

02:33:10.000 --> 02:33:12.810
So you are absolutely
free to modify the order.

02:33:12.810 --> 02:33:15.630
You're absolutely free to
modify the primary outcome.

02:33:15.630 --> 02:33:18.930
And then, adjust the
specifics within each quarter

02:33:18.930 --> 02:33:24.390
based upon what is needed to
do to optimize that outcome.

02:33:24.390 --> 02:33:27.750
I think maybe one
more tool we can

02:33:27.750 --> 02:33:31.980
offer people is maybe giving
the individual week a little bit

02:33:31.980 --> 02:33:32.890
more structure.

02:33:32.890 --> 02:33:35.850
So the system I laid
out is month by month.

02:33:35.850 --> 02:33:39.630
And maybe we can lay out,
say, a three-day a week

02:33:39.630 --> 02:33:42.630
workout program and a
four-day a week program.

02:33:42.630 --> 02:33:45.960
That would still hit some of the
same well-rounded adaptations.

02:33:45.960 --> 02:33:50.300
That probably covers maybe
not individualized per bucket

02:33:50.300 --> 02:33:51.300
that we've talked about.

02:33:51.300 --> 02:33:53.820
A, B, and C, but
it's going to cover

02:33:53.820 --> 02:33:57.630
75%, 80% of what we'll need
to occur in all three buckets.

02:33:57.630 --> 02:34:01.710
And then, you can use that last
25% for your individual goal

02:34:01.710 --> 02:34:02.670
or specialization.

02:34:02.670 --> 02:34:04.292
So maybe we can
jump into that next.

02:34:04.292 --> 02:34:05.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great.

02:34:05.250 --> 02:34:06.090
Let's hear it.

02:34:06.090 --> 02:34:07.465
ANDY GALPIN: The
first one I want

02:34:07.465 --> 02:34:10.440
to give you is just a
basic three-day split.

02:34:10.440 --> 02:34:11.880
That, again, same idea.

02:34:11.880 --> 02:34:13.860
It's a well-rounded
exercise program.

02:34:13.860 --> 02:34:17.160
I actually wrote this
all in an article

02:34:17.160 --> 02:34:19.213
that is on XPT's website.

02:34:19.213 --> 02:34:20.880
So perhaps we can
link directly to that.

02:34:20.880 --> 02:34:22.713
I will just jump you
straight to the answer.

02:34:22.713 --> 02:34:25.350
You can read more about why
and details in that article

02:34:25.350 --> 02:34:25.960
if you'd like.

02:34:25.960 --> 02:34:29.370
But this is day one,
day two, day three.

02:34:29.370 --> 02:34:32.550
You could do these days
where you split them up,

02:34:32.550 --> 02:34:34.590
actually having, say,
24 hours in between,

02:34:34.590 --> 02:34:36.210
or you could do
these back to back.

02:34:36.210 --> 02:34:38.400
It doesn't necessarily matter.

02:34:38.400 --> 02:34:40.320
In this particular
case, say, day one

02:34:40.320 --> 02:34:43.623
you would start off and do a
little bit of speed and power.

02:34:43.623 --> 02:34:45.540
And then you may finish
that with a little bit

02:34:45.540 --> 02:34:46.630
of hypertrophy.

02:34:46.630 --> 02:34:49.380
Now, if you want to gain
more speed and power

02:34:49.380 --> 02:34:50.820
you just do more of it.

02:34:50.820 --> 02:34:53.070
If you want to maybe just
do a little bit to touch it,

02:34:53.070 --> 02:34:54.737
and you really want
to gain some muscle,

02:34:54.737 --> 02:34:56.400
you would do more of
an emphasis there.

02:34:56.400 --> 02:34:58.830
So the template
can stay the same,

02:34:58.830 --> 02:35:01.890
and you would just increase the
amount of either adaptation,

02:35:01.890 --> 02:35:04.080
the speed and power
stuff, or the hypertrophy,

02:35:04.080 --> 02:35:07.500
based on how high it is
in your priority list.

02:35:07.500 --> 02:35:09.630
Those are combined together
because, as we talked

02:35:09.630 --> 02:35:11.172
about earlier, they
don't necessarily

02:35:11.172 --> 02:35:12.300
interfere with each other.

02:35:12.300 --> 02:35:15.248
You would do the speed
and power stuff first

02:35:15.248 --> 02:35:17.040
because it wouldn't
hamper the hypertrophy.

02:35:17.040 --> 02:35:19.230
If you did the hypertrophy
first in that workout,

02:35:19.230 --> 02:35:21.780
it would probably compromise
your speed and power.

02:35:21.780 --> 02:35:24.055
And in that case, you
would actually not

02:35:24.055 --> 02:35:25.180
be getting your adaptation.

02:35:25.180 --> 02:35:27.480
So day one you do that.

02:35:27.480 --> 02:35:30.450
And that could be a 20-minute
workout total, or a two and 1/2

02:35:30.450 --> 02:35:32.610
hour, up to you.

02:35:32.610 --> 02:35:35.487
Then you would come back maybe
the next day or two days later,

02:35:35.487 --> 02:35:36.570
whatever you'd like to do.

02:35:36.570 --> 02:35:38.460
In your second day
of exercise, you

02:35:38.460 --> 02:35:42.600
would start off with a
pure strength protocol.

02:35:42.600 --> 02:35:44.850
And you would finish that
with what I'm calling just

02:35:44.850 --> 02:35:46.090
a higher heart rate.

02:35:46.090 --> 02:35:49.330
So this could be something like
our anaerobic capacity stuff.

02:35:49.330 --> 02:35:51.600
It could be the
aerobic capacity,

02:35:51.600 --> 02:35:54.360
something where you're getting
up to close to high heart rate.

02:35:54.360 --> 02:35:55.950
It could be those
20-second bursts.

02:35:55.950 --> 02:35:58.320
It could be a 90-second burst,
five-minute mile repeats,

02:35:58.320 --> 02:35:59.400
anything you like.

02:35:59.400 --> 02:36:01.080
You can just plug
and play this in.

02:36:01.080 --> 02:36:03.120
You're getting to
a spot now where

02:36:03.120 --> 02:36:04.500
you've had a little
bit of speed,

02:36:04.500 --> 02:36:06.750
a little bit of strength, a
little bit of hypertrophy,

02:36:06.750 --> 02:36:08.375
and you've touched
the high heart rate.

02:36:08.375 --> 02:36:11.430
So we've checked off most of the
boxes already in two sessions.

02:36:11.430 --> 02:36:15.210
Our last session then would
be more of a steady state

02:36:15.210 --> 02:36:16.740
long duration endurance.

02:36:16.740 --> 02:36:19.470
And so, a three-day
week split like that

02:36:19.470 --> 02:36:22.548
is going to be a pretty nice
setup for the average person.

02:36:22.548 --> 02:36:24.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
this could be a Monday,

02:36:24.340 --> 02:36:26.520
Wednesday, Friday, Tuesday,
Thursday, Saturday.

02:36:26.520 --> 02:36:28.800
What's happening on
the intervening days?

02:36:28.800 --> 02:36:30.840
ANDY GALPIN: Totally off
if you want it to be.

02:36:30.840 --> 02:36:33.300
So I set this up as the
best I can give you,

02:36:33.300 --> 02:36:34.900
Andy, is three days.

02:36:34.900 --> 02:36:35.400
Great.

02:36:35.400 --> 02:36:37.780
If you have more, we could
certainly improve it.

02:36:37.780 --> 02:36:39.540
But this was my
worst case scenario.

02:36:39.540 --> 02:36:41.550
I've got other
things in my life.

02:36:41.550 --> 02:36:43.912
The most I could do for
exercise is three days a week.

02:36:43.912 --> 02:36:45.870
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
given that it's three days

02:36:45.870 --> 02:36:47.070
per week, how long--

02:36:47.070 --> 02:36:49.938
approximately how long each of
these workouts going to last?

02:36:49.938 --> 02:36:52.480
ANDY GALPIN: I would do a whole
body exercises for almost all

02:36:52.480 --> 02:36:53.350
that.

02:36:53.350 --> 02:36:54.880
I would do your full body parts.

02:36:54.880 --> 02:36:56.672
And I think you could
certainly finish that

02:36:56.672 --> 02:36:58.330
in 45 minutes of work time.

02:36:58.330 --> 02:37:01.210
A little bit of time to warm
up, some down-regulation

02:37:01.210 --> 02:37:01.840
at the end.

02:37:01.840 --> 02:37:04.030
You could be in and out
of that gym in certainly

02:37:04.030 --> 02:37:05.110
under 60 minutes.

02:37:05.110 --> 02:37:07.402
The reality of it is you
could probably be out of there

02:37:07.402 --> 02:37:08.590
in under 50 minutes.

02:37:08.590 --> 02:37:12.767
The total work time could be
30, 35 once you get going.

02:37:12.767 --> 02:37:14.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
that's three days.

02:37:14.350 --> 02:37:17.890
As you pointed out,
probably more work per week

02:37:17.890 --> 02:37:21.460
is going to be better
in terms of maximizing

02:37:21.460 --> 02:37:25.150
goals of aesthetic goals, and
performance-enhancing goals,

02:37:25.150 --> 02:37:26.740
and longevity goals.

02:37:26.740 --> 02:37:27.490
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:37:27.490 --> 02:37:29.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I mean,
the numbers that I've heard

02:37:29.800 --> 02:37:32.890
is that we should all try
to get somewhere between 150

02:37:32.890 --> 02:37:35.800
and probably more like 180
to 200 minutes of zone two

02:37:35.800 --> 02:37:37.930
cardio per week minimum.

02:37:37.930 --> 02:37:41.260
But as I recall, you
consider zone two cardio

02:37:41.260 --> 02:37:44.770
so low intensity
that just walking

02:37:44.770 --> 02:37:49.450
around qualifies as really
zone one, zone two cardio.

02:37:49.450 --> 02:37:50.200
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:37:50.200 --> 02:37:51.970
Not to take us
too far off track.

02:37:51.970 --> 02:37:55.270
But I think it's actually
useful to differentiate

02:37:55.270 --> 02:37:59.750
what I consider to be exercise
and physical activity.

02:37:59.750 --> 02:38:04.090
So physical activity
is out for a walk.

02:38:04.090 --> 02:38:08.260
It is using a walking
treadmill while you're at work.

02:38:08.260 --> 02:38:11.800
It is parking farther
in the parking lot

02:38:11.800 --> 02:38:12.910
and taking more steps.

02:38:12.910 --> 02:38:14.470
These are all important.

02:38:14.470 --> 02:38:19.300
And what's clear, you are
not going to reach, likely,

02:38:19.300 --> 02:38:22.870
optimal health by only
exercising hard and then

02:38:22.870 --> 02:38:25.922
sitting around the other 23
and 1/2 hours of the day.

02:38:25.922 --> 02:38:27.130
So it's very, very important.

02:38:27.130 --> 02:38:30.100
Whether you want to do that in
the form of zone one or zone

02:38:30.100 --> 02:38:33.460
two and hit 30 minutes a day.

02:38:33.460 --> 02:38:35.620
Various organizations
will say things like that.

02:38:35.620 --> 02:38:37.750
You need to have
30 minutes a day

02:38:37.750 --> 02:38:40.187
of moderate to
low-intensity exercise.

02:38:40.187 --> 02:38:41.020
I don't really care.

02:38:41.020 --> 02:38:43.520
You can combine it
like that if you want.

02:38:43.520 --> 02:38:48.350
What you don't want to do is
just physical activity only,

02:38:48.350 --> 02:38:51.850
which is almost always
going to be like zone one

02:38:51.850 --> 02:38:53.020
to maybe zone two.

02:38:53.020 --> 02:38:54.910
You also don't want
to go the other end

02:38:54.910 --> 02:38:57.340
of the spectrum, which is,
again, I lift hard three days

02:38:57.340 --> 02:38:57.850
a week.

02:38:57.850 --> 02:38:59.170
And then what to do you
do the rest of the time?

02:38:59.170 --> 02:39:00.340
Nothing.

02:39:00.340 --> 02:39:01.910
That's not optimal either.

02:39:01.910 --> 02:39:05.410
And so, I guess the system
I walked you through here--

02:39:05.410 --> 02:39:08.320
or the example, rather,
I walked you through is--

02:39:08.320 --> 02:39:10.262
you would need to
maybe supplant that

02:39:10.262 --> 02:39:11.470
with being physically active.

02:39:11.470 --> 02:39:15.307
If you work, say you're a
nurse, and you're on your feet.

02:39:15.307 --> 02:39:16.390
You're moving up and down.

02:39:16.390 --> 02:39:17.765
You're probably
actually covering

02:39:17.765 --> 02:39:19.480
a decent amount of
your physical activity

02:39:19.480 --> 02:39:22.570
because you might be
at 15,000 steps a day.

02:39:22.570 --> 02:39:24.730
If you're sitting in
front of your computer

02:39:24.730 --> 02:39:26.325
and you do this same
three-day split,

02:39:26.325 --> 02:39:27.700
you would probably
need to go out

02:39:27.700 --> 02:39:30.550
of your way to make sure you're
adding a bunch more steps.

02:39:30.550 --> 02:39:32.890
And so, you might need
to add several hours

02:39:32.890 --> 02:39:36.460
of walking to hit that
150, 180 minutes a week

02:39:36.460 --> 02:39:37.600
of physical activity.

02:39:37.600 --> 02:39:41.050
Because the program I laid out
is, if you're doing, really,

02:39:41.050 --> 02:39:43.840
45 minutes three-days a
week, maybe 60 minutes,

02:39:43.840 --> 02:39:46.120
at best you've hit 180.

02:39:46.120 --> 02:39:47.888
60 minutes times
three, 180 a week.

02:39:47.888 --> 02:39:49.930
So you might actually need
to then throw in maybe

02:39:49.930 --> 02:39:51.040
some more specific walks.

02:39:51.040 --> 02:39:54.260
So you could do that
in a number of ways.

02:39:54.260 --> 02:39:56.515
It could be, again, actual
structured exercise.

02:39:56.515 --> 02:39:59.140
It could be simply I'm going to
do a 10-minute walk three times

02:39:59.140 --> 02:39:59.640
a day.

02:39:59.640 --> 02:40:02.530
It could be the exercise
snacks that we talked about

02:40:02.530 --> 02:40:03.670
in a previous episode.

02:40:03.670 --> 02:40:05.380
So there's lots
of ways to engage

02:40:05.380 --> 02:40:07.870
in more physical activity.

02:40:07.870 --> 02:40:10.510
But to me, those are
different, oftentimes,

02:40:10.510 --> 02:40:12.408
than structured exercise.

02:40:12.408 --> 02:40:14.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think many
people will appreciate that you

02:40:14.950 --> 02:40:18.820
put out there for us a
three-day-a-week protocol,

02:40:18.820 --> 02:40:22.780
because many people simply don't
have more time to exercise.

02:40:22.780 --> 02:40:25.323
They're putting emphasis
on these other bins

02:40:25.323 --> 02:40:25.990
in the quadrant.

02:40:25.990 --> 02:40:29.840
And frankly, those other bins
are very important as well.

02:40:29.840 --> 02:40:33.550
So wonderful that
people can check off

02:40:33.550 --> 02:40:35.500
some critical boxes
for aesthetics,

02:40:35.500 --> 02:40:39.670
and performance, and longevity
with three days of work

02:40:39.670 --> 02:40:42.902
or workouts, per
week, I should say.

02:40:42.902 --> 02:40:44.610
What are some other
schedules that people

02:40:44.610 --> 02:40:47.400
can follow if they're willing
to dedicate a bit more time

02:40:47.400 --> 02:40:48.420
toward their fitness?

02:40:48.420 --> 02:40:49.170
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

02:40:49.170 --> 02:40:51.600
If you wanted to do
another sample of maybe

02:40:51.600 --> 02:40:52.800
a four-day week.

02:40:52.800 --> 02:40:55.680
And again, to clarify this,
I'm really happy you said that.

02:40:55.680 --> 02:40:58.320
This is a four-day a week
of structured exercise.

02:40:58.320 --> 02:41:00.465
This would not account
your physical activity

02:41:00.465 --> 02:41:01.215
and moving around.

02:41:01.215 --> 02:41:03.257
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Which
everybody should be doing.

02:41:03.257 --> 02:41:04.410
ANDY GALPIN: Absolutely.

02:41:04.410 --> 02:41:07.012
Maybe this is
something like day one

02:41:07.012 --> 02:41:08.970
you're going to do a
strength-training session,

02:41:08.970 --> 02:41:13.770
and you'll stay in the five
to 10 or so repetition range.

02:41:13.770 --> 02:41:16.650
A little bit of strength, a
little bit of hypertrophy,

02:41:16.650 --> 02:41:19.230
you've checked off
a couple of boxes.

02:41:19.230 --> 02:41:22.500
Probably whole body, so
that you get all the body

02:41:22.500 --> 02:41:24.600
parts covered or close.

02:41:24.600 --> 02:41:27.270
We're looking at generally
multi-joint exercises.

02:41:27.270 --> 02:41:29.160
Could be combination
of barbells,

02:41:29.160 --> 02:41:34.080
free-weights, bands,
machines, anything like that

02:41:34.080 --> 02:41:35.700
would be day one.

02:41:35.700 --> 02:41:37.740
You could come back
the very next day,

02:41:37.740 --> 02:41:39.780
or you could wait 24 hours.

02:41:39.780 --> 02:41:41.850
But the second day
of your exercise

02:41:41.850 --> 02:41:43.723
would be maybe
your long duration.

02:41:43.723 --> 02:41:46.140
And this is actually sort of
similar to how you set it up.

02:41:46.140 --> 02:41:47.520
It's you do a little
bit of the inverse.

02:41:47.520 --> 02:41:48.990
But what you're
kind of saying is,

02:41:48.990 --> 02:41:52.190
I'm probably going to be a
little bit sore from day one.

02:41:52.190 --> 02:41:54.970
And I don't have any free
body parts that aren't sore.

02:41:54.970 --> 02:41:57.490
So instead of trying to do
another lift or something,

02:41:57.490 --> 02:42:00.670
I'm just going to put in some
restorative longer duration

02:42:00.670 --> 02:42:02.770
stuff-- same exact
principles for long duration

02:42:02.770 --> 02:42:03.820
we just talked about.

02:42:03.820 --> 02:42:05.380
It could be a swim.

02:42:05.380 --> 02:42:07.810
It could be any
number of things.

02:42:07.810 --> 02:42:09.460
Could be your sport.

02:42:09.460 --> 02:42:11.300
It could be you're
out, ride the bike

02:42:11.300 --> 02:42:15.820
and go for a jog in the sun,
whatever you would like to do.

02:42:15.820 --> 02:42:19.022
If you're feeling better, maybe
that's a little harder longer.

02:42:19.022 --> 02:42:21.230
If you're feeling pretty
beat up from the day before,

02:42:21.230 --> 02:42:23.260
maybe that's a little
bit shorter and slower.

02:42:23.260 --> 02:42:24.640
You can modify it.

02:42:24.640 --> 02:42:27.055
Then maybe you take
the next day off.

02:42:27.055 --> 02:42:28.900
Or that's open.

02:42:28.900 --> 02:42:31.690
Your third day of
exercise is now,

02:42:31.690 --> 02:42:35.290
instead of being that five to 10
repetition range for your lift,

02:42:35.290 --> 02:42:39.820
you do something like
11 to 30 reps range.

02:42:39.820 --> 02:42:42.640
Also, this could be
exchanged for something

02:42:42.640 --> 02:42:45.967
more like body weight,
more muscular endurance

02:42:45.967 --> 02:42:46.550
type of stuff.

02:42:46.550 --> 02:42:48.700
So this is a great
day, maybe it's yoga.

02:42:48.700 --> 02:42:51.910
Maybe it is a gymnastics
thing you're working on,

02:42:51.910 --> 02:42:54.070
or any of the many
other styles of training

02:42:54.070 --> 02:42:56.770
that are not quote
unquote lifting weights.

02:42:56.770 --> 02:42:58.750
But they're not just
walking and hiking.

02:42:58.750 --> 02:43:01.312
So it could be a Pilates,
or equivalent, anything

02:43:01.312 --> 02:43:02.770
like this where
you're going to get

02:43:02.770 --> 02:43:03.937
some muscular burn in there.

02:43:03.937 --> 02:43:06.340
But it's probably not
any additional weight

02:43:06.340 --> 02:43:07.930
outside of body
weight, or if it is,

02:43:07.930 --> 02:43:11.890
it's fairly minimal, five, 10,
15 pounds, something like that

02:43:11.890 --> 02:43:13.030
would be nice.

02:43:13.030 --> 02:43:15.700
Could also be done in a circuit.

02:43:15.700 --> 02:43:18.070
So we could hit
our high heart rate

02:43:18.070 --> 02:43:21.640
and we could hit some
muscular endurance in there.

02:43:21.640 --> 02:43:24.790
Group activity class
might be nice here.

02:43:24.790 --> 02:43:28.357
Even maybe something like a
spin class or a dance class.

02:43:28.357 --> 02:43:29.690
All these things could be great.

02:43:29.690 --> 02:43:32.710
And then, maybe you even
finish that with 10 minutes

02:43:32.710 --> 02:43:36.610
of some light weights to hit the
body part you say didn't get.

02:43:36.610 --> 02:43:38.350
So maybe you did
the dance class.

02:43:38.350 --> 02:43:41.710
And then you finish and you do
10 minutes of upper body sets

02:43:41.710 --> 02:43:44.615
of 30 to make sure you
get a nice pump there,

02:43:44.615 --> 02:43:46.990
because your legs probably
got some work during the dance

02:43:46.990 --> 02:43:48.010
class, but your
upper body didn't.

02:43:48.010 --> 02:43:49.968
And so you balance the
system out a little bit.

02:43:49.968 --> 02:43:53.260
So all body parts got a little
bit of muscular endurance.

02:43:53.260 --> 02:43:56.350
Your heart rate got really
high, came back down,

02:43:56.350 --> 02:43:59.560
and you checked
both of those boxes.

02:43:59.560 --> 02:44:06.310
Now, it's important to remember
the hypertrophy episode.

02:44:06.310 --> 02:44:11.140
Doing sets of, say,
15-plus repetitions per set

02:44:11.140 --> 02:44:15.520
is as effective as doing
sets of five to 10 or 12

02:44:15.520 --> 02:44:17.740
for hypertrophy--
gaining muscle.

02:44:17.740 --> 02:44:20.020
It's not effective though
for strength gains.

02:44:20.020 --> 02:44:22.030
So you wouldn't want
to do this only,

02:44:22.030 --> 02:44:24.920
because you'd really be doing
nothing to improve your muscle

02:44:24.920 --> 02:44:25.420
strength.

02:44:25.420 --> 02:44:27.170
And you want to make
sure that that box is

02:44:27.170 --> 02:44:30.700
ticked at least a little bit.

02:44:30.700 --> 02:44:33.100
Then, again, you could take
the day off after this.

02:44:33.100 --> 02:44:35.500
Or you could roll right
into your fourth exercise

02:44:35.500 --> 02:44:38.380
day, which would be your last
exercise session of the week.

02:44:38.380 --> 02:44:43.160
And you would do something
more of a medium intensity.

02:44:43.160 --> 02:44:46.540
So this is a little bit higher
intensity than our second day.

02:44:46.540 --> 02:44:50.350
And this could be something
like shadowboxing,

02:44:50.350 --> 02:44:51.370
or hitting a heavy bag.

02:44:51.370 --> 02:44:54.610
It could be a little bit of
higher intensity intervals,

02:44:54.610 --> 02:44:56.380
but not all the way up.

02:44:56.380 --> 02:45:01.580
So maybe this is you're
going to do a one minute on,

02:45:01.580 --> 02:45:03.520
one minute off on the bike.

02:45:03.520 --> 02:45:06.100
But you're only going to
go to 85%, 90% heart rate.

02:45:06.100 --> 02:45:08.950
And then, instead of going
off during that one minute,

02:45:08.950 --> 02:45:10.970
you drop it down to 50%.

02:45:10.970 --> 02:45:13.690
So we would actually look like
30 minutes of straight work,

02:45:13.690 --> 02:45:16.570
but you would have a little
bit of rolling intensity

02:45:16.570 --> 02:45:19.780
as opposed to staying
really nice and restorative.

02:45:19.780 --> 02:45:21.730
It's going to be
some work there.

02:45:21.730 --> 02:45:25.130
And you would finish it
with something like five

02:45:25.130 --> 02:45:28.330
to six minutes total of
max heart rate stuff.

02:45:28.330 --> 02:45:30.625
Which lines up perfectly
with that number you actually

02:45:30.625 --> 02:45:34.180
[LAUGHS] created on
our endurance episode

02:45:34.180 --> 02:45:38.770
of hitting six minutes total
per week of maximum heart rate

02:45:38.770 --> 02:45:39.710
or close.

02:45:39.710 --> 02:45:42.730
So you could wrap that
all up into one session.

02:45:42.730 --> 02:45:44.800
You could do those
in the inverse order,

02:45:44.800 --> 02:45:47.980
thorough warm-up, a few
minutes, whether you

02:45:47.980 --> 02:45:50.260
want to do 30-second
bursts or a minute burst,

02:45:50.260 --> 02:45:51.400
or straight five minutes.

02:45:51.400 --> 02:45:55.060
This is a protocol I like to
use a ton on the assault bike.

02:45:55.060 --> 02:45:59.410
It is simply a good warm-up,
10 minutes solid warm-up.

02:45:59.410 --> 02:46:01.780
Recover, and then I'm
going to go five minutes

02:46:01.780 --> 02:46:04.240
and cover as much distance
as I can in five minutes.

02:46:04.240 --> 02:46:04.870
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Brutal.

02:46:04.870 --> 02:46:05.620
ANDY GALPIN: It is brutal.

02:46:05.620 --> 02:46:06.430
And it's amazing.

02:46:06.430 --> 02:46:10.240
And you get a lot
done in five minutes.

02:46:10.240 --> 02:46:13.480
10 minutes on the back
of that is a very gradual

02:46:13.480 --> 02:46:15.160
bring back to Earth there.

02:46:15.160 --> 02:46:16.648
I actually, in
that case, I don't

02:46:16.648 --> 02:46:18.190
need to do
down-regulation breathing,

02:46:18.190 --> 02:46:21.970
because I've spent 10 minutes
actually coming way back down.

02:46:21.970 --> 02:46:25.720
And the last two minutes or
so of that is very deliberate,

02:46:25.720 --> 02:46:29.050
five-second inhale through
the nose, five-second exhale

02:46:29.050 --> 02:46:31.068
through the nose while
I'm barely just moving.

02:46:31.068 --> 02:46:32.860
And you end up being
in a pretty good spot.

02:46:32.860 --> 02:46:37.180
So that, again, time-wise could
easily be done in 30 minutes.

02:46:37.180 --> 02:46:38.860
And you'd be rounded off there.

02:46:38.860 --> 02:46:42.280
So the nice part about
this four day a week split

02:46:42.280 --> 02:46:44.680
as well as a three
day a week split is it

02:46:44.680 --> 02:46:46.840
does give you a little
bit of flexibility.

02:46:46.840 --> 02:46:49.720
And so, what I mean
is, maybe Monday

02:46:49.720 --> 02:46:53.230
your plan is to do
the day one lift.

02:46:53.230 --> 02:46:55.900
And then, any number of
things popped up in life.

02:46:55.900 --> 02:46:57.907
Just shift it back to Tuesday.

02:46:57.907 --> 02:47:00.490
Rather than saying Monday is leg
day and then all of a sudden,

02:47:00.490 --> 02:47:01.530
something happened,
you miss leg day.

02:47:01.530 --> 02:47:03.570
It's just you're doing
these things in order.

02:47:03.570 --> 02:47:07.800
And you would like to get all
four done in a seven-day span.

02:47:07.800 --> 02:47:10.080
But if it doesn't happen, fine.

02:47:10.080 --> 02:47:11.820
The next day you
get to work out,

02:47:11.820 --> 02:47:13.860
you just go right back
into the next workout.

02:47:13.860 --> 02:47:17.430
And it doesn't matter what
day they land on exactly.

02:47:17.430 --> 02:47:20.520
For the three-day routine,
that works very nice,

02:47:20.520 --> 02:47:23.040
because the assumption
there is you

02:47:23.040 --> 02:47:26.340
really only have time for
three workouts a week.

02:47:26.340 --> 02:47:27.948
And so, that's
sort of implicit is

02:47:27.948 --> 02:47:29.490
there's probably
some chaos happening

02:47:29.490 --> 02:47:31.080
in the schedule a little bit.

02:47:31.080 --> 02:47:32.820
And you don't really
have the ability

02:47:32.820 --> 02:47:34.780
to lock in three days per week.

02:47:34.780 --> 02:47:36.780
If that's not the
case, you can go.

02:47:36.780 --> 02:47:40.260
But we're trying to
listen to the pain points

02:47:40.260 --> 02:47:43.590
that people have with exercise
and see if we can give them

02:47:43.590 --> 02:47:46.627
some solutions for those.

02:47:46.627 --> 02:47:48.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Several
things about this program

02:47:48.710 --> 02:47:50.270
are attractive to me.

02:47:50.270 --> 02:47:52.250
One of them you just
mentioned, which

02:47:52.250 --> 02:47:57.190
is that by not rigidly
attaching individual workouts

02:47:57.190 --> 02:48:01.640
to specific days of the week,
one, in theory, could say,

02:48:01.640 --> 02:48:02.240
OK, it was--

02:48:02.240 --> 02:48:03.907
I didn't get that
much sleep last night.

02:48:03.907 --> 02:48:04.970
I don't feel--

02:48:04.970 --> 02:48:06.890
I know that a lot of
people say, what is feel?

02:48:06.890 --> 02:48:08.515
But I don't feel
recovered, or like I'm

02:48:08.515 --> 02:48:10.910
going to get that much out
of the workout tomorrow.

02:48:10.910 --> 02:48:14.960
So I'm-- or today-- so I'm just
going to push it forward a day.

02:48:14.960 --> 02:48:17.660
And the ability to slide
workouts forward or back

02:48:17.660 --> 02:48:22.190
by day I think is incredibly
valuable for the consistency's

02:48:22.190 --> 02:48:22.770
sake.

02:48:22.770 --> 02:48:25.910
I also really like this idea of
some of the long duration work

02:48:25.910 --> 02:48:28.400
coming a day after
hitting the strength

02:48:28.400 --> 02:48:30.150
and a bit of hypertrophy work.

02:48:30.150 --> 02:48:32.450
So this would be the day two.

02:48:32.450 --> 02:48:35.150
One thing that I've
experienced over and over

02:48:35.150 --> 02:48:38.700
is that if I'm very sore in a
given muscle group, especially

02:48:38.700 --> 02:48:40.460
my legs, doing
some low intensity

02:48:40.460 --> 02:48:45.200
cardio, whether or not
it's a jog, or on the bike,

02:48:45.200 --> 02:48:49.135
typically for me it's a
jog, or even skipping rope

02:48:49.135 --> 02:48:51.920
and walking does seem to
dissipate the soreness.

02:48:51.920 --> 02:48:53.420
I'm sure there's a
mechanism-- there

02:48:53.420 --> 02:48:55.130
has to-- there's a mechanism
for everything, frankly.

02:48:55.130 --> 02:48:55.880
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:48:55.880 --> 02:48:58.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But I
like that arrangement.

02:48:58.740 --> 02:49:02.990
And then, I also like
this idea of making sure

02:49:02.990 --> 02:49:06.920
that there's a workout
for muscular endurance.

02:49:06.920 --> 02:49:10.100
Because I feel like unless I've
been stuck without a good gym,

02:49:10.100 --> 02:49:13.010
or I've decided to
specifically train

02:49:13.010 --> 02:49:15.330
bodyweight exercise, which
I did a few years ago,

02:49:15.330 --> 02:49:18.390
I got really excited about some
of Pavel Tsatsouline's work.

02:49:18.390 --> 02:49:19.140
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

02:49:19.140 --> 02:49:19.520
Great stuff.

02:49:19.520 --> 02:49:19.880
Yeah.

02:49:19.880 --> 02:49:20.380
Amazing.

02:49:20.380 --> 02:49:22.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He has a
book, The Naked Warrior,

02:49:22.820 --> 02:49:24.770
which doesn't involve training
naked, although I suppose

02:49:24.770 --> 02:49:25.590
you could if you wanted.

02:49:25.590 --> 02:49:27.200
But it was really
about no weights.

02:49:27.200 --> 02:49:29.570
And involved building
up to pistol squats

02:49:29.570 --> 02:49:32.270
and one-arm push-ups,
and things of that sort,

02:49:32.270 --> 02:49:33.687
even doing pull-ups on doors.

02:49:33.687 --> 02:49:36.020
And I discovered that some
door frames are much stronger

02:49:36.020 --> 02:49:37.350
than others in hotels.

02:49:37.350 --> 02:49:37.850
[LAUGHTER]

02:49:37.850 --> 02:49:41.340
I just accidentally
caused some damage there.

02:49:41.340 --> 02:49:44.450
But in any case, muscular
endurance, I think,

02:49:44.450 --> 02:49:46.940
is a really
interesting one that I

02:49:46.940 --> 02:49:48.650
plan to incorporate
into my schedule.

02:49:48.650 --> 02:49:52.790
But that is, I think, is
one that's often overlooked,

02:49:52.790 --> 02:49:54.470
unless people really
have an aversion

02:49:54.470 --> 02:49:56.207
to weights and to machines.

02:49:56.207 --> 02:49:57.290
ANDY GALPIN: You're right.

02:49:57.290 --> 02:49:58.123
And it shouldn't be.

02:49:58.123 --> 02:50:00.620
Because it's pretty
low-hanging fruit.

02:50:00.620 --> 02:50:03.110
You don't need a lot of
equipment for it typically.

02:50:03.110 --> 02:50:04.520
It doesn't hurt that bad.

02:50:04.520 --> 02:50:06.440
You don't often get
that sore out of it.

02:50:06.440 --> 02:50:09.420
And you're going to feel a
nice wonderful pump afterwards.

02:50:09.420 --> 02:50:10.770
So it's great.

02:50:10.770 --> 02:50:12.410
And as we discussed
many times now,

02:50:12.410 --> 02:50:14.103
it is quite effective
at hypertrophy.

02:50:14.103 --> 02:50:15.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:50:15.020 --> 02:50:17.390
I also-- I don't know
if they fit specifically

02:50:17.390 --> 02:50:18.630
with muscular endurance.

02:50:18.630 --> 02:50:21.470
But if you look at the
physiques, for example,

02:50:21.470 --> 02:50:24.500
on rock climbers, I
mean, they have-- to me,

02:50:24.500 --> 02:50:26.360
of course they have--

02:50:26.360 --> 02:50:32.690
usually the experienced climbers
have pretty remarkable body

02:50:32.690 --> 02:50:33.353
compositions.

02:50:33.353 --> 02:50:35.270
They tend to be lean,
and lithe, and flexible,

02:50:35.270 --> 02:50:37.530
all those things that
many people aspire to.

02:50:37.530 --> 02:50:39.530
But the other thing is,
their development always

02:50:39.530 --> 02:50:41.420
looks exceedingly balanced.

02:50:41.420 --> 02:50:43.370
You don't really
tend to see climbers

02:50:43.370 --> 02:50:44.930
that are overdeveloped
in the torso

02:50:44.930 --> 02:50:47.570
and underdeveloped in the arms,
or overdeveloped in the arms

02:50:47.570 --> 02:50:50.360
despite all the climbing and
underdeveloped relatively

02:50:50.360 --> 02:50:51.740
in the other limb movement.

02:50:51.740 --> 02:50:54.560
And that's true
for women and men.

02:50:54.560 --> 02:50:56.690
It's not a sport that
I participate in.

02:50:56.690 --> 02:51:00.173
But it seems like what
they're doing is essentially

02:51:00.173 --> 02:51:01.340
muscular endurance training.

02:51:01.340 --> 02:51:01.850
ANDY GALPIN: Basically.

02:51:01.850 --> 02:51:03.067
Yeah.
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:51:03.067 --> 02:51:06.110
So there's really something
there to be valued.

02:51:06.110 --> 02:51:09.830
So that's a four-day a week
schedule with off days or rest

02:51:09.830 --> 02:51:12.480
days inserted as needed.

02:51:12.480 --> 02:51:14.570
And then just-- and continuing.

02:51:14.570 --> 02:51:17.600
For those that are a bit more
committed to their fitness

02:51:17.600 --> 02:51:21.350
and want to do a five or
six-day a week program,

02:51:21.350 --> 02:51:23.787
would you recommend
just collapsing

02:51:23.787 --> 02:51:26.120
some of the off days, paying
more attention to recovery,

02:51:26.120 --> 02:51:28.260
and cycling through
more quickly?

02:51:28.260 --> 02:51:29.510
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, absolutely.

02:51:29.510 --> 02:51:31.910
You could combine that
and just run that--

02:51:31.910 --> 02:51:33.210
either one of those programs.

02:51:33.210 --> 02:51:36.530
So you could run that three-day
week program back to back.

02:51:36.530 --> 02:51:38.005
Do it, get that
done in six days.

02:51:38.005 --> 02:51:38.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Ah.

02:51:38.310 --> 02:51:40.220
So day one, speed,
power, hypertrophy.

02:51:40.220 --> 02:51:43.280
Day two, strength, work
with elevated heart rate,

02:51:43.280 --> 02:51:44.110
anaerobic capacity.

02:51:44.110 --> 02:51:45.110
And day three endurance.

02:51:45.110 --> 02:51:46.940
And then just-- and then
just cycle through again.

02:51:46.940 --> 02:51:47.690
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:51:47.690 --> 02:51:49.880
You take day four
off of the week.

02:51:49.880 --> 02:51:51.290
And then you go back again.

02:51:51.290 --> 02:51:55.430
So we would be having six
days of exercise, one day off.

02:51:55.430 --> 02:51:57.650
And you'd be getting every
one of those adaptations

02:51:57.650 --> 02:51:58.863
in multiple times a week.

02:51:58.863 --> 02:52:01.280
That is almost exactly how I
would set up a six-day-a-week

02:52:01.280 --> 02:52:01.850
program.

02:52:01.850 --> 02:52:04.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great I love
the elegance and the simplicity

02:52:04.430 --> 02:52:05.888
of that and the
thoroughness of it,

02:52:05.888 --> 02:52:08.900
because it checks off so
many, if not all of the nine

02:52:08.900 --> 02:52:11.090
major adaptations to
exercise that we've been

02:52:11.090 --> 02:52:13.100
talking about these episodes.

02:52:13.100 --> 02:52:17.660
And I suppose the one thing that
I want to highlight and pose

02:52:17.660 --> 02:52:22.268
this also as a question is
that early in our discussions,

02:52:22.268 --> 02:52:24.310
in a previous episode,
you mentioned that so much

02:52:24.310 --> 02:52:28.660
of what people think of
and apply as it relates

02:52:28.660 --> 02:52:30.670
to resistance
training is borrowed

02:52:30.670 --> 02:52:32.770
from bodybuilding
and hypertrophy

02:52:32.770 --> 02:52:33.790
training specifically.

02:52:33.790 --> 02:52:36.880
Which typically involves getting
close to failure or failures,

02:52:36.880 --> 02:52:39.580
sometimes even involving rest
pause, where you hit failure,

02:52:39.580 --> 02:52:40.870
then set the weight
down for a few sections

02:52:40.870 --> 02:52:42.760
and repeat these high
intensity techniques,

02:52:42.760 --> 02:52:46.030
accentuating the negative,
so-called the centric,

02:52:46.030 --> 02:52:47.660
et cetera.

02:52:47.660 --> 02:52:50.660
In hearing about these
protocols of three-day-a-week,

02:52:50.660 --> 02:52:53.000
or four-day-a-week,
six-day-a-week,

02:52:53.000 --> 02:52:58.310
it's very clear to me that if
one is not careful to omit that

02:52:58.310 --> 02:53:01.880
kind of thinking, and suddenly
is taking their strength work

02:53:01.880 --> 02:53:04.953
and speed to failure, or is
pushing too hard on muscular

02:53:04.953 --> 02:53:07.370
endurance to the point where
you're just grinding out that

02:53:07.370 --> 02:53:10.640
very last push-up on every set,
that the amount of soreness

02:53:10.640 --> 02:53:15.380
and the amount of recovery that
results from these workouts

02:53:15.380 --> 02:53:17.950
might start to cause
progress issues.

02:53:17.950 --> 02:53:19.700
So one thing that's
in the back of my mind

02:53:19.700 --> 02:53:21.440
is, as you've described
these programs is,

02:53:21.440 --> 02:53:23.273
that even though some
of them are very brief

02:53:23.273 --> 02:53:26.065
or involve a minimum
of time commitment,

02:53:26.065 --> 02:53:28.440
in particular a three-day week
but also the four-day week

02:53:28.440 --> 02:53:32.490
schedule, that there is
a discipline involved

02:53:32.490 --> 02:53:34.830
in making sure that you
stick to the workout

02:53:34.830 --> 02:53:36.330
that you're supposed
to do that day.

02:53:36.330 --> 02:53:39.930
And not go ham, as
they say, and just

02:53:39.930 --> 02:53:42.338
throw in a couple of extra
sets of bicep curls and tricep

02:53:42.338 --> 02:53:44.130
pushes, because you
want to do that and you

02:53:44.130 --> 02:53:45.690
thought you could maybe you
could get away with that.

02:53:45.690 --> 02:53:47.820
But you have to come back
pretty quickly and do

02:53:47.820 --> 02:53:52.680
some serious, meaning devoted,
speed and power work and/or

02:53:52.680 --> 02:53:53.470
strength work.

02:53:53.470 --> 02:53:55.740
And if you haven't
been disciplined

02:53:55.740 --> 02:53:58.350
about not doing certain
forms of exercise,

02:53:58.350 --> 02:54:01.253
I could see how the whole
thing could crash quickly,

02:54:01.253 --> 02:54:03.420
and one could think, oh,
this is just too much work,

02:54:03.420 --> 02:54:04.620
or it's not for me.

02:54:04.620 --> 02:54:05.370
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:54:05.370 --> 02:54:07.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So this, I suppose,

02:54:07.240 --> 02:54:09.060
is now where the
question comes, which

02:54:09.060 --> 02:54:11.970
is, what are some
of the key points

02:54:11.970 --> 02:54:15.750
that people need to keep in mind
when they embrace a program?

02:54:15.750 --> 02:54:18.150
How rigidly do they
need to stay attached

02:54:18.150 --> 02:54:20.670
to today's endurance day.

02:54:20.670 --> 02:54:21.900
I'm just doing endurance.

02:54:21.900 --> 02:54:23.002
Today's strength day.

02:54:23.002 --> 02:54:24.210
I'm just doing strength work.

02:54:24.210 --> 02:54:26.360
I'm not going to take
things to absolute failure

02:54:26.360 --> 02:54:27.630
or beyond failure.

02:54:27.630 --> 02:54:32.710
ANDY GALPIN: I am
absolutely happy with anyone

02:54:32.710 --> 02:54:35.920
modifying any of
the sample programs

02:54:35.920 --> 02:54:37.840
however they would like to.

02:54:37.840 --> 02:54:42.250
My only recommendation for
the question you just posed

02:54:42.250 --> 02:54:45.730
would be set your program.

02:54:45.730 --> 02:54:48.190
And then, if you're going
to make a change, fine.

02:54:48.190 --> 02:54:50.900
But that is a change
to your program.

02:54:50.900 --> 02:54:55.000
In other words, don't just
make decisions every single day

02:54:55.000 --> 02:54:55.780
and make changes.

02:54:55.780 --> 02:54:58.270
If you're doing that, you might
as well not have a program.

02:54:58.270 --> 02:54:59.860
And as we described
earlier, there

02:54:59.860 --> 02:55:02.860
is clear evidence that
having a program is

02:55:02.860 --> 02:55:05.050
better than not, regardless
of the effectiveness

02:55:05.050 --> 02:55:06.290
of the program.

02:55:06.290 --> 02:55:09.550
And so, my general comment
to that is, OK, fine,

02:55:09.550 --> 02:55:12.130
a day or two, you made
some modifications.

02:55:12.130 --> 02:55:13.240
No problem.

02:55:13.240 --> 02:55:15.460
We're in a situation now
where you're basically

02:55:15.460 --> 02:55:18.070
changing the workout
every day as you go,

02:55:18.070 --> 02:55:20.140
then we just need to
write a new program.

02:55:20.140 --> 02:55:21.700
We need to reassess
where we're at.

02:55:21.700 --> 02:55:23.740
Because we need to
have some structure.

02:55:23.740 --> 02:55:27.490
Look, the reality of it is,
I change the programming

02:55:27.490 --> 02:55:30.253
I'm going to do the
day of often because

02:55:30.253 --> 02:55:31.420
of any number of situations.

02:55:31.420 --> 02:55:33.110
I just don't feel like it.

02:55:33.110 --> 02:55:35.233
I way overestimated today.

02:55:35.233 --> 02:55:37.150
We talked a little bit
in the previous episode

02:55:37.150 --> 02:55:40.870
about autoregulation, which
is a style of periodization

02:55:40.870 --> 02:55:43.030
and program design in which
you're adjusting based

02:55:43.030 --> 02:55:45.010
on how you're actually
feeling that day,

02:55:45.010 --> 02:55:46.437
but with some
specific structures.

02:55:46.437 --> 02:55:48.520
So you're going to take
some measurements that day

02:55:48.520 --> 02:55:49.020
and adjust.

02:55:49.020 --> 02:55:51.820
So autoregulation is a
very, very effective tool.

02:55:51.820 --> 02:55:54.310
You just need to make
sure that auto is dialed.

02:55:54.310 --> 02:55:56.935
In other words, is it
because your body actually

02:55:56.935 --> 02:55:58.060
needed something different?

02:55:58.060 --> 02:56:01.120
Or is it because you're now
just getting a little bit lazy?

02:56:01.120 --> 02:56:03.280
Now you're just not
feeling like it today.

02:56:03.280 --> 02:56:06.550
So there's a little bit of an
impossible line to draw there.

02:56:06.550 --> 02:56:08.230
Both scenarios are real--

02:56:08.230 --> 02:56:11.273
gray area-- a lot is
real in the moment.

02:56:11.273 --> 02:56:12.940
And so, you just need
to be a little bit

02:56:12.940 --> 02:56:17.500
aware of having some reality
check, listening to your body,

02:56:17.500 --> 02:56:20.565
but then also being like,
hey, no, I'm talking to you.

02:56:20.565 --> 02:56:21.940
I'm telling you
this is the plan.

02:56:21.940 --> 02:56:22.898
We're going to do this.

02:56:22.898 --> 02:56:24.185
And staying within it.

02:56:24.185 --> 02:56:28.200
It is going to be
challenging to progressively

02:56:28.200 --> 02:56:32.900
overload and therefore get a
higher likelihood of success

02:56:32.900 --> 02:56:35.120
at your training
program if you're just

02:56:35.120 --> 02:56:38.150
making decisions and changing
the program right before you

02:56:38.150 --> 02:56:38.670
work out.

02:56:38.670 --> 02:56:40.623
You're probably
not-- you're probably

02:56:40.623 --> 02:56:42.290
going-- for most
people, you're probably

02:56:42.290 --> 02:56:47.760
going to choose less or off
more so than you choose more.

02:56:47.760 --> 02:56:51.342
Now, having said that, there
are more than a few clients

02:56:51.342 --> 02:56:52.800
that have come
through our programs

02:56:52.800 --> 02:56:56.770
where they choose more always.

02:56:56.770 --> 02:56:57.730
They add a set.

02:56:57.730 --> 02:56:59.170
They add an exercise.

02:56:59.170 --> 02:57:01.090
They add in another workout.

02:57:01.090 --> 02:57:02.780
And that can be OK.

02:57:02.780 --> 02:57:05.120
But we're going to track
various markers on them.

02:57:05.120 --> 02:57:07.840
And if we see these things
consistently going down,

02:57:07.840 --> 02:57:09.490
we're going to
identify whether they

02:57:09.490 --> 02:57:11.980
are-- which phase of this
overtraining thing we'll

02:57:11.980 --> 02:57:13.570
talk about next
they're actually in.

02:57:13.570 --> 02:57:15.100
Some phases I'm OK.

02:57:15.100 --> 02:57:16.930
Some of them I'm not.

02:57:16.930 --> 02:57:20.320
If we're seeing certain
things happen physiologically,

02:57:20.320 --> 02:57:21.940
we're going to make
a conversation.

02:57:21.940 --> 02:57:27.310
We're also then going to really
think carefully about why

02:57:27.310 --> 02:57:29.048
are you making this choice?

02:57:29.048 --> 02:57:30.840
Do you feel like the
training isn't enough?

02:57:30.840 --> 02:57:31.620
OK, great.

02:57:31.620 --> 02:57:33.700
Let's modify it then.

02:57:33.700 --> 02:57:35.110
Are you not making progress?

02:57:35.110 --> 02:57:38.822
Or are there some other
reasons why you're doing this?

02:57:38.822 --> 02:57:40.780
Obviously, I'm not a
psychologist or therapist.

02:57:40.780 --> 02:57:44.380
But there are clearly situations
in which folks dose themselves

02:57:44.380 --> 02:57:48.040
with far too much exercise
for reasons that are not

02:57:48.040 --> 02:57:50.540
because it's productive to
their training or goals.

02:57:50.540 --> 02:57:52.780
And if such a case,
we would probably

02:57:52.780 --> 02:57:55.142
bring in somebody that
specializes in those areas

02:57:55.142 --> 02:57:57.100
to clear that out and
just make sure it's like,

02:57:57.100 --> 02:58:02.860
we're not doing this for
anxiety issues or energy things.

02:58:02.860 --> 02:58:05.620
If it's, I just don't think the
program's enough, OK, great.

02:58:05.620 --> 02:58:06.310
Let's go back.

02:58:06.310 --> 02:58:07.393
Let's look at our metrics.

02:58:07.393 --> 02:58:09.020
Let's evaluate our
tests and go there.

02:58:09.020 --> 02:58:11.050
But if there's other
reasons, then we

02:58:11.050 --> 02:58:13.050
may bring in somebody to
have that conversation.

02:58:13.050 --> 02:58:13.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:58:13.390 --> 02:58:15.630
Usually when I've seen
people deviate from programs,

02:58:15.630 --> 02:58:17.830
it's because they tend to
revert to something that they've

02:58:17.830 --> 02:58:18.640
done for a long time.

02:58:18.640 --> 02:58:20.185
It just feels really
comfortable to them.

02:58:20.185 --> 02:58:21.460
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it worked.

02:58:21.460 --> 02:58:22.180
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:58:22.180 --> 02:58:24.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it was
giving them decent results.

02:58:24.100 --> 02:58:25.960
So they're skeptical
to try something else.

02:58:25.960 --> 02:58:30.022
Or there is a phenotype
of haphazardness

02:58:30.022 --> 02:58:31.480
sometimes, especially
if people get

02:58:31.480 --> 02:58:32.590
really caffeinated
before a workout

02:58:32.590 --> 02:58:33.630
and just want to
throw something in.

02:58:33.630 --> 02:58:34.750
And then, there's
a third category.

02:58:34.750 --> 02:58:36.417
And this is one that
I've had to contend

02:58:36.417 --> 02:58:38.938
with a lot in my life, which
is that I really enjoy training

02:58:38.938 --> 02:58:40.855
with other people when
I have the opportunity.

02:58:40.855 --> 02:58:41.530
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:58:41.530 --> 02:58:42.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And a certain day

02:58:42.550 --> 02:58:43.570
rolls around where
you're supposed

02:58:43.570 --> 02:58:46.040
to do something and not do
other things and people say,

02:58:46.040 --> 02:58:47.915
hey, do you want to go
for a long ocean swim?

02:58:47.915 --> 02:58:48.850
Or you want to train?

02:58:48.850 --> 02:58:52.810
And you end up doing some
Kenny Kane, this one's for you,

02:58:52.810 --> 02:58:56.980
some ridiculous 20 wall
ball CrossFit type workout.

02:58:56.980 --> 02:58:59.240
And I'm not acclimated
for that sort of thing.

02:58:59.240 --> 02:58:59.990
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:58:59.990 --> 02:59:01.365
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And then it does

02:59:01.365 --> 02:59:03.160
tend to throw things
off, not because--

02:59:03.160 --> 02:59:07.170
no pun intended, Kenny-- because
there's nothing wrong with a 20

02:59:07.170 --> 02:59:08.920
sets of wall balls if
you're-- that's part

02:59:08.920 --> 02:59:09.630
of your conditioning.

02:59:09.630 --> 02:59:10.130
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:59:10.130 --> 02:59:11.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But if it's
not appropriate for where

02:59:11.500 --> 02:59:12.958
you are in your
schedule, it really

02:59:12.958 --> 02:59:14.650
can disrupt what
you're trying to do.

02:59:14.650 --> 02:59:15.400
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

02:59:15.400 --> 02:59:17.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Even as
a non-competitive athlete,

02:59:17.680 --> 02:59:21.430
like myself, years
since I've competed

02:59:21.430 --> 02:59:23.170
in any athletic program.

02:59:23.170 --> 02:59:25.030
But as a
non-competitive athlete,

02:59:25.030 --> 02:59:30.370
I think there's a beauty to
and a really strong incentive

02:59:30.370 --> 02:59:34.780
to being disciplined about
the program that one follows.

02:59:34.780 --> 02:59:38.145
As a mentor and professor that
I worked with years ago used

02:59:38.145 --> 02:59:39.520
to say, I'd come
into his office,

02:59:39.520 --> 02:59:41.187
all these ideas and
things I want to do.

02:59:41.187 --> 02:59:44.470
And he'd say, let's
constrain this walk.

02:59:44.470 --> 02:59:46.310
And then the question
you always want

02:59:46.310 --> 02:59:48.310
to arrive at in a discussion
with your students,

02:59:48.310 --> 02:59:50.530
as you know is, what's
the experiment exactly?

02:59:50.530 --> 02:59:52.810
And then you go and you do
that specific experiment.

02:59:52.810 --> 02:59:55.060
I think I view a
workout the same way,

02:59:55.060 --> 02:59:58.135
that there are multiple
adaptations, goals,

02:59:58.135 --> 03:00:00.010
and things that people
are trying to achieve.

03:00:00.010 --> 03:00:02.050
Really knowing why
you're there each time

03:00:02.050 --> 03:00:04.570
and really sticking to
that, even if it means

03:00:04.570 --> 03:00:06.055
not training with other people.

03:00:06.055 --> 03:00:07.930
Or I always say, well,
you can train with me,

03:00:07.930 --> 03:00:09.070
but I'm not going
to train with you.

03:00:09.070 --> 03:00:09.610
[LAUGHTER]

03:00:09.610 --> 03:00:10.567
ANDY GALPIN: Selfish.

03:00:10.567 --> 03:00:12.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
that's one way to do it.

03:00:12.400 --> 03:00:14.080
But really sticking
to a schedule

03:00:14.080 --> 03:00:16.450
is really what allows
the progress to emerge.

03:00:16.450 --> 03:00:19.240
But that doesn't necessarily
mean being antisocial.

03:00:19.240 --> 03:00:20.565
You can invite people along.

03:00:20.565 --> 03:00:20.930
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

03:00:20.930 --> 03:00:21.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
But in this case,

03:00:21.530 --> 03:00:23.680
I'm telling people to be
the host, not the guest.

03:00:23.680 --> 03:00:25.150
ANDY GALPIN: I have a
little bit of a rule here.

03:00:25.150 --> 03:00:27.580
Maybe I should have answered
your question this way.

03:00:27.580 --> 03:00:32.020
I actually like doing things
totally different occasionally.

03:00:32.020 --> 03:00:35.320
So I'll do-- when I'm traveling,
I tend to do hotel workouts.

03:00:35.320 --> 03:00:38.740
What I mean by that is, I will
go down to the workout room.

03:00:38.740 --> 03:00:42.610
And I will do a set of 10 to
15 reps of every single machine

03:00:42.610 --> 03:00:44.963
in the exact order in
which they are laid out.

03:00:44.963 --> 03:00:45.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Whoa.

03:00:45.880 --> 03:00:47.590
ANDY GALPIN: Just
for the sake of fun.

03:00:47.590 --> 03:00:48.865
Just for the sake of, OK.

03:00:48.865 --> 03:00:50.845
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's like the
tarot card version of workouts.

03:00:50.845 --> 03:00:51.190
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, totally.

03:00:51.190 --> 03:00:52.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
like, whatever comes up,

03:00:52.210 --> 03:00:53.440
I'm going to make sense of it.

03:00:53.440 --> 03:00:54.400
ANDY GALPIN: And you just move.

03:00:54.400 --> 03:00:54.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

03:00:54.700 --> 03:00:56.200
ANDY GALPIN: And
those are typically

03:00:56.200 --> 03:00:58.870
things of like, I just want to
move a little bit for jet lag

03:00:58.870 --> 03:01:01.390
and other purposes.

03:01:01.390 --> 03:01:04.040
That's often, I wasn't going
to get to work out today.

03:01:04.040 --> 03:01:07.485
And so, now I'm going to
do something to feel great.

03:01:07.485 --> 03:01:08.860
I don't travel
that much, though,

03:01:08.860 --> 03:01:12.530
so it's not really
throwing my things off.

03:01:12.530 --> 03:01:16.070
I also, I don't get
a lot of free time.

03:01:16.070 --> 03:01:18.130
And so, if I am
traveling, and I'm

03:01:18.130 --> 03:01:20.920
seeing someone I haven't
seen in many years

03:01:20.920 --> 03:01:22.975
or, for the first
time, I mean, we

03:01:22.975 --> 03:01:25.100
got to train together this
week for the first time.

03:01:25.100 --> 03:01:25.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It
was a lot of fun.

03:01:25.240 --> 03:01:26.710
ANDY GALPIN: I'm not going
to burn that opportunity.

03:01:26.710 --> 03:01:27.880
My rule is this though.

03:01:27.880 --> 03:01:29.380
I'm not going to
do something that's

03:01:29.380 --> 03:01:31.790
going to cost me
more than three days.

03:01:31.790 --> 03:01:35.440
So I'm absolutely
happy to get out there,

03:01:35.440 --> 03:01:37.240
and maybe tomorrow
morning, or tonight, we

03:01:37.240 --> 03:01:39.100
go do something fun
that's off my schedule.

03:01:39.100 --> 03:01:39.760
I'm in.

03:01:39.760 --> 03:01:41.065
I'm in 100%.

03:01:41.065 --> 03:01:42.940
I'm just going to
down-regulate a little bit.

03:01:42.940 --> 03:01:45.065
I'm not going to maybe do
as much as you or as hard

03:01:45.065 --> 03:01:46.150
as you or whatever.

03:01:46.150 --> 03:01:48.130
I'll do more than I should.

03:01:48.130 --> 03:01:51.940
But if it costs me tomorrow,
it was worth the exchange.

03:01:51.940 --> 03:01:54.770
I don't have a world record
I'm setting anytime soon.

03:01:54.770 --> 03:01:55.750
I don't have--

03:01:55.750 --> 03:01:56.830
I got many years.

03:01:56.830 --> 03:02:00.400
I'm happy to give up a
couple of days of exercise

03:02:00.400 --> 03:02:02.410
to be a little sorer
than I need to be

03:02:02.410 --> 03:02:05.230
for the exchange of
a lifetime memory.

03:02:05.230 --> 03:02:08.320
And this stuff is
so important to me.

03:02:08.320 --> 03:02:10.720
This stuff lands as
true lifetime memories.

03:02:10.720 --> 03:02:12.850
I can look back-- many
of my fond memories

03:02:12.850 --> 03:02:15.250
from my life are training
sessions with friends, whatever

03:02:15.250 --> 03:02:18.102
it is, like doing
jujitsu with somebody

03:02:18.102 --> 03:02:19.060
who's a world champion.

03:02:19.060 --> 03:02:21.040
You're just like, whatever
the thing is, you're like,

03:02:21.040 --> 03:02:22.248
that was really, really cool.

03:02:22.248 --> 03:02:23.680
Absolutely worth
missing two days.

03:02:23.680 --> 03:02:26.097
If it's going to be more than
three days though, where I'm

03:02:26.097 --> 03:02:29.322
going to be so wrecked I can't
work out for five or six days,

03:02:29.322 --> 03:02:31.780
then I'm probably like, all
right, that's kind of nonsense.

03:02:31.780 --> 03:02:33.738
Unless it's just an
opportunity where I'm like,

03:02:33.738 --> 03:02:35.410
I absolutely can't pass that up.

03:02:35.410 --> 03:02:37.940
So that's how I think about it.

03:02:37.940 --> 03:02:40.720
That doesn't happen too often
with me though, maybe once

03:02:40.720 --> 03:02:41.220
a month.

03:02:41.220 --> 03:02:43.118
And so I'm like, OK,
fine, I lost a day.

03:02:43.118 --> 03:02:45.410
Reality of it is it's probably
more like once a quarter

03:02:45.410 --> 03:02:46.080
that that happens.

03:02:46.080 --> 03:02:47.038
So I don't really care.

03:02:47.038 --> 03:02:52.490
So you do want to
balance joy and life.

03:02:52.490 --> 03:02:54.950
You don't want to be so rigid
about your training program

03:02:54.950 --> 03:02:57.050
that it ruins and robs
those experiences.

03:02:57.050 --> 03:03:01.688
Physical activity should be fun.

03:03:01.688 --> 03:03:03.230
Your fitness and
your training should

03:03:03.230 --> 03:03:04.938
be something that
makes your life better,

03:03:04.938 --> 03:03:08.600
not some task you
have to get done

03:03:08.600 --> 03:03:11.990
so that 75 years
from now you've hit

03:03:11.990 --> 03:03:13.912
some metric of who knows what.

03:03:13.912 --> 03:03:16.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Just alone in
your room with your training

03:03:16.370 --> 03:03:16.940
logs.

03:03:16.940 --> 03:03:17.690
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

03:03:17.690 --> 03:03:18.290
[LAUGHTER]

03:03:18.290 --> 03:03:20.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No,
in all seriousness,

03:03:20.390 --> 03:03:22.400
I think, you point to
the richness of life.

03:03:22.400 --> 03:03:25.280
And you can draw these boxes
like work, relationships,

03:03:25.280 --> 03:03:26.030
fitness, recovery.

03:03:26.030 --> 03:03:28.792
But the boundaries between those
boxes are blurry, because--

03:03:28.792 --> 03:03:29.750
ANDY GALPIN: Of course.

03:03:29.750 --> 03:03:30.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And I should say,

03:03:30.410 --> 03:03:31.490
I love training with people.

03:03:31.490 --> 03:03:33.532
I greatly enjoyed training
with you this morning,

03:03:33.532 --> 03:03:35.900
not just because I was
receiving so many useful tips.

03:03:35.900 --> 03:03:37.220
In fact, thank you.

03:03:37.220 --> 03:03:39.190
First time I PR'd in a
number of things today.

03:03:39.190 --> 03:03:39.450
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

03:03:39.450 --> 03:03:41.617
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Thanks to
your input in the moment.

03:03:41.617 --> 03:03:43.355
And that's an
irreplaceable kind of gift.

03:03:43.355 --> 03:03:45.230
But mostly, it's the
gift of getting to train

03:03:45.230 --> 03:03:46.397
with a colleague and friend.

03:03:46.397 --> 03:03:49.760
So I want to underscore,
highlight, and put

03:03:49.760 --> 03:03:52.680
an exclamation mark
behind what you just said.

03:03:52.680 --> 03:03:57.470
Thank you once again,
and again, for giving us

03:03:57.470 --> 03:04:01.880
so much interesting,
clear, actionable,

03:04:01.880 --> 03:04:06.170
and at times somewhat
counterintuitive information

03:04:06.170 --> 03:04:09.080
in order to build out
an exceptional training

03:04:09.080 --> 03:04:13.340
program to meet any of-- and
in some cases-- all of the nine

03:04:13.340 --> 03:04:15.770
major adaptations that
exercise can create

03:04:15.770 --> 03:04:19.070
toward aesthetic,
performance-related, and

03:04:19.070 --> 03:04:23.150
healthspan lifespan,
aka longevity goals.

03:04:23.150 --> 03:04:26.310
It's really a treasure
trove of information there.

03:04:26.310 --> 03:04:28.730
And I look forward to
our next discussion

03:04:28.730 --> 03:04:31.700
about how to best
recover from exercise,

03:04:31.700 --> 03:04:36.350
both within the exercise bout,
and between exercise bouts,

03:04:36.350 --> 03:04:39.900
and in the more macroscopic
structure of a week, a month,

03:04:39.900 --> 03:04:41.310
a year.

03:04:41.310 --> 03:04:42.085
I can't wait.

03:04:42.085 --> 03:04:43.460
ANDY GALPIN: I
can't wait either.

03:04:43.460 --> 03:04:44.630
I love that topic.

03:04:44.630 --> 03:04:47.502
And I've got a lot to
cover, so it'll be fun.

